	(72,144,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-09-28 14:05:49','2005-09-28 21:05:49','Because the job of a government is to make its people healthy.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(73,144,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.16','2005-09-28 17:39:39','2005-09-29 00:39:39','Oh I actually happen to agree with the EU on this particular WTO idiocy--it\'s one of the (admittedly few) examples of free trade doing exactly the wrong thing. \r\n<br />\r\nAmerica needs to realize that we\'ve basically told the government \"we don\'t care what we eat as long as it\'s convenient,\" which has allowed them to go ahead and foster businesses that feed us shit (conveniently). \r\n<br />\r\nThe problem is laziness: if we weren\'t too lazy to go to the store twice a week, maybe we would buy food that wasn\'t more preservative than food. Etc, etc, etc...',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(74,115,'&raquo; Level 60','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=147','216.119.127.83','2005-09-29 07:47:56','2005-09-29 14:47:56','[...] As I&#8217;ve mentioned before, playing a game to the &#8220;end&#8221; has never been my strong point, and up until now I&#8217;ve never hit the level limit in an RPG. Not in Final Fantasy VII, not in Diablo (I or II), and not even in TIE Fighter, the greatest game of all time, did I get to General (highest rank). [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.1.3','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(75,182,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-10-11 16:26:46','2005-10-11 23:26:46','Actually, sci-fi television shows originally <i>were</i> just westerns in disguise (hence \"Star Trek\"\'s creator\'s background in westerns, and the show\'s original name, \"Wagon Train to the Stars\"). On top of that, \"Firefly,\" the TV show that Serenity was based off of, was actually <i>supposed</i> to be a mix of the sci-fi and western genres.  In Serenity it wasn\'t all that obvious, but the TV show was basically just a western with some sci-fi sprinkled in.\r\n\r\n...which is why I\'m a virgin.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(76,181,'Kyle','kyle@kylecobelry.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','68.227.8.214','2005-10-11 18:15:15','2005-10-12 01:15:15','It doesn\'t say anything about how much the vehicles cost. If you get a $5,000 write-off for getting a $20,000 car, and a $2,000 write-off for an $8,000 car, how is that so weird? If you\'re going to get an SUV anyway, and there\'s an incentive for you to get the more efficient one, I don\'t see how that\'s so criminal, especially since I don\'t agree with the idea incentives in the first place.\r\n\r\nWhen gasoline gets expensive enough, people will be knocking eachother over to get hybrids. Dealers already can\'t keep Civics in stock. As it stands now, I pay about $70 a month to gas my little Mazda, and I drive it every single day. I\'m going to switch to a scooter this month, I think, because it gets 100 miles to the gallon and doesn\'t need insurance, which will save me in the neighborhood of $5000-$6000 over the next couple years. Why do we need to bleed taxpayers into following the market?',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(77,182,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-12 04:23:01','2005-10-12 11:23:01','Have faith...someday you may invent Star Wars (complete with western-style shootout in an alien-packed canteen) and get laid by freaky alien women. \r\n\r\nIs Firefly any good? Or should I go straight to the highly recommended Farscape?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(78,181,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-12 04:27:46','2005-10-12 11:27:46','I think it\'s a favor to the auto industry as well (the tax breaks), because they helps foster a new market for new cars. \r\n\r\nWhat I think is funny is that legislation designed to encourage the purchase of more fuel-efficient vehicles gives the largest (literal, not relative--I hadn\' t thought of that) breaks to the cars that aren\'t the most efficient.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(80,182,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-10-12 22:11:33','2005-10-13 05:11:33','\"Firefly\" is fantastic. You would be hard-pressed to find better-written characters than those on \"Firefly\" anywhere in television. It also takes the western/sci-fi mix far enough to distinguish it from other sci-fi, giving it a particularly unique feel. For the most part, the show\'s only weakness is that it lasts 14 episodes; it\'s probably the best show Fox has cancelled, which really means something. \"Farscape\" is also great, but...  different.  It\'s definitely a good show, but it\'s also definitely Australian, and made with puppets.  I love it, personally, but I could also understand being put off by it if you\'re used to American TV.  Which you, as a film student, probably shouldn\'t have a problem with.  But different goes both ways; it has its weaknesses, but there are things it does that no other show could even touch.  Basically, they\'re both great, but \"Firefly\" probably appeals to a wider audience than \"Farscape\" does.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(81,102,'Jason-Preston.com &raquo; Switching Pockets','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=189','216.119.127.83','2005-10-13 12:52:17','2005-10-13 19:52:17','[...] In Israel, however, my wallet was stolen. I didn&#8217;t even notice it&#8217;d been taken from my back pocket until I tried to pay the cab fare home. [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.2','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(82,196,'Kyle','kyle@kylecobelry.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','68.227.8.214','2005-10-16 11:15:32','2005-10-16 18:15:32','Farscape Farscape Farscape.\r\n\r\nIt\'s not exactly a movie, it\'s a little more like 90 hours of setup and then a 4 hour movie, but the love story of John Crichton and Aeryn Sun is not only good for a sci-fi, I think it\'s the most well-developed, engaging love story I\'ve ever seen.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(83,197,'Rachael Preston','lil_04_mermaid@yahoo.com','','71.115.100.153','2005-10-16 20:41:21','2005-10-17 03:41:21','HEY HEY!\r\n\r\nGuess who? its ur favorite cousin! well i have nothing witty or sage to add to your seinfeld-esque account of life in England....but i WILL fill in a bunch of space with unprofound, non-thought provoking ramblings to remind you of your loving cousin....who by the way, can\'t type to save her life (forgive me) annnd i just spent 10 seconds retyping \"forgive\" b/c the keys wouldnt obey my fingers and a stray letter kept getting left out, further proving my point.....i am going to laugh if you dont even get this b/c i dont technically have a web site and all this tedious typing will be in vain, just my luck....sooo i would ask how it\'s going but given the circumstances of this note, i am pretty much well informed.anyways, long story short......::ahem::..::cough::. .::cough::. miss seeing you, hope you\'re having a grand time, and if you get bored, my email is [ deleted ]\r\n!Tienes un buen semana!\r\n*your cousin*\r\n\r\n-Rachael-',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/312.5.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/312.3.1','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(84,196,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-17 06:19:22','2005-10-17 13:19:22','More or less the only reason I haven\'t gotton to Farscape yet is that it\'s gonna be damn hard for me to get my hands on it out here. The internet has most of my downloading capability blocked, and the DVDs are all the wrong region.\r\n\r\nSo unless someone gmails me the episodes one by one, I\'m out of luck for the next few months *g*',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(85,197,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-17 06:20:25','2005-10-17 13:20:25','Thanks! I\'ll drop you an e-mail sometime!\r\n\r\nHope you\'re doing well (and everyone else) back home!',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(86,198,'Hillary','Hillary.A.Preston@Dartmouth.edu','','129.170.160.191','2005-10-17 08:17:33','2005-10-17 15:17:33','Just thought I\'d let you know that the homonym \"threw\" does not mean the same thing as \"through\" :)  Though it was amusing to read that you \"blew threw\" a book -- very interesting mental image.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(87,199,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-17 11:15:41','2005-10-17 18:15:41','Pubs close at 11?? That is seriously ghetto. I think Las Vegas has it going on with 24-hour alcohol sales. Truth be told, I don\'t even really understand what mandatory last calls are supposed to do.\r\n\r\nI think Elyse had a somewhat similar experience with her year in Germany. She didn\'t spend the whole time getting her mind blown away by all the *culture*, she probably spent most of it in class or in her dorm room talking to me. I think people feel obligated to prattle on about whatever oh-so-prolific adventure (and since when is getting wasted at a club so adventurous?) they feel like they\'re on because their normal lives are so lame. For the rest of us every day is as much of an adventure as the last, even if it\'s just not knowing what conversation you\'re going to have, what cool thing you\'re going to read, or which ho at the bank you\'re going to get in a fight with. I mean, that\'s life isn\'t it? What\'s so wrong with that?',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(88,198,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-17 11:19:51','2005-10-17 18:19:51','Ever read \"Choke\" by Chuck Palahniuk? It\'s about a guy who\'s addicted to support groups. I think there\'s even an imaginary friend and a surprise twist at the end. It honestly might as well have been called \"Choke Club.\"',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(89,198,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-10-17 12:53:08','2005-10-17 19:53:08','Whoops! Fixed the error...thanks for pointing it out!\r\n<br /> <br />\r\nRE: Choke<br /><br />\r\n\r\nNo, I haven\'t, but I\'m currently about 3/4 of the way through \"Non-Fiction\" which is Palahniuk\'s collection of essays, and he mentions how his Choke research led into Fight Club.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(90,199,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-17 12:56:15','2005-10-17 19:56:15','Word',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(91,200,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-17 13:01:53','2005-10-17 20:01:53','Dude, you\'re a film student and you haven\'t gone through a Dune phase yet? Personally, I fucking hated the Lynch version, but the Sci-Fi channel one was pretty good, and its sequel (Children of Dune) was probably one of the best movies I\'ve ever seen. I still haven\'t finished the book series yet, but suffice to say that \"Dune\" barely scratches the surface.\r\n\r\nAs for its political implications, those aren\'t all that coincidental. Frank Herbert based Arakkis on Saudi Arabia, he spent some time in the middle east, and I\'m pretty sure he speaks Arabic (which is what the Fremen language is based on). The next two books deal with (surprise) terrorism and insurgencies.\r\n\r\nFrank Herbert went to UW (I think), and his son, Brian Herbert, lives in Seattle and does book signings and speaking engagements and shit all the time. He\'s writing the conclusion to the dune series right now, supposedly, and it\'s been about as anticipating as the final fucking Harry Potter book.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(92,200,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.19','2005-10-17 13:06:54','2005-10-17 20:06:54','Wow, that\'s a lot I didn\'t know. \r\n\r\nDune was one of those books, along with the Foundation Trilogy, that my Dad kind of shoved on my bookshelf in middle school, and for some reason it\'s one of those things I never got around too. I\'d always meant to read the book first. \r\n\r\nThis is honestly my first real sci-fi run through as far as film goes---hell, I even watched Chronicles of Riddick because I can\'t get anything better over here (and yes, I still hate Vin Diesel). I\'m downloading Firefly at 17 k/s, but only while i\'m in the computer lab. \r\n\r\nTomorrow I\'m gonna go to the actual film library here and see if I can\'t find any Dune movies with slightly better claymation ;)',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(93,200,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-17 14:24:34','2005-10-17 21:24:34','Well, good luck finding anything other than the Lynch version, especially in another country. The Sci-Fi version had slightly tighter production, but it relied heavily on things like painted backdrops and a kind of sloppy \"spice eyes\" effect. The acting was *way* better though, the narrative made more sense, and it had a more memorable Baron Harkonnen and no fuckin\' Sting or Captain Picard.\r\n\r\nChildren of Dune had incredible production, amazing acting, an iconic score, and a brilliant screenplay. It\'s also just a cooler story (and Susan Sarandon is totally badass).',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(94,200,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.16','2005-10-17 15:30:23','2005-10-17 22:30:23','I look forward to it *g*',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(95,202,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-18 09:28:13','2005-10-18 16:28:13','Yet leave it to some stuffy college professor to think that you can.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(97,194,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-21 10:41:21','2005-10-21 17:41:21','Well, I don\'t think Claire gets it. At some point you kind of stop giving a shit about excitement, and start looking for fulfillment. The implication of looking for everlasting excitement in a relationship is that you also need to be \'entertained\' all the time.\r\n\r\nI have what most people would call a boring relationship. Every morning, Elyse and I both sleep 10 minutes past our respective alarms, kiss goodbye, go to our respective boring ass jobs, come home, cook and eat dinner together, watch TV shows or movies, and then go to bed. On the weekends, we clean the house, go shopping, go to the pet store and look at the new animals, and go to the exact same bar. She\'ll usually read or knit, and I\'ll usually make music or work out. Occasionally, we\'ll talk about going somewhere and making friends, but we never really get around to it. This has basically been the gist of just about every week for the last 10 months.\r\n\r\nIt\'s *awesome*.\r\n\r\nI also have someone who\'ll make me an omlete when I\'m in a shitty mood, someone to send, on average, 20 emails a day to at work, someone to listen to my mixes, someone to watch my shows with, someone to get good at cooking for, someone to play basketball with... I\'ve got a partner, and it\'s better than any \'exciting\' girlfriend I\'ve ever had (you never know when she\'ll move away or fuck your friends!). Our relationship itself is pretty tame, but damn is it fulfilling.\r\n\r\nSo keep your head up. Chicks looking for \'excitement\' really just need to be constantly entertained and have some settling down to do.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(98,153,'My_name','john@email.com','http://www.google.com','82.76.94.42','2005-10-23 13:09:23','2005-10-23 20:09:23','This is just a Google test :)',0,'1','Mozilla/3.0 (compatible; Indy Library)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(99,28,'My_name','john@email.com','http://www.google.com','82.76.94.42','2005-10-23 13:09:40','2005-10-23 20:09:40','This is just a Google test :)',0,'1','Mozilla/3.0 (compatible; Indy Library)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(101,226,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-24 11:38:15','2005-10-24 18:38:15','Stunt public discussion? That assumes that the NYT and it\'s ilk are where our public discourse is taking place, and frankly, I\'m getting a little weary of even taking the Times, The Post, and TIME seriously anymore, let alone worshipping at their collective altars. The real public discourse is taking place right here right now, and if people started charging for blog subscriptions then there would also still be free ones. It\'s in the daily kos, it\'s in Ann Coulter\'s blog, it\'s in the drudge report, it\'s in media matters, it\'s on talk radio, it\'s in coffee shops. The so-called serious media has increasingly become all propaganda (anytime they bring up the Bush administration or the military) or baseless hype (Katrina coverage, anytime a white woman disappears, and a CIA leak thing that no one really understands or gives a shit about), and people will give them a pass on anything because they\'re the \"real\" news and they\'re necessary to our discussion. Well guess what, anyone can be a reporter now, and I\'d trust something that 50 people on the internet blogged about more readily than I\'d believe anything Joe Klein or some AP goon writes. Let the NYT charge $500 a year, they\'re already just a vestige from a time when they were beneficial.\r\n\r\nAs far access to free shit, business is finally starting to catch up. It\'s about selection, quality, and delivery, and it took the business world like 5 years to figure out that you can make money off of technology. Why the hell would I spend half an hour trying to trackdown \"Shebeen\" by the Toasters on a p2p network when it\'s a painless click away on iTunes, and I have it immediately. Why would I steal movies when for $20 a month I can get unlimited access to any movie ever on netflix?\r\n\r\nAs for wifi in cities, the only value I see in it is making your city \'competitive\' in a business sense. If you don\'t care if you live in San Francisco or San Diego, and one has wifi and the other doesn\'t, and you choose based on that then the tax base of that city increases. What I don\'t want to see happen is things like internet access starting to become \'rights\' that everyone is \'entitled\' to. If you don\'t want to pay big telecommunications bills, prepay your cell phones and don\'t use them unless you have to, use the internet at the library (or starbucks), and for the love of god don\'t subscribe to cable. The entitlement mentality is what\'s gotten us hip-deep into this \'health insurance is necessary and doctors should be free\' crap.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(102,226,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-10-24 13:32:55','2005-10-24 20:32:55','What <i>are</i> people entitled to?  As Americans, I mean.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(103,226,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-24 15:04:31','2005-10-24 22:04:31','As a human, you are entitled to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. Those rights are endowed by our creator, and therefore cannot be negated or mitigated by any vote or piece of paper.\r\n\r\nAs an American, you are entitled to be protected against foreign and domestic invasion, you are entitled to elect a representative government, and you are entitled to enjoy the freedoms granted to you by the constitution. \"Oh, and free doctors and welfare money too\" isn\'t anywhere in there, nor is the right to have an abortion. Both of those things are outside of the federal government\'s jurisdiction per the tenth admendment, but since 9 lawyers can apparently invent new constitutional laws that\'s become increasingly meaningless.\r\n\r\nAs a resident of Seattle (or Las Vegas, or whatever your municipality is), you have also have certain entitlements. These include things like the right to not get beaten up or have your stuff stolen, have abortions if your state allows them (which most states did pre-1973), have guaranteed golf club memberships, have clean streets, and so on.\r\n\r\nAs a resident of the Sterling Springs HOA, you are entitled to have a uniform neighborhood with maintained lawns and appearances conducive to increasing everybody\'s property value.\r\n\r\nAs a guest in my house, you are entitled to all the water you can drink, you can petition me for Coke:Zeros, but you may not smoke on premises.\r\n\r\nIt\'s a tiered system, and that\'s awesome, and it needs to be respected more.\r\n\r\nEvery law ultimately derives from morality, and given the diversity of Americans the more localized you can make that the better. For example, I think the best way to make people happy is to allow them to get jobs, and the best way to make jobs is to make it easy to start one. That\'s pretty much the Las Vegas motto, so we get along splendidly. Seattle believes that the best way to make people happy is to micromanage their lives into being happy, and they do that by making them dependent on government. I think that\'s insane and immoral, but there\'s still a half million in Seattle, so more power to them- I don\'t have to pay their stupid taxes anymore or live with their consequences.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(104,226,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-25 04:27:29','2005-10-25 11:27:29','Access to the internet, cable, or the New York Times certainly isn\'t a right by any means. \n\nI\'m suggesting that if business wants to monetize what we <i>do</i> online, the system should be restructured so that <i>getting</i> online is cheaper (from ISPs) or easier (libraries are great, but limited, and usually not as convenient as lazy American bastards want them to be). \n\nAs you pointed out---making money in America is largely about convenience. iTunes is a lot easier than stealing music. I buy DVDs because, shit, I don\'t have the patience or hard drive space to pirate them. (In fact, as a side note, I tried to buy Firefly before I started downloading the episodes, but thanks to idiotic region-coding on DVDs, I actually <i>can\'t</i> buy them in Britain, which is just idiocy). \n\nA shitload of what the media covers now is, as you say, either melodramatic overcoverage (boat in the street?) or on someone\'s agenda. But the one thing the traditional news media still does better is systematic aggregation. If I want to find out about literal events all over the world, I still have to go to a news site. Maybe it exists and I don\'t know about it, or maybe it should exist, but I haven\'t found a site that collects relatively well-written personal accounts about earthquakes, fires, elections, deaths, polls, opinions, speeches, and just <i>things that happen</i> all over the world. \n\nWhen that happens, and it\'s daily and reliable, then we\'ll have new media. Not until then. And you know what? I\'d consider paying for that, too.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(105,226,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-25 14:12:23','2005-10-25 21:12:23','I don\'t want to sound like a pessimist or a postmodernist, but maybe it\'s time to shed notions of having reporting agencies that give us an \"objective\" perspective on reality. Even when TIME occasionaly gets the story right, it\'s never being told from a worldview that makes any sense to me. They just doesn\'t even use the same system of measurement as me.\r\n\r\nI think of \'union\' as being more synonymous with \'gang\' than \'team\', so while I do want to hear the facts about the propositions facing them in California, I don\'t want it phrased in terms of whether or not big bad state government will silence the voices of poor oppressed workers. I don\'t want to hear about whether or not they think this or that judge is a liberal or a conservative, because I care a lot more about whether or not they\'re originalist or activist. I want to know what\'s going on, but I want the gauge of success to be calibrated on successes or failures of the new government, not how many people died or what new guy from another country blew himself up, or how involved the UN was. I don\'t think of socialism as a neutral, different way of doing things, I think of it as an immoral denial of economic freedom. So give me the facts good or bad, but don\'t put it through some lens that celebrates the so-called victory of workers in Venezuela for making their state-run businesses even more inefficient. For me to be informed by the news, it needs to be told to me in a way that makes some degree of sense. They can\'t tell me facts while simultaneously telling me up means down.\r\n\r\nI pay $7 a month just to listen to Dr. Laura without the stupid CBS breaks, and I gladly pay $32 a year to get the Weekly Standard. It\'s facing competition from blogs like everything else is though, and pretty soon it\'ll probably just become one. Hopefully all your news, music, and entertainment subscriptions will be managed via RSS (which will hopefully be super-tightly integrated into the new Windows) pretty soon, and we can all start getting news that makes sense to us.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(106,226,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-10-25 18:30:33','2005-10-26 01:30:33','Yes, I hope so. \n\nAt the moment, though, I\'m simply saying that you have to slog through the opinionated parts to get the facts, and I don\'t know of a better way at the moment.\n\nMaybe we should start an uber blog service? We can create a service that aggregates blog posts and enlists \"citizen journalists\" (whatever that means) to provide a news-replacement service. Package it to people like traditional media is delivered now? We could be rich!\n\nAnd by the way, I played around with Longhorn at the MS offices this summer, and RSS is gonna be there in a BIG way. I promise you.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(108,150,'Frustrating technology (rant) at Jason-Preston.com','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=239','216.119.127.83','2005-10-27 06:06:47','2005-10-27 13:06:47','[...] So I bring my mouse. But the optical mouse doesn&#8217;t work on the desks they have for computers here, so I have to bring my mousepad. And with all this stuff, usually at least one thing falls on the floor between my room and the computer lab (which is how I broke my mouse). Also, I tend to hit my laptop on things (like heavy fire doors), and generally, when I do bring everything out here, I end up not staying long enough for it to be worthwhile. It&#8217;s hard to say when I&#8217;m going to want to be online for a long time without actually being online. [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.2','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(109,250,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-10-30 15:00:44','2005-10-30 22:00:44','\"Maybe\"?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(110,250,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.16','2005-10-30 15:05:05','2005-10-30 22:05:05','I know. It really gets the adrenaline going, you know. Like...what is it really?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(111,234,'Man Bytes Blog','','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus/?p=294','66.152.98.73','2005-10-31 03:49:29','2005-10-31 10:49:29','<strong>Monday Meme: Check This Out</strong>\n\n	I like Meme Mondays, mostly because it&#8217;s alliterative.  It doesn&#8217;t mean that every Monday will feature a meme, as 99% of memes bore me at best, or annoy me at worst.\n	I was the target of a flattering meme last week, over at Jason Preston&amp;#...',0,'1','WordPress/1.5.1','trackback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(112,255,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-10-31 11:07:30','2005-10-31 18:07:30','You\'re going to see it with the Sam Alito hearing. The Judicial Originalist (or \"Rule of Law\") philosophy versus the Judicial Activist (or \"Rule of What We Think Should Happen\") philosophy has been denied to the public for 30 years, and conservatives have spent the last 15 (since the Bork rejection) pretty finely crafting it for the knock-down drag-out we\'re about to witness.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(113,234,'Man Bytes Blog &raquo; Blog Archive &raquo; Monday Meme: Check This Out','','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus/2005/10/monday-meme-check-this-out/','66.152.98.74','2005-11-01 03:17:37','2005-11-01 10:17:37','[...] I was the target of a flattering meme last week, over at Jason Preston&#8217;s blog. He started the &#8220;Check This Out&#8221; meme wherein you pick a blog that you&#8217;re pretty certain your regular readers don&#8217;t read and talk it up a bit.  I immediately decided against listing any of the Frenz Stuph links from my sidebar, as that&#8217;d be redundant. Likewise, featuring Vinyl Pulse didn&#8217;t seem the right thing to do as I&#8217;m pretty sure I linked to it from one of your sites in the first place. I recently discovered Jane McGonigal&#8217;s blog, Avant Game, but even if you haven&#8217;t already found it yourself, you&#8217;d certainly find it when I posted a link and response to her Minor Statement on Avant Gaming later this week. [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.1','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(114,245,'When is it mine? at Jason-Preston.com','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=254','216.119.127.83','2005-11-01 05:33:31','2005-11-01 12:33:31','[...] On the flip side, if a site wanted to syndicate my content as part of a service to others, (as I touched on here) I would definitely feel entitled to a portion of any profits they made. The relationship has changed &#8212; my content is a service to them. Imagine if newspapers didn&#8217;t pay their writers? [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.2','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(115,258,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-11-03 08:27:08','2005-11-03 15:27:08','No aliens is the new thing; all Battlestar Galactica has is evil religious robots.  Aliens have been pawned off to crappy network dramas with single-word names, like \"Surface,\" or \"Threshold,\" or \"Invasion.\"\n\nI\'m pretty sure at some point they do state that the planets were terraformed, so good call.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(116,249,'Corvus','manbytesblog@pjsattic.com','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus','24.235.69.226','2005-11-04 03:52:23','2005-11-04 10:52:23','My belief that my opinion stands on its own is my higher education Achilles heel.  It\'s also why I tend to hate arguing with academics, who often seem to feel that if someone didn\'t publish it, it isn\'t worth arguing.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Konqueror/3.4; Linux 2.6.11.4-21.9-smp; X11; i686; en_US) KHTML/3.4.2 (like Gecko)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(117,258,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-11-04 06:53:49','2005-11-04 13:53:49','I\'m not saying aliens can\'t work; Zoidberg is possibly the best character in ever of ever, but it does seem to be creeping out of Sci-Fi, yeah. \n\nSomeone needs to invent a rollicking new sci fi movie trilogy based on myth and extremely human characters to replace the recent Star Wars disasters.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(118,249,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-11-04 07:02:36','2005-11-04 14:02:36','It\'s annoying isn\'t it?\n\nBlogging is probably the worst thing that could happen to my academic writing, because out here it\'s all about the ideas...and referencing something is when you link back to them ;)',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(119,261,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-11-04 11:32:32','2005-11-04 18:32:32','That\'s not all that weird; there\'s a certain amount of time-testing that I think a film should be subjected to before it gets to the top of those kind of lists. Jurassic Park would have been in the top 10 in the list was made in 1995 and paid no regard to longevity.\r\n\r\nMy prediction is that the LOTR flicks will be way, way up there.\r\n\r\nAs for your other thing, I think Livejournal should be left to the kids.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(120,261,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.111.85','2005-11-04 18:27:56','2005-11-05 01:27:56','Batman!',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(121,261,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-11-05 08:14:45','2005-11-05 15:14:45','Re: Batman -- that was a damn sweet movie. I\'ve seen it four times already? I\'m still not tired of it. I grant it the award for second-most-re-watchable (next to the Saints, of course)\n\nRe: LJ\n\nI just have this love for the way LJ pulls together communities. Yes, that\'s also it\'s big flaw, but it\'s an endearing system in its own way, and I see no reason someone couldn\'t (or rather, I see no reason why there <i>should</i> be a problem) using LJ as a platform for big-audience, legitimate blogging.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(122,264,'Honor Gunday','honors@gmail.com','http://honorico.com/?p=20','24.18.238.30','2005-11-06 16:32:51','2005-11-06 23:32:51','Amazon\'s choice of the name Mechanical Turk for their new service is culturally incentive and ridiculously geeky.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.10) Gecko/20050716 Firefox/1.0.6','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(123,264,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-11-06 18:11:19','2005-11-07 01:11:19','Essentially, I agree. \n\nI understand why Amazon went looking for a name that went with the character of what they were trying to sell, and names with stories tend to run better than names without, but it\'s not a <i>clear</i> allusion. I guess that\'s really the problem.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(125,274,'IceRocket','rhodes@icerocket.com','http://icerocket.com','71.11.196.21','2005-11-08 12:19:35','2005-11-08 19:19:35','Thanks for using!\r\n\r\nBlake Rhodes\r\nIceRocket.com',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322; Alexa Toolbar)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(126,274,'Dick Costolo','dickc@feedburner.com','http://www.feedburner.com','69.17.22.177','2005-11-08 20:45:29','2005-11-09 03:45:29','Yep, we know what you mean. what you want to do is either\n\na) make sure your blog source code refers only to your feedburner feed, so that when bots search your site, they \"discover\" your FB feed, OR\n\nb) you can take the deep plunge and use Steve Smith\'s wordpress plugin for FeedBurner, which you can find at http://orderedlist.com/articles/wordpress-feedburner-plugin/\n\nThat will route everything through FeedBurner and ensure that nothing ever sees anything but your FB feed so that we track all your traffic.\n\nLots of posts and support for it at both the wordpress site, steve\'s site, and our forums.  The first trick should be sufficient for what you\'re trying to do, though.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; rv:1.7.3) Gecko/20040913 Firefox/0.10','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(127,274,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.16','2005-11-09 06:34:08','2005-11-09 13:34:08','Thanks!\n\nI\'ve already cleaned the original feed info from the php, so it must be buried somewhere I don\'t know where...I think i\'ll try the plugin in a bit, see how it works.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(128,276,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-11-09 15:13:20','2005-11-09 22:13:20','Academia = People who couldn\'t hack it in reality-land. Their arbitrary assessments of how things are supposed to be done (especially regarding how verbose and dry writing is supposed to be) don\'t really mean anything.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(129,267,'DL Byron','byron@texturadesign.com','http://texturadesign.com','206.124.153.162','2005-11-09 15:54:28','2005-11-09 22:54:28','Good points and why I enjoy reading RSS in Safari. It\'s browser based, more intuitive and not a 3-pane email metaphor. When IE 7 comes out, it\'ll boost RSS becaue it works just like Safari does, rendered right in the browser. I think Firefoxs definition of \"live bookmarking\" makes the most sense. A while ago, I wrote a post titled, \"fear of bold,\" where my interest in RSS was quickly deflated by 3K rss articles all alerted by bold, sitting waiting for me to read them.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/416.11 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/416.12','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(130,276,'Jeremy','jziskind@ucla.edu','','71.106.184.132','2005-11-09 16:04:33','2005-11-09 23:04:33','Wrong. Academia = people who spend their lives analyzing and writing about important issues that are promptly ignored by politicians. Also, people that would rather work under relatively free conditions without constant oversight from a boss. Most professors in political science and public policy that I\'ve had thus far got sick of the private sector and its bureaucratic mudslinging.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.10) Gecko/20050716 Firefox/1.0.6','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(131,116,'Jeremy','jziskind@ucla.edu','','71.106.184.132','2005-11-09 16:11:35','2005-11-09 23:11:35','Something that wasn\'t mentioned that seems pretty obvious: about 99% of all blog entries rely on so-called \"traditional media\" for their info. I would surmise that less than 1% of all blog entries are a result of any blogger doing some sort of personal interview or serious investigative journalism. Most blogs are commentary on some issue that would only have come to their attention because of traditional media. Because the vast majority of the blog world depends on this media, it\'s ludicrous to think that blogging could ever replace it.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.10) Gecko/20050716 Firefox/1.0.6','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(132,276,'Jeremy','jziskind@ucla.edu','','71.106.184.132','2005-11-09 21:30:44','2005-11-10 04:30:44','I  forgot to mention that most professors also hold positions in the private sector outside of academia and thus hold a unique perspective on both.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.10) Gecko/20050716 Firefox/1.0.6','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(133,276,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','68.227.8.214','2005-11-10 00:07:23','2005-11-10 07:07:23','Working in the private sector is different from having had a job. Being a consultant or \'working\' for a think-tank allows one ample opportunity to divorce one\'s self from reality.\r\n\r\nAlso, you\'re sorely mistaken if you think that academia is some kind of freedom from bureaucracy. If you want to find good examples of committees that study committees, the first place to look is a school. Or the senate.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(134,267,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-11-10 06:05:05','2005-11-10 13:05:05','Yeah, the more bold, the more scary the reading list. I tend to deal with that by ignoring the vast majority of my list, and trying to keep up with only 5-10 blogs. The rest I look at periodically.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(135,116,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','80.58.35.107','2005-11-11 18:44:52','2005-11-12 01:44:52','Yes, you\'re absolutely right. I think I intended to include that in the whole \"investigative journalism\" subcategory, but I didn\'t really state it explicitly. \n\nThe vast majority of discussion on the blogosphere is about something that\'s either been included in a press release or on the front page of Yahoo! (which might as well be news).',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.0; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(136,276,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','80.58.35.107','2005-11-11 18:52:47','2005-11-12 01:52:47','Regardless of the bureaucrotic nonsense, academia is still fairly important to the scientific sector, and probably other areas as well. I think that a good portion of genetic and medicinal research is conducted on university grounds through various government research grants that aren\'t often given to corporations. \n\nYes, corporations and the private sector are those who make investments in developing and bringing to market specific drugs, which is due largely to the ridiculously high development (and patent) costs involved in medicine, but I still think academia is uselful. \n\nThat is not to say, however, that they shouldn\'t try to write in plain english ;)',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.0; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(139,276,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-11-14 11:17:13','2005-11-14 18:17:13','Pretty much any huge breakthrough in technology was done by some guy, not some committee or scientists union, and usually not even the standard life-long college cats. Cars? Some guy. Airplanes? TV and Radio? Some guy. Optical media? Some guy. Computers? Some guy. Any software that anyone actually uses? Some guy. Academics may have invented the internet, but they were a little short-sighted (to say the least) on it\'s application. While we depend on academics to do the oh-so-important jobs of scratching their beards over whether Lincoln was gay and making our penises as hard as possible, I think we\'ve been led to treasure them a bit too highly. They\'re not the engine of progress- they\'re wrap-around.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(140,264,'SEO vs. Blogs at Jason Preston','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=288','216.119.127.83','2005-11-15 10:41:22','2005-11-15 17:41:22','[...] Second of all, my post on Mechanical Turk, hardly a scholarly work of enormous import, is hit #5 if you type &#8220;Mturk&#8221; into Google and search. That&#8217;s ridiculous.      // Used for showing and hiding user information in the comment form function ShowUtils() { document.getElementById(\"authorinfo\").style.display = \"\"; document.getElementById(\"showinfo\").style.display = \"none\"; document.getElementById(\"hideinfo\").style.display = \"\"; } function HideUtils() { document.getElementById(\"authorinfo\").style.display = \"none\"; document.getElementById(\"showinfo\").style.display = \"\"; document.getElementById(\"hideinfo\").style.display = \"none\"; } [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.2','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(141,293,'Hillary','Hillary.A.Preston@Dartmouth.edu','','129.170.162.195','2005-11-18 09:47:00','2005-11-18 16:47:00','Let me be the first to say that that is not fair.  Especially since it\'s not even showing at the local theater and I probably won\'t get to see it until I\'m home for Thanksgiving.  I hope it was good though.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(142,293,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-11-18 11:28:31','2005-11-18 18:28:31','It was excellent',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(159,267,'B2B Digital Media','','http://b2bdigitalmedia.blogspot.com/2005/11/rss-growing-pains.html','72.9.234.70','2005-11-27 20:24:12','2005-11-28 03:24:12','<strong>RSS growing pains</strong>\n\nAs liberating as RSS can be for readers and publishers, incorporating it into your daily routine isn\'t as easy as it looks, and may take years before mainstream users adopt it, if ever.',0,'1','HaloScan/2.1 (http://www.haloscan.com/; Sender username: dnewcorn)','trackback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(160,308,'Josh','josh@nonhuman.net','http://blog.nonhuman.net','216.73.132.134','2005-11-28 11:26:55','2005-11-28 18:26:55','It shouldn\'t matter if bloggers are journalists or not because <em>everyone</em> should enjoy equal freedom of speech, not just some journalistic elite. The creation of the so-called \'fourth estate\' was really just a way of usurping the rights of the public and reserving them for a select group.\n\nThe press does not have a monopoly on the truth and therefore should not have a monopoly of protected speech.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/416.11 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/416.12','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(161,308,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-11-28 12:00:01','2005-11-28 19:00:01','I wholeheartedly agree with basically everything J.S. Mill ever wrote on free speech. Unfortunately, this is not a matter of protected speech. \r\n\r\nAs the laws stand now (and have stood for a long time), people have every right to state their opinions out loud, in a blog, in letters, however they like so long as it\'s not putting others in physical danger. \r\n\r\nThe question here is whether or not bloggers should be able to keep their sources secret from the most powerful judicial force in the country. I think that\'s ridiculous. If someone is a blogger practicing journalism--great, they should go get a license that lets them <i>be a journalist</i>. \r\n\r\nBut just creating a blog shouldn\'t make people journalists ;)',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(162,268,'Space Blankets at Jason Preston','','http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=319','216.119.127.83','2005-11-29 07:14:31','2005-11-29 14:14:31','[...] Although it doesnâ€™t look like NASA has done much recently aside from providing public explosions, I think thereâ€™s still some deep-seated sense of glamour in searching the stars for other aliens and, more importantly, a better way to preserve space piss. Letâ€™s face it, who doesnâ€™t want to be an astronaut? [...]',0,'1','The Incutio XML-RPC PHP Library -- WordPress/1.5.2','pingback',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(177,322,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.118.10','2005-11-30 19:14:23','2005-12-01 02:14:23','Say what you want, those things were awesome.  Those and the \"Encyclopedia Brown\" books (which were basically the same thing) were the mainstay of my literary repertoire back in the day.  And I was in the PRISM program, which obviously means that I was fantastically smart.  Plus I use words like \"repertoire.\"',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(179,322,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.17','2005-12-01 05:26:44','2005-12-01 12:26:44','My libarary was constructed from <i>Goosebumps</i> and <i>Lord of the Rings</i>, but to each their own.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(180,326,'Corvus','manbytesblog@pjsattic.com','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus','68.174.71.169','2005-12-01 06:25:17','2005-12-01 13:25:17','I don\'t talk about it much, for fear of being thought looney, but if I wear my cell phone on my person for any length of time, the area around it will vibrate upon occasion, making me think I\'m receiving a call.\r\n\r\nIf I carry it in the same spot for any length of time, the area will begin to vibrate even when the phone isn\'t on my person.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Konqueror/3.5; Linux 2.6.11.4-21.9-smp; X11; i686; en_US) KHTML/3.5.0 (like Gecko)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(181,326,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-12-01 09:59:37','2005-12-01 16:59:37','That definitely happens to me. \r\n\r\nUntil about three months ago, I carried my phone in my right-hand pocket, and my right leg still twitches occasionally, and I have to remind myself that no, my phone is <i>not</i> in that pocket, and no, it is <i>not</i> vibrating. \r\n\r\nThese things are probably so much worse for us than anybody realizes. \r\n\r\nThe problem is, as I\'m looking for a better place to carry it, I\'m discovering that all pockets on clothes everywhere are either right next to my heart or next to my general waist region.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(182,326,'Corvus','manbytesblog@pjsattic.com','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus','68.174.71.169','2005-12-01 11:14:51','2005-12-01 18:14:51','I\'m not the only one?!  Well, that\'s both more frightening and a great relief.\r\n\r\nI\'m thinking of building some sort of armature I can wear that\'ll suspend my phone a couple of feet away from me at all times.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Konqueror/3.5; Linux 2.6.11.4-21.9-smp; X11; i686; en_US) KHTML/3.5.0 (like Gecko)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(184,319,'John Androsavich','jra6@pitt.edu','','139.184.30.18','2005-12-01 14:28:53','2005-12-01 21:28:53','So here is the real deal on the infamous \"space pen\" thanks to our beloved snopes.com which, in the case of Hannah, is now a verb. So I am theoretically snopsing you right now Jason:\r\n\r\nNASA never asked Paul C. Fisher to produce a pen. When the astronauts began to fly, like the Russians, they used pencils, but the leads sometimes broke and became a hazard by floating in the [capsule\'s] atmosphere where there was no gravity. They could float into an eye or nose or cause a short in an electrical device. In addition, both the lead and the wood of the pencil could burn rapidly in the pure oxygen atmosphere. Paul Fisher realized the astronauts needed a safer and more dependable writing instrument, so in July 1965 he developed the pressurized ball pen, with its ink enclosed in a sealed, pressurized ink cartridge. Fisher sent the first samples to Dr. Robert Gilruth, Director of the Houston Space Center. The pens were all metal except for the ink, which had a flash point above 200Â°C. The sample Space Pens were thoroughly tested by NASA. They passed all the tests and have been used ever since on all manned space flights, American and Russian. All research and developement costs were paid by Paul Fisher. No development costs have ever been charged to the government. \r\n\r\nBecause of the fire in Apollo 1, in which three Astronauts died, NASA required a writing instrument that would not burn in a 100% oxygen atmosphere. It also had to work in the extreme conditions of outer space: \r\nIn a vacuum. \r\nWith no gravity. \r\nIn hot temperatures of  150Â°C in sunlight and also in the cold shadows of space where the temperatures drop to -120Â°C \r\n(NASA tested the pressurized Space Pens at -50Â°C, but because of the residential [sic] heat in the pen it also writes for many minutes in the cold shadows.) \r\n\r\nFisher spent over one million dollars in trying to perfect the ball point pen before he made his first successful pressurized pens in 1965. Samples were immediately sent to Dr. Robert Gilruth, Manager of the Houston Space Center, where they were thoroughly tested and approved for use in Space in September 1965. In December 1967 he sold 400 Fisher Space Pens to NASA for $2.95 each. \r\n\r\nLead pencils were used on all Mercury and Gemini space flights and all Russian space flights prior to 1968. Fisher Space Pens are more dependable than lead pencils and cannot create the hazard of a broken piece of lead floating through the gravity-less atmosphere.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(193,335,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.118.140','2005-12-06 07:59:41','2005-12-06 14:59:41','I do that pretty much every day, so I hope not.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(194,335,'Hillary','Hillary.A.Preston@Dartmouth.edu','','129.170.216.170','2005-12-06 11:11:34','2005-12-06 18:11:34','Yeah I do that a lot too.  The worst part is how much it tingles as you regain feeling. Since it\'s not like you\'ve put a tourniquet on or anything, you can\'t be completely shutting off circulation, so it can\'t be that bad for you(probably not great for you either though).',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1; SV1)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(195,335,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.18','2005-12-06 11:30:56','2005-12-06 18:30:56','That\'s the first time it happened to me. Damn, and here I thought I was being unique! ;)\n\nI still have this image in my head of me ripping my useless arm off and bludgeoning a doctor with it until he re-attatches it. I imagine that, aside from the immense pain, blood loss, and pointless gore, it would be pretty funny. A good story for posterity, anyway.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(196,338,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-12-06 13:48:53','2005-12-06 20:48:53','First of all, he has no American civil liberties. He\'s not an American. You can argue that his human rights were violated, or that we were mean, or that Merkel should seek castigation of the US or something, but it confuses the issue to say that his civil liberties were violated. It\'s like getting mad because his right to vote in a US election was denied.\r\n\r\nThis is also the same for enemy combatants, which the media has done a stellar job of confusing. If you capture a Jordanian soldier, you have a prisoner of war. You can negotiate his release with the Jordanian government, and he is entitled to certain protections that have been laid out for soldiers of countries. Jihadists, Al-Qaeda, insurgents, terrorists and the like fight for no country and have no specific protections and are certainly not entitled to the rights of US citizens (judgment under our legal system, access to a speedy trial, so on). You can argue that that\'s mean or that we should change it, but that\'s just not the way it is.\r\n\r\nThis guy seems to fall into a different category, as he was identified (mistakenly) as an enemy, but I\'m guessing he wasn\'t baring arms or anything so he wouldn\'t necessarily be an enemy combatant. That\'s probably why he wasn\'t shot on sight. It also doesn\'t say what was done to him, and I\'m sorry if I just don\'t trust the ACLU\'s definition of torture, which apparently includes listening to rap music and seeing boobs. Torture is peeling off the soles off your feet and making you walt across salt, or shoving bamboo shoots under your fingernails. \"Humiliation\" and being \"offended\" and listening to 50 Cent REALLY loud is almost laughable considering that hardly 50 years ago torture was like, vivisection and appendage removal.\r\n\r\nI\'m not even saying the guy shouldn\'t get reparations or that he shouldn\'t be able to sue, but I do think that his case is probably being overstated for maximum political effect and that Seattle\'s smoking ban is a bigger indication of our dwindling liberties.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(198,336,'Furry Lap Flounder','jra6@pitt.edu','','139.184.30.17','2005-12-07 05:58:28','2005-12-07 12:58:28','Sir Jason....I certainly hope that you received payment in the form of a \nBelgian waffle for your proofreading troubles....or should i say THE Belgian waffle....yes i shall say that\n\np.s.  shes into some kinky hard shit',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(199,336,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.19','2005-12-07 07:21:18','2005-12-07 14:21:18','Oh John. What in hell is a Furry Lap Flounder?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(200,338,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','139.184.30.16','2005-12-07 07:37:47','2005-12-07 14:37:47','I forget where the reference is, and I\'m unfortuantely far too lazy to fact-check my memory (at least i\'ll admit it!), but I\'m pretty sure that with the PATRIOT act American citizens can be declared \"enemy combatants\" i.e. terrorists by \"the government\" (some branch of law enforcement) and basically get screwed hardcore. \n\nWhile a German citizen does not, technically, have the protection of <i>American</i> law, I feel like there are certain universal human rights that are being blatantly ignored by international law enforcement. There\'s no reason we should treat (apparently) non-threatening citizens of other soverign states with any less respect for those basic rights than we treat our own. The automatic assumption that Americans deserve better by virtue of the fact that they were born in a different geographical location is a little bit ridiculous.\n\nAlso, whether or not smoking bans are more telling examples of infringing on civil liberties is inconsequential; if this will make people pay attention and <i>do something</i> about preserving them, that\'s good enough for me.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.12) Gecko/20050915 Firefox/1.0.7','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(202,338,'Kyle','kyle@kylecoberly.com','http://www.kylecoberly.com','12.110.40.22','2005-12-07 09:49:10','2005-12-07 16:49:10','I don\'t think anyone thinks we deserve better because of where we live, but our rights as Americans are kind of like our property. We pay for the system that creates those rights, and generations of our soldiers have died so that we can have them, not so that the rest of the world can have them. Other countries can also have these rights, but it\'s *their* responsibility to maintain them. If Merkel wanted to impose sanctions on us for this or something, she\'d be within her rights to do so.\r\n\r\nThe human rights you\'re referring to derive from this- it\'s called the UDR (http://www.un.org/Overview/rights.html). Unfortunately, it was crafted by one of the most useless organizations on Earth, so none of those points are or ever have been enforceable. You can\'t lose something you never had.\r\n\r\nThe policy toward unlawful enemy combatants is spelled out here http://www.cfr.org/publication.html?id=5312.\r\n\r\nLike I said, I think reparations should be awarded to the guy, but I think this is less a 10 guity men/1 innocent man thing and more of an omlette/broke a few eggs sort of thing.',0,'1','Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.0)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(208,338,'Jens','kosstamojan@hotmail.com','','207.180.118.140','2005-12-08 11:12:35','2005-12-08 18:12:35','I may have misinterpreted, but I think Jason may have been referring to the rights of man more in the God-given sense (IE \"We hold these truths to be self-evident,\" \"unalienable Rights\" and etc.), as opposed to the actual official civil rights that the US pledged to protect in the UN declaration, and I tend to agree with him.  Not to be confrontational, but it IS somewhat ironic that the United States abandon the ideas that were formative of its inception at the exact same time that it\'s trying to sculpt a new independent nation.  Maybe what we really ought to do is go all out and tax Iraq\'s tea.\n\nAlso, I\'ve always loved that omelette metaphor, partially because it\'s always used by the bad guy in cheesy action movies and political thrillers, but mainly because the idea of an omelette formed from human flesh and indignity just seems like it would be downright unappetizing.\n\nThat smoking ban is kind of ridiculous, isn\'t it?  Don\'t look at me, I didn\'t vote for it.\n\nBy the way, Kyle, what would your definition of torture be?  Would it include waterboarding?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.7.8) Gecko/20050511 Firefox/1.0.4','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
