	(3113,708,'Ben Adlin','badlin@oxy.edu','http://www.adlin.com','66.167.137.7','2006-06-24 05:03:30','2006-06-24 12:03:30','I think it\'s cute that the post right below this one is called \"Too Much Hilarity.\"\n\nStill, buddy, I\'m really sorry you\'re having such a hard time with this. Sounds like more trouble than BlueHost...',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/417.9.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3115,708,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','209.91.62.197','2006-06-24 11:45:31','2006-06-24 18:45:31','Yes, what fun it has been. I\'m beginning to think I should have just kept the one I had originally.\n\nShould it really be this difficult to give someone close to a thousand dollars?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.8.0.4) Gecko/20060508 Firefox/1.5.0.4','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(3132,670,'HÃ¥kon','sorrynoneed@mailinator.com','','84.20.109.251','2006-06-25 06:22:21','2006-06-25 13:22:21','The find as you type feature in Firefox is, as you say, a really cool feature. Opera has had a \'find in page\' option in their search field for a long time, but it was not until recently that I discovered that if you just press \'.\', you\'ll get the same thing. Perhaps this is what you wanted.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, there is actually a reasonable explanation for why Opera won\'t just let you start typing like you can in Firefox (although I find that so intuitive I often stretch for a keyboard while looking for something even in a book). Opera is made to be quite a lot more flexible in respect to your surfing preferences, and this involves using a great deal of hotkeys. If you just started typing, you\'d suddenly press keys that actually meant something and send your browser dancing far away from the page you were on. I guess the most used hotkeys that do not require the ctrl key to be pressed too are z and x, which work as back and forward. When you add up the ability to surf using just your keyboard, your mouse, your voice or any mix of the three, you end up with a stunningly powerful and functional setup.\r\n\r\nHave fun :)',0,'1','Opera/9.00 (Windows NT 5.1; U; en)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3150,704,'Jens','the_tubanator@hotmail.com','','71.112.44.3','2006-06-25 17:09:51','2006-06-26 00:09:51','I don\'t know, but I do know that my dad has Qwest DSL service at his office, and that Qwest is the only company that he can get service from.  He wouldn\'t have Qwest if he could have something else, because Qwest is ass-terrible.  So, at the very least, my dad would be stuck with it if Qwest decided to do it, and his office is in Bellevue, not, like, Nointernetville, so I\'m sure he wouldn\'t be an exceptional case.\n\nPersonally, I just don\'t like the idea of it.  I mean, I think it\'s possible that a free market system could compensate for all this, but given that the market is currently very few, very large providers that require significant effort to switch between, and that these providers have a history of abusing this power even when they technically didn\'t have it, I\'d prefer not to take the chance.  And if things did go wrong, who would we turn to?  The FCC?',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.8.0.3) Gecko/20060426 Firefox/1.5.0.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3169,708,'Ben Adlin','badlin@oxy.edu','http://www.adlin.com','66.167.252.164','2006-06-26 13:48:43','2006-06-26 20:48:43','I\'ll give you much better customer service if you choose to give me close to a thousand dollars.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/417.9.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3175,704,'Jason','xwing@seanet.com','http://','209.91.62.197','2006-06-26 16:17:04','2006-06-26 23:17:04','Yeah, I\'d like to think that leglislating the net neutrality rules is the solution, but I think it\'ll just cause problems down the line as rules and definitions online change.\n\nThe best solution is clearly to have a competitve free market, but too many people are making too much money from letting three or four big companies fuck the public over.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.8.0.4) Gecko/20060508 Firefox/1.5.0.4','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(3176,707,'Jason','jason@calacanis.com','http://www.calacanis.com','66.171.49.245','2006-06-26 17:09:01','2006-06-27 00:09:01','... just build a track record. You notice all my press comes with the note that a) I did Silicon Alley Reporter and b) I did Weblogs, Inc.\n\nThe reason press people even take a meeting with me is because I have a track record. When I started SAR no one knew who I was and I didn\'t get any attention from the press for at least six months.\n\nThe fact is the press isn\'t that important... product is most important. Make great product and people will use it. The press just gives you a little head start. If your product sucks people won\'t use it... the press just gets you the first wave.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.8.0.3) Gecko/20060426 Firefox/1.5.0.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3195,715,'Josh','josh@joshourisman.com','http://www.joshourisman.com','72.18.249.233','2006-06-27 10:40:27','2006-06-27 17:40:27','Now hopefully they\'ll actually find the nerve to do something about it...',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/417.9.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(3298,728,'Josh','josh@joshourisman.com','http://www.joshourisman.com','72.18.249.233','2006-07-03 14:08:25','2006-07-03 21:08:25','Well they <em>used</em> to come with GraphicConverter, which is a great little free (shareware) graphic editing app. But now they apparently don\'t, which is really annoying (especially since when it came on Macs it came with a license and you didn\'t have to wait 30 seconds before you could use it while you decided not to buy it).',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418.8 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/419.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
