	(12138,1052,'Ben','badlin@oxy.edu','http://www.adlin.com','67.160.127.197','2007-01-13 04:26:44','2007-01-13 11:26:44','sure, there\'s always the apple tax to factor in, but i dunno, friend. phones are expensive when they\'re the new kids on the block. look hat how much the 1st gen razr cost (even with a plan) back when it first came out.\n\nin the next couple revisions, one or more things has to happen:\n\n- hard drive to get massive (so it can actually replace my ipod)\n- price to drop significantly\n- craaaaaazy features (app development, gps, iris scan, car detailing, etc.)\n\nalso, do we know what the battery life is? (not that it won\'t improve)\n\nthat said, it\'s a flat-out beeeeeautiful machine. johnathan ive is the shit.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418.9.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/419.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(12171,1052,'Jason','j@jason-preston.com','http://','24.41.60.9','2007-01-13 11:37:23','2007-01-13 18:37:23','The problem with the price Ben is that the iPhone costs 5 or 6 hundred dollars *with* a contract, whereas most other expensive phones cost that much *before* they are subsidized by the phone company with a contract. \n\nThat said, there are still people who will <a href=\"http://teresacentric.com/2007/01/my-two-questions-about-the-iphone/\" rel=\"nofollow\">have to have it</a>.\n\nI\'m also waiting for an iPhone that allows third party applications and after this lawsuit business gets figured out with apple\'s shitty DRM.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; Intel Mac OS X; en-US; rv:1.8.1.1) Gecko/20061204 Firefox/2.0.0.1','',0,1,NULL,'N'),
	(12179,1052,'Ben','badlin@oxy.edu','http://www.adlin.com','67.160.127.197','2007-01-13 13:29:52','2007-01-13 20:29:53','When the RAZR came out, it was available for $500 *with* a contract. Without a plan it was a whopping $800 (Wikipedia). I think I payed about $60 for mine with a plan.\n\nCould that be an exception to the rule? Sure. Maybe the RAZR was simply priced too high to begin with, targeting an elite niche rather than the average consumer. Still, it illustrates how radically an initial price point can change within just a couple years.\n\nI generally think all the price-point criticism is a little premature. Yeah, $600 is a bit too pricey for an iPhone, but I say it\'s a surprisingly fair starting point, provided either the price drops or the functionality increases. I mean, if I can eventually replace my 60 gig iPod with an 80 gig iPhone (assuming that eventually happens), I\'m willing to shell out as much as the iPod costs, plus another 50-100 bucks.\n\nMy biggest gripe is that it\'s only available with Cingular, and that an unlocked version probably won\'t be available at Apple stores (though I wouldn\'t be surprised to see the formerâ€”if not the latterâ€”change within the next year or two).\n\nThat said, don\'t even get me started with how fucked-up I think mobile plans are.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418.9.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/419.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(12185,1050,'Ben','badlin@oxy.edu','http://www.adlin.com','67.160.127.197','2007-01-13 14:19:19','2007-01-13 21:19:19','\"The fact of the matter is that a lot of people, dare I even say the majority of people in the United States have pretty rational reasons for their beliefs. And before you ask, yes, Iâ€™m counting religious belief as a â€œrational reason.â€\"\n______________\n\nI think it\'s important to make the distinction between opinions, beliefs, etc. being *rational* and being *natural*. I think the majority of Americans react naturally to the state of affairs in our country, but not really rationally.',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; U; PPC Mac OS X; en) AppleWebKit/418.9.1 (KHTML, like Gecko) Safari/419.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
