	(40787,1245,'Corvus','manbytesblog@pjsattic.com','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus','72.94.220.89','2007-05-21 16:44:55','2007-05-21 23:44:55','\'Grats!',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (X11; U; Linux i686; en-US; rv:1.8.1.3) Gecko/20061201 Firefox/2.0.0.3 (Ubuntu-feisty)','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
	(41726,1249,'Corvus','manbytesblog@pjsattic.com','http://blog.pjsattic.com/corvus','67.93.219.138','2007-05-24 10:40:03','2007-05-24 17:40:03','\"And besides, entertainment is so much more entertaining if you just sit back and allow yourself to be entertained. More people should try it.\"\n\nThat\'s a highly subjective opinion! What if some of us aren\'t coded in such a way as to be able to accept the messages inherent in even the most banal of entertainment media? What if our enjoyment actually comes from examining the subtext of every narrative we experience?\n\nYour argument is suspiciously close to \"you think too much,\" an accusation that rankles me to no end!\n\n1. Couch forts: reenactment of childhood ritual which recalls a time when we felt protected and safe\n2. Watching someone with sea legs on dry land: subconscious assessment of survival probability (I can run faster if a Puma attacks. ha ha!)\n3. Bad Boys II: shallow and unintentional commentary on the casual ignorance of other\'s cultural norms\n4. Shiny objects: oooo, pretty! shiny often equals jangly. keeping jangly bits around  alerts us to intruders with sea legs stumbling about our homes\n5. Jeff Goldblum: I got nothin\'!',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 5.1; en-US; rv:1.8.1.3) Gecko/20070309 Firefox/2.0.0.3','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
