	(128707,1277,'Dr. Farnsworth','aimamput3e@gmail.com','','80.121.75.179','2009-12-04 09:03:26','2009-12-04 16:03:26','Good news, everyone! I had the same problem with Windows 7 64bit. I have a 160GB HDD and Windows claimed that I had only 1GB remaining diskspace, even though, when when looking at the properties of all the folders on C: (including the invisible) it said I was only using 64 GB. So I searched around and read alot about space being used for recovery and other crap, but disabling recovery, deleting backupfiles and windows.old only gave me back about 9GB. So i downloaded linux ubuntu and booted it (no ned for installation, just boot it) and because linux doesn\'t give a sh*** about windows file restrictions, I was able to see what the space was used for.\r\nLooks like, when I was installing Windows 7, the the drive wasn\'t formated and the old files of my user account from Windows XP where still on the drive, but invisible in Windows 7. To sum it up, SHIFT + DELETE solved the problem :)',0,'1','Mozilla/5.0 (Windows; U; Windows NT 6.1; de; rv:1.9.1.5) Gecko/20091102 Firefox/3.5.5','',0,0,NULL,'N'),
