	(721,1,'2006-06-27 15:43:51','2006-06-27 22:43:51','Until midnight, anyway. T-mobile is retarted enough to charge me for incoming text messages. \r\n\r\n<center><img id=\"image720\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/06/notext.jpg\" alt=\"notext\"/></center>','Nobody text me!',0,'','publish','open','open','','nobody-text-me','','','2006-06-27 15:43:51','2006-06-27 22:43:51','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/06/27/nobody-text-me/',0,'post','',0),
	(722,1,'2006-06-29 01:40:34','2006-06-29 08:40:34','One of the cool things about delicious is that it allows you to keep a reverse blog. \r\n\r\nFor example, I occasionally tag articles I\'ve written or cool links I find around the internet for people who I think will be interested. \r\n\r\nBasically, with a link, short description, and a personalized list, everyone else is making posts specifically for you. I feel like this is reverse blogging. ','Reverse blogging',0,'','publish','open','open','','reverse-blogging','','','2006-06-29 01:40:34','2006-06-29 08:40:34','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/06/29/reverse-blogging/',0,'post','',0),
	(723,1,'2006-06-29 15:13:18','2006-06-29 22:13:18','I was going through my old file drawer and trying to make everything look like it\'s in some semblance of order (this is the first time in my life I\'ve bothered to do this - it occurred to me that sooner or later I <i>would</i> need some sort of file storage system), when I found something vexing. <br /><br />An envelope very carefully (and clearly, and multiple times) dated December 4, 2004. It\'s addressed to me, sealed and stamped, and from myself. <br /><br />The only thing on it that suggests something out of the ordinary is the little \"TMA\" after my name in the part that addresses the envelope to me. <br /><br />I remember doing this. I remember coming up with a really really good idea, and, not wanting to bother with patent lawyers, etc, I decided to describe the idea fully, and then mail it to myself so that should I ever need to prove in court that the idea was mine (first), I could brandish the sealed and dated envelope, striking fear into the hearts of those that dared copy me. <br /><br />The problem is that I can\'t remember what my great idea was. So now I\'m faced with a difficult decision - do I open the envelope, thus ruining my brilliant plan, but possibly revealing to me my own former brilliance (which I would then, of course, use to get ridiculously rich)? or do I leave well enough alone, just in case someone <i>does</i> think of it, at which point I will have my proof?<br /><br />I do not know.<br />','Hooray for December 2004',0,'','publish','open','open','','hooray-for-december-2004','','','2006-06-29 15:13:19','2006-06-29 22:13:19','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/06/29/hooray-for-december-2004/',0,'post','',0),
	(724,1,'2006-06-29 15:37:17','2006-06-29 22:37:17','<a href=\"http://www.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,3-2248170,00.html\">This</a> is simply amazing. <br /><br />Sure, the information printed may make a dent in tracking down terrorists. But the fault is in <i>whoever leaked the fucking information</i>, not the New York Times. Go fix your own problems. <br /><br />The NYT is a <i>news organization</i>. Does free press mean nothing anymore? If the government can dictate what the press can or cannot print, even if it does so by threat of legal action, then we not longer have freedom of information, speech, or thought. <br /><br />It\'s disgraceful to suggest that these liberties are expendable. Next I expect the White House to line up and spit on Ben Franklin\'s grave. <br /><br /><blockquote>GEORGE BUSH and Dick Cheney are calling <i>The</i> <i>New York Times</i> a disgrace, Republican congressmen say it is guilty of treason and demand the prosecution of the Editor, while a right-wing radio presenter suggests most of its readers must be â€œjihadistsâ€.<br /><p>They never need much encouragement to attack Americaâ€™s most venerable title which is, at least in criticsâ€™ eyes, a beacon for antiwar sentiment.  </p><p><table valign=\"TOP\" align=\"right\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tbody><tr><td name=\"mpuHeader\" id=\"mpuHeader\"><br /></td></tr><tr align=\"right\"><td align=\"right\"><script type=\"text/javascript\">NI_MPU(\'middle\');</script><br /></td></tr></tbody></table>But this time the anger is palpably stronger.  The newspaperâ€™s offence was to publish an article revealing that the US Administration had kept tabs on suspected terrorists by tapping into bank records which track global transactions.  </p><p>â€œWhat we did was fully authorised under the law,â€ said President Bush.  â€œAnd the disclosure of this is disgraceful.  Weâ€™re at war with a bunch of people who want to hurt the United States of America, and for people to leak that programme, and for a newspaper to publish it, does great harm.â€&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <a href=\"http://www.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,3-2248170,00.html\">read more...</a><br /></p></blockquote>\r\n','Give me liberty or give me...less?',0,'','publish','open','open','','give-me-liberty-or-give-meless','','','2006-06-29 15:38:54','2006-06-29 22:38:54','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/06/29/give-me-liberty-or-give-meless/',0,'post','',0),
	(725,1,'2006-07-01 02:12:12','2006-07-01 09:12:12','As I\'m working for Parnassus Ventures, the company that does the <a href=\"http://www.blogbusinesssummit.com\">Blog Business Summit</a>, I\'ve been spending some time working around blogs built in both Movable Type (the BBS page) and Wordpress (basically everything I do, including the not-yet-live Parnassus site). \r\n\r\nIt\'s kind of odd to see the two engines stacked up against each other. Teresa, who webmasters the BBS site, just recently figured out how to make the post titles permalinks, which is how Wordpress is by default. This seems ridiculously overcomplicated. \r\n\r\nHowever, if you want to create a sub blog in Wordpress, you literally have to do a second install, and then re-work your navigation menu to reflect that. I think the process is somewhat (although not entirely) easier in Movable Type. \r\n\r\nI still think there\'s precious little that can be done in Movable Type (which you pay for) that can\'t be done in Wordpress (which is free). Hooray open source. ','Little differences between Movable Type and Wordpress',0,'','publish','open','open','','little-differences-between-movable-type-and-wordpress','','','2006-07-01 02:12:12','2006-07-01 09:12:12','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/01/little-differences-between-movable-type-and-wordpress/',0,'post','',0),
	(726,1,'2006-07-01 02:21:17','2006-07-01 09:21:17','It\'s strange to me how some thoughts are really condusive to blogging and others aren\'t. \r\n\r\nA lot of the time I think that if I just kept a running tab of my neverending stream of consciousness, I could have a zillion interesting posts per day, but then I realize that I sometimes I go for days where I\'m either too busy to make a thoughtful post, or none of my thoughts would really make good blog posts. \r\n\r\nFor example, if I\'m wandering around a mall and I think to myself \"How long does it take Auntie Anne\'s to make a pretzel?\" it\'s probably not good blogging material. \r\n\r\nI go whole days on thoughts like that. It\'s incredible. I think that\'s why I made a \"nonsense\" category. ','Pretzel thoughts',0,'','publish','open','open','','pretzel-thoughts','','','2006-07-01 02:21:17','2006-07-01 09:21:17','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/01/pretzel-thoughts/',0,'post','',0),
	(727,1,'2006-07-02 11:51:15','2006-07-02 18:51:15','Last night, just for fun, my friends and I watched Batman Begins on a screen the size of a Queensize bedsheet. \r\n\r\nBasically, we tacked the sheet to the wall and set up my projector just to see how it would look ridiculously large. It looks <em>nice</em>. \r\n\r\nI really couldn\'t tell you there were any problems. It looked great, even on a wrinkled sheet. When I get this thing set up and roof-mounted with a proper screen, it\'s going to look gorgeous. ','Huuuuuuge screen TV',0,'','publish','open','open','','huuuuuuge-screen-tv','','','2006-07-02 11:51:15','2006-07-02 18:51:15','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/02/huuuuuuge-screen-tv/',0,'post','',0),
	(728,1,'2006-07-03 14:01:36','2006-07-03 21:01:36','If I were that Mac dude in those commercials, I\'d be pretty embarrassed that I went on this big spiel about coming pre-packaged with cool applications but then it turns out that the Mac doesn\'t come with the most basic image editing funtions installed.\r\n\r\nLike resizing an image, for example. Not possible without going to find software online. \r\n\r\nEmbarrassing. ','Little issues with Macs',0,'','publish','open','open','','little-issues-with-macs','','','2006-07-03 14:01:36','2006-07-03 21:01:36','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/03/little-issues-with-macs/',0,'post','',3),
	(729,1,'2006-07-03 18:40:52','2006-07-04 01:40:52','','flicker technorati ranking',0,'','inherit','open','open','','flicker-technorati-ranking','','','2006-07-03 18:40:52','2006-07-04 01:40:52','',730,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/fg-technorati.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(730,1,'2006-07-03 18:47:15','2006-07-04 01:47:15','<img id=\"image729\" align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/fg-technorati.png\" alt=\"flicker technorati ranking\" />According to Technorati, Flicker is now three times as cool as this blog (ranked at 264,711).\r\n\r\nIt\'s pretty cool to watch as Flicker continues to pump out good content because I get to see links coming in from new sites almost every week now. \r\n\r\nI keep checking our Google PageRank, traffic, and Technorati rank almost daily, just to see how they all correlate and (hopefully) how they\'re all going up. \r\n\r\nWhat\'s funny is that Jason-Preston.com has both a higher PageRank and a lot more traffic (although I steadfastly refuse to believe that there are actually 400 people reading this blog every day), but Flicker has a lot more inbound links and has been seeing a lot more growth. \r\n\r\nI guess Google gives a lot of points for just being older. ','Ranking Flicker Gaming',0,'','publish','open','open','','ranking-flicker-gaming','','','2006-07-03 18:48:51','2006-07-04 01:48:51','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/03/ranking-flicker-gaming/',0,'post','',0),
	(731,1,'2006-07-05 17:04:12','2006-07-06 00:04:12','One of things on <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/03/28/my-101-list/\">my 101 list</a> that might be somewhat more achievable than others is the aspiration to be quoted (like on the google homepage). \r\n\r\nI was driving towards the freeway today and I spotted some kids running lemonade stand - I was going to stop and buy some until I noticed that the sign they were holding said \"GATORADE 25 cents.\"\r\n\r\nGatorade? I passed them by. \r\n\r\nBut it inspired me to come up with a sweet, quotable quote:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\nA child\'s lemonade stand is like a paradigm of the American dream, through which we teach our children to steal the work of others and peddle it on the street for money.</blockquote>\r\n\r\n- ME!! (feel free to quote)','Being quotable',0,'','publish','open','open','','being-quotable','','','2006-07-05 17:05:30','2006-07-06 00:05:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/05/being-quotable/',0,'post','',0),
	(732,1,'2006-07-06 20:19:46','2006-07-07 03:19:46','I just had to point out that the story ben found <a href=\"http://adlin.com/?p=69\">is hilarious</a>. \r\n\r\nGo check it out. ','Blatant linkage',0,'','publish','open','open','','blatant-linkage','','\nhttp://adlin.com/?p=69','2006-07-06 20:19:46','2006-07-07 03:19:46','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/06/blatant-linkage/',0,'post','',0),
	(733,1,'2006-07-07 03:41:56','2006-07-07 10:41:56','Just got back from watching the midnight showing of Pirates 2 - I\'m pretty tired so I\'ll keep the thoughts brief. \r\n\r\nIt was a lot better than I expected it to be, and for the most part, as good as the first movie. There was a little too much nostalgia and a few scenes that went just a bit too much over the top, but it\'s definitely a solid picture. \r\n\r\nAlso, you should know before going in that the movie does not end - it leads into the \"second part\" - <a href=\"http://imdb.com/title/tt0449088/\">due out sometime in 2007-ish</a>.','Dead Man\'s Chest',0,'','publish','open','open','','dead-mans-chest','','','2006-07-07 03:41:56','2006-07-07 10:41:56','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/07/dead-mans-chest/',0,'post','',0),
	(734,1,'2006-07-07 13:30:21','2006-07-07 20:30:21','Honestly, sometimes it\'s hard for me to believe people are actually buying cars that guzzle so much gas. <br /><br />The new lexus GS 450h little hybrid sedan thing got a big article in the Seattle Times Today which proudly added that the car gets 28mpg on the highway. <br /><br />That\'s good for a car (at least in the US) - but a <i>hybrid?</i> That\'s ridiculous!<br />','My Civic gets better mileage than Lexus Hybrid',0,'','publish','open','open','','my-civic-gets-better-mileage-that-lexus-hybrid','','','2006-07-07 13:31:08','2006-07-07 20:31:08','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/07/my-civic-gets-better-mileage-that-lexus-hybrid/',0,'post','',2),
	(735,1,'2006-07-09 13:04:00','2006-07-09 20:04:00','It\'s not really that rare to have a dream that puts some random sequence of seemingly unrelated experiences together. \r\n\r\nBut last night was the first time I can remember my dream making a mistake, and then trying to correct it. It\'s actually kind of funny. \r\n\r\nCome August I\'m going to be living off-campus for my Senior year of College, and it\'s the first time I\'ve actually gotten an Apartment and, well, lived off campus. \r\n\r\nIn my dream last night, I retured to school and was going through the usual process of mundane things in interesting ways that only dreams can concieve of, when, about a day into the experience, I realized that I <em>wasn\'t</em> supposed to be living on campus anymore. \r\n\r\nSo I spent the rest of my dream trying to contact my new roommate and get keys to the apartment.\r\n\r\n??','Dreams make mistakes, too',0,'','publish','open','open','','dreams-make-mistakes-too','','','2006-07-09 13:04:00','2006-07-09 20:04:00','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/09/dreams-make-mistakes-too/',0,'post','',0),
	(736,1,'2006-07-09 15:32:44','2006-07-09 22:32:44','I suppose it was only a matter of time until this happened - especially since the Mac dude <em>is</em> kind of&nbsp; pompous jackassy. \r\n\r\n<embed allowScriptAccess=\"never\" width=\"448\" height=\"365\" src=\"http://www.ifilm.com/efp\" quality=\"high\" bgcolor=\"000000\" name=\"efp\" align=\"middle\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" pluginspage=\"http://www.macromedia.com/go/getflashplayer\" flashvars=\"flvbaseclip=2751590\" />','VH1 craps on the Mac Ads',0,'','publish','open','open','','vh1-craps-on-the-mac-ads','','','2006-07-09 15:33:27','2006-07-09 22:33:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/09/vh1-craps-on-the-mac-ads/',0,'post','',0),
	(737,1,'2006-07-09 18:27:46','2006-07-10 01:27:46','Did anyone record the Men\'s Final this morning? I managed to not see it and not record it.','Wimbledon Final',0,'','publish','open','open','','wimbledon-final','','','2006-07-09 18:27:46','2006-07-10 01:27:46','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/09/wimbledon-final/',0,'post','',0),
	(738,1,'2006-07-10 15:00:50','2006-07-10 22:00:50','One of the funny things about everything on Fred Wilson\'s <a href=\"http://onlyonce.blogs.com/onlyonce/2006/03/a_new_member_of.html\">Internet Axis of Evil</a> is that they\'re also all landmarks of success in their own odd ways. \r\n\r\nIn other words, if you\'re getting spammed and stolen from, it means you\'re on the map. <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net\">Flicker</a>\'s on the map - we\'ve been victims of <a href=\"http://avc.blogs.com/a_vc/2005/07/really_simple_s.html\">Really Simple Stealing</a> for a while now, although from very polite and friendly thiefs. I\'m not going to link to them.\r\n\r\nI\'ve been waiting it out because the inbound links will help our google PageRank, and to see if we get any real boost from the re-syndication, but so far it doesn\'t look like it\'s driving much traffic our way, so I\'ll probably ask them to cut it out pretty soon.\r\n\r\nA network is one thing, but it annoys me that people go around stealing content and pasting ads all over it for revenue. It makes me wonder who the hell reads those instead of the original sites. ','Syndicated Stealing',0,'','publish','open','open','','syndicated-stealing','','','2006-07-10 15:00:50','2006-07-10 22:00:50','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/10/syndicated-stealing/',0,'post','',2),
	(739,1,'2006-07-12 13:18:51','2006-07-12 20:18:51','','homelandletter',0,'','inherit','open','open','','homelandletter','','','2006-07-12 13:18:51','2006-07-12 20:18:51','',740,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/homelandletter.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(740,1,'2006-07-12 13:21:26','2006-07-12 20:21:26','My friend <a href=\"http://www.rabidspacedog.com\">Ethan</a> pointed me to <a href=\"http://virus.org.ua/unix/keylog/klog.htm?link\">an article</a> he found on digg today that describes how an engineer found a hidden <a href=\"http://www.keyghost.com/\">hardware keystroke recorder</a> in his new dell laptop.  \r\n\r\nDell support refused to explain the device, and after contacting the police, he was turned to the Department of Homeland security who asked him to submit a freedom of information act request. This is the letter he got back:\r\n\r\n<img id=\"image739\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/homelandletter.png\" alt=\"homelandletter\" />\r\n\r\nEXCUSE ME?\r\n\r\nSo not only do new dell laptops apparently come with hardware that records every keystroke, but you are not allowed to know why it\'s there or what the STOLEN PRIVATE DATA is used for or how it is STORED OR TRANSMITTED??\r\n\r\nThat\'s one damn good reason not to buy a Dell.','Dell laptops spy on their users',0,'','publish','open','open','','dell-laptops-spy-on-their-users','','','2006-07-12 17:45:57','2006-07-13 00:45:57','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/12/dell-laptops-spy-on-their-users/',0,'post','',0),
	(741,1,'2006-07-12 16:58:07','2006-07-12 23:58:07','I know that I\'m one of the few people who didn\'t particularly like Scarface, but damnit, it just wasn\'t that enjoyable. It was the story of some asshole drug dealer who did excessively excessive things and did them in ridiculous ways and none of it was particularly interesting (except for the bath tub scene...that was interesting). \r\n\r\nAnyways I ran across a bit from <a href=\"http://theferrett.livejournal.com/766351.html\">TheFerrett</a> on Tony Montana which makes me feel all jealous that I couldn\'t say it:\r\n\r\n<blockquote><strong>Tony Montana, from Scarface.</strong>\r\nHmm. A thuggish brute rises to power because he\'s so thuggish that no one can get in his way, then dies to his own excess. There was absolutely nothing about Tony Montana I admired â€“ he wasn\'t particularly bright or cunning, he was obsessed and greedy, and in an ideal world this would be seen as a warning to people about not to be this asshole. But alas, apparently the culture looks at what Tony <em>got </em>as opposed to what he <em>is</em>.\r\n\r\nI know, I know, he dies horribly. But not horribly enough. I want a Naked Gun-style horrific death, where he gets shot, and falls off a balcony, and run over by a tractor, and then caught on the end of the tractor and dragged through a nettle field, and then I punch him until he dies.</blockquote>\r\n','Scarface thoughts',0,'','publish','open','open','','scarface-thoughts','','','2006-07-12 16:59:18','2006-07-12 23:59:18','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/12/scarface-thoughts/',0,'post','',0),
	(742,1,'2006-07-12 17:22:07','2006-07-13 00:22:07','1. My latest batch of checks from Bank of America (in LA) that came with the free checking account cost $11, a charge that I was not informed about beforehand nor that I knowingly agreed to. I can live with that. What pisses me off is that they also carry advertisements - so not only did BofA charge <em>me</em> for my checks, but they charged other companies to fucking <em>advertise</em> on my checks. \r\n\r\n2. An article in the <a href=\"http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2003121242_sims12m.html\">Seattle Times today</a> about how Ron Sims has been all about global warming since 1988 carries the following \"response\" from a Republican politician:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\"It\'s largely symbolic, but it allows him to get on the national stage,\" Republican political consultant Randy Pepple said. \"It gets used for partisan reasons to beat up on Republicans because [Republicans] tend to focus on the here-and-now and issues people are facing today.\"</blockquote>\r\n\r\nI hate that people apparently can\'t get over their petty differences to deal with a situation. This idea that we shouldn\'t worry about the glowing embers in the dry grass because they\'re not yet on fire is silly and stupid. \r\n\r\nFolks, <a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2006/03/the_problem_wit.html\">atmosphere cancer</a> is a problem. Let\'s stop yelling at each other and fix it. ','Two entirely unrelated things that upset me',0,'','publish','open','open','','two-entirely-unrelated-things-that-upset-me','','','2006-07-12 17:22:07','2006-07-13 00:22:07','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/12/two-entirely-unrelated-things-that-upset-me/',0,'post','',0),
	(743,1,'2006-07-12 22:50:24','2006-07-13 05:50:24','<a href=\"http://www.longtail.com/the_long_tail/2006/07/and_to_cap_off_.html\">Lycos is finally selling Wired News</a>, which is good news because I had basically stopped reading it since the damn Lycos bar atop the pages annoyed the hell out of me. \r\n\r\nInterestingly enough I never even noticed that Wired News and Wired Magazine were operating under different ownership - I could get to both through the same site so who cares, right?\r\n\r\nAlso, I just bought <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1401302378/sr=8-1/qid=1152769789/ref=pd_bbs_1/103-8525790-9102244?ie=UTF8\">The Long Tail</a> at Barnes and Noble today, I look forward to reading it. ','Good news for Wired (and me)',0,'','publish','open','open','','good-news-for-wired-and-me','','\nhttp://www.longtail.com/the_long_tail/2006/07/and_to_cap_off_.html#trackback','2006-07-12 22:50:24','2006-07-13 05:50:24','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/12/good-news-for-wired-and-me/',0,'post','',0),
	(744,1,'2006-07-13 16:28:06','2006-07-13 23:28:06','Nobody ever told me how important the sitemeter was going to be when I started Flicker. I live by that thing - days are prettier and birds sing a little bit more when we get lots of traffic. \r\n\r\nSometimes the graph really slopes up, and we\'ve gotten a bunch of links from a bunch of different places (technorati likes us), but our base traffic is the same. On days (like today) where we aren\'t really linked from anywhere, our traffic is right back down to the baseline. \r\n\r\nWhat I want to figure out is how to grow the number of people that go \"where should I look today? how about Flicker Gaming\"\r\n\r\nWe need to move from being a site that comes up with some cool stuff to an actual <em>presence</em> in the internet as a gaming site. I\'m not exactly sure what kind of exposure makes that happen, but people need to think Joystick, Kotaku, Flicker Gaming. \r\n\r\nWe need to get onto blogrolls. We need to start showing up at lan parties, conferences, events. We need to shake hands and meet people. Get shout outs from blogs in high places. We need mentions in the mainstream press. We need <em>oomph</em>. \r\n\r\nMost of all we need oomph without having to pay for it. It\'s a tricky road. ','The exciting drudgery',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-exciting-drudgery','','','2006-07-13 16:28:49','2006-07-13 23:28:49','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/13/the-exciting-drudgery/',0,'post','',0),
	(745,1,'2006-07-13 18:06:12','2006-07-14 01:06:12','Man oh man is <a href=\"http://marchtogether.blogspot.com/2006/07/murder-without-conscience.html\">this guy</a> getting <a href=\"http://blog.blogpulse.com/archives/000699.html\">trashed</a> <a href=\"http://teresacentric.typepad.com/files/2006/07/stupid_wingnut_.html\">all over</a> <a href=\"http://www.blogpulse.com/search?query=http%3A%2F%2Fmarchtogether.blogspot.com%2F2006%2F07%2Fmurder-without-conscience.html\">the internet</a>. \r\n\r\nAnd deservedly so.','hahahahahahahahaha',0,'','publish','open','open','','hahahahahahahahaha','','','2006-07-13 18:06:12','2006-07-14 01:06:12','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/13/hahahahahahahahaha/',0,'post','',8),
	(746,1,'2006-07-14 12:37:41','2006-07-14 19:37:41','Instead of spending so much time thinking about these things, I should probably go out a buy a few books on marketing, since I\'ll wager this has all been thunk through several times over. \r\n\r\nMarketing a small blog like <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net\">Flicker</a> seems to boil down to two things:\r\n\r\n<strong>Have something that makes people want to come back to the site</strong>\r\n\r\nThis has to be something that happens on a regular schedule, and it\'s going to have to happen somewhat frequently. For some reason \"regular content\" doesn\'t do the trick for acquiring new eyeballs. For people who already visit regularly, this is probably enough...what you need is a gimmick to get one-time visitors to check back a few times and realize there\'s content worth looking at, and the site is worth bookmarking. \r\n\r\n<strong>Do something that makes people want to share</strong>\r\n\r\nI get e-mails from friends and family all the time that include links to something cool online. People share these things because they want to be a part of it - if I recommend a good book to you, then <em>I get credit</em>, even though I had nothing to do with writing the book. Do something on your site that\'s so cool that people want to be part of it, and they\'ll share it. (Also, duh: make it easy for people to share).\r\n\r\n','Two bullets of cheap marketing',0,'','publish','open','open','','two-bullets-of-cheap-marketing','','','2006-07-19 21:20:07','2006-07-20 04:20:07','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/14/two-bullets-of-cheap-marketing/',0,'post','',0),
	(748,1,'2006-07-16 09:24:10','2006-07-16 16:24:10','I\'ve been reading through Mark Cuban\'s posts on how <a href=\"http://www.blogmaverick.com/entry/1234000380073791/\">the internet is boring</a> and <a href=\"http://www.blogmaverick.com/entry/1234000207073794/\">broadband video is overrated</a>, and I agree with a lot of what he\'s saying (except that the internet isn\'t boring), but I think he (and a lot of companies right now) are looking at things the wrong way. \r\n\r\nThe internet isn\'t exciting because it\'s a bunch of pipes anymore, it\'s the type of stuff that\'s starting to show up on the pipe (largely social interaction, which is basically what has driven human society for eons) - we\'re realizing more and more ways to interact and connect *using* the same infrastructure that\'s been around for years. \r\n\r\nSo I guess to take his analogy of the water pipes, it\'s as though people are starting to see wine come through the pipes. That\'s worth getting excited about. \r\n\r\nCuban is also right that broadband video is never going to work as a traditional replacement to traditional TV - the \'net and <em>broadcast</em> work on fundamentally different levels. Personally, I don\'t want internet TV on my tube, I want regular TV on my computer!\r\n\r\nBut where broadband video really shines is microchunking and sharing - nothing on broadcast medium can do that the way the internet can. AND the infrastructure for this *does* exist, so long as the chunks are the right size and format. \r\n\r\nI bet that\'s where it\'ll go.','Internet and Broadband Video are overrated but unrealized',0,'','publish','open','open','','internet-and-broadband-video-are-overrated-but-unrealized','','','2006-07-16 09:24:10','2006-07-16 16:24:10','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/16/internet-and-broadband-video-are-overrated-but-unrealized/',0,'post','',0),
	(747,1,'2006-07-15 21:13:54','2006-07-16 04:13:54','i\'m watching this play at meydenbauer center in bellevue it\'s quite good (yay one line phone posts)','i hate hamlet',0,'','publish','open','open','','i-hate-hamlet','','','2006-07-16 03:31:47','2006-07-16 10:31:47','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/15/i-hate-hamlet/',0,'post','',2),
	(749,1,'2006-07-16 11:02:34','2006-07-16 18:02:34','<a href=\"http://avc.blogs.com/a_vc/2006/07/how_to_be_a_fil.html\">There\'s a girl</a> who\'s doing big things by releasing her videos on YouTube. <br /><br /><br />This is a damn good idea, but it won\'t work for everyone. By definition<br />you only hear about the success stories (in terms of The Long Tail:<br />hits), so it\'s a skewed picture.<br /><br />This kind of thing always makes me conflicted, but I think it\'s because deep down I must be a horrible person. I\'m happy that it\'s possible and I\'m happy that people are doing cool things like this, but at the same time I always get upset because it\'s not me doing them. <br /><br />I also get frustrated that nobody thinks what I\'m doing is as cool as these things ;)<br /><br />on a side note:<br /><br />Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off is an amazing movie for Sunday Morning. Also, what the hell happened to Mia Sara? <br />','On doing cool things',0,'','publish','open','open','','on-doing-cool-things','','','2006-07-16 11:16:45','2006-07-16 18:16:45','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/16/on-doing-cool-things/',0,'post','',0),
	(750,1,'2006-07-17 12:15:01','2006-07-17 19:15:01','It\'s been my plan for some time to try and run a contest on Flicker to drive some traffic and help put our site \"out there.\" \r\n\r\nPart of the problem with arranging a contest though is that you have to strike a balance between making it simple enough that people will do it (and having a good enough prize), and still retaining value from it yourself. \r\n\r\nBut I\'ve been second-guessing that strategy since people are basically lazy and un-motivated. My friends at Destructoid are <a href=\"http://www.destructoid.com/giveaway.php\">running a contest</a> this month for a Nintnendo DS - to enter, (as far as I can tell) you guess a number between 0-200, and register at their site. \r\n\r\nSounds like a low barrier, right? Especially for something as juicy-sweet as a Nintendo DS.\r\n\r\nWell, I\'ve told a few of my friends who have <em>considered buying a DS from a store</em> about the contest, and none of them have shown any interest in participating. Which doesn\'t bode well for contests. ','Drive traffic with a contest',0,'','publish','open','open','','drive-traffic-with-a-contest','','','2006-07-17 12:15:01','2006-07-17 19:15:01','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/17/drive-traffic-with-a-contest/',0,'post','',2),
	(751,1,'2006-07-17 19:30:41','2006-07-18 02:30:41','I\'m reading and enjoying <a href=\"http://www.thelongtail.com\">The Long Tail</a>, and so far (I\'m about halfway through) it\'s an excellent read. I\'ll post more complete thoughts on it when I\'m finished, although there\'s a lot of digesting to be done. \r\n\r\nI ran across a quote, however, that so aptly describes my attitude I can\'t help but post it here:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>It\'s one thing to see a movie or listen to music and to think \"genius\"--that some gifted person and exalted appartus has put together this unique work of art we appreciate. However, once you know what\'s behind the curtain, you begin to realize that it could be <em>you</em>. It is when the tools of production are transparent that we are inspired to create. When people understand how great work is made, they\'re more likely to want to do it themselves. </blockquote>','Paint a picture of me',0,'','publish','open','open','','paint-a-picture-of-me','','','2006-07-17 19:30:41','2006-07-18 02:30:41','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/17/paint-a-picture-of-me/',0,'post','',0),
	(752,1,'2006-07-18 13:37:54','2006-07-18 20:37:54','In today\'s day and age, it is absolutely essential for a company offering a service or a product to have a positive online experience; <a href=\"http://www.marketingshift.com/2006/07/shoppers-search-before-buying.cfm\">a whopping 87% of consumers</a> research products online before buying, even in stores. \r\n\r\nSo it amazes me that the Adelphia web site is such utter incomprehensible garbage. \r\n\r\nThe fat cable pigs are so happy with their little monopoly that they don\'t bother with customers any more. I\'ve been looking at options for piping channels into my apartment down in LA for a relatively small amount of ca$h (seeing as how I\'ve basically no income), but it doesn\'t look like it\'s going to happen. \r\n\r\nAdelphia doesn\'t list prices anywhere I can find on their web site (<em>excuse me?</em>) - they want you to fill in all your information and then someone from their end will call you - then, and only after they have all your contact/spam/junk mail information will they tell you how much their services cost. \r\n\r\nDirectTV has a much nicer site but their basic plan appears to be $40 per month, which is too much. \r\n\r\nI think I might just get an antenna. It\'s all crap anyways because I would only buy Cable for three channels out of the 100zillion they charge me for. ','Good God Adelphia sucks balls',0,'','publish','open','open','','good-god-adelphia-sucks-balls','','','2006-07-18 13:37:54','2006-07-18 20:37:54','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/18/good-god-adelphia-sucks-balls/',0,'post','',1),
	(753,1,'2006-07-18 14:00:33','2006-07-18 21:00:33','Design-wise. Hopefully it won\'t look too terribly different when I\'m done, but I wanted to upgrade the site to a theme compatible with widgets.\r\n\r\nI\'m starting with <a href=\"http://www.obharath.net/blog/2006/06/15/widgetized-3-column-k2/\">Bharath Kumar\'s 3-column K2</a>, and making it my own. \r\n\r\n[ Edit: got frustrated and changed my mind completely. Back to square minus one. ]\r\n\r\n[ Edit 2: turns out my old theme woulnd\'t let people comment in IE6 (seriously folks, if you\'re going to keep using IE, at least upgrade to beta 7, which is MUCH better). It should work now anyways. ]','Back to square one (again)',0,'','publish','open','open','','back-to-square-one-again','','','2006-07-18 16:18:44','2006-07-18 23:18:44','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/18/back-to-square-one-again/',0,'post','',0),
	(754,1,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','0000-00-00 00:00:00','','Sharp XR-10X Projector Review',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','0000-00-00 00:00:00','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=754',0,'post','',0),
	(755,1,'2006-07-19 14:38:27','2006-07-19 21:38:27','When you lock your archives behind sixteen different walls then all the work you put into them (that is, the sum total of journalism for everything but the past two weeks) is completely unsearchable, unusable, and therefore completely worthless. \r\n\r\nYou <em>have to keep the content out there</em> or it loses any value it once had. Get that through your skulls. ','Message to the media',0,'','publish','open','open','','message-to-the-media','','','2006-07-19 14:38:27','2006-07-19 21:38:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/19/message-to-the-media/',0,'post','',0),
	(756,1,'2006-07-19 17:57:22','2006-07-20 00:57:22','If you scroll halfway down the page, you\'ll notice something in my sidebar that is practically ubiquitous, and so so so important to the internet economy right now: a google ad bar. \r\n\r\nOn my blog here, it\'s kind of like a tip jar - if you like what I do, click an interesting ad sometime, you know...just because. I\'m not going to shove things down your throat. \r\n\r\nBut what\'s amazing about that little bar is that it lets practically anyone serve ads on their site. It\'s what makes little publishing nightmares like <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net\">Flicker Gaming</a> even possible in the first place - there\'s no way we could launch with any expectation of revenue otherwise. \r\n\r\nBut all ad networks are not created equal. We launched sporting the shiny Google ads and the \"network links\" from Adbrite, a cool service that lets people buy links on your site at a price you set, instead of a price that google sets. I think it\'s a pretty neat idea, but it only works if people want to be buying ads. \r\n\r\nIn the meantime, we filled in with network ads that have, since launch, consistently advertised cell phone ring tones and \"how to cheat at online poker.\" Nobody\'s clicking, and I don\'t blame them. Today I added a different ad service to our mix, an experimental \"media exchange\" from <a href=\"http://www.rightmedia.com/\">Right Media</a>. \r\n\r\nLet\'s hope their ads target better than AdBrite. ','Trying new stuffs',0,'','publish','open','open','','trying-new-stuffs','','','2006-07-19 17:57:22','2006-07-20 00:57:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/19/trying-new-stuffs/',0,'post','',1),
	(758,1,'2006-07-20 15:11:36','2006-07-20 22:11:36','In The Long Tail, Chris Anderson divides most of what we consume into \"hits\" and, for lack of a better term, \"misses,\" and spends a long time explaining how thoroughly our culture is set into the \"hit\" mentality. \r\n\r\nEverything has, since capitalism began, been tied inexorably to the physical world and therefore to the economic limits that go with it (in other words, the \"long tail\" gets chopped off in favor of the hits). \r\n\r\nWhat\'s fascinating is how digital goods (via the internet) have all but eliminated the restrictions held on goods in the physical world, so that the \"virtual\" inventory of a retailer can extend into infinity, covering the entirety of demand instead of merely the hits. \r\n\r\nThat\'s the light version. Read the book for the full one. \r\n\r\nBut the basic idea that comes across is that, <em>for some businessess</em> the era of hits is over. Hits are selling less in movies, music, books (ironically, I think The Long Tail is selling extremely well), and office chairs.\r\n\r\nIn the book, Anderson relays the success story of the Lonely Island crew (from hollywood failure to internet video to SNL Chronicles of Narnia rap) as an example of how the long tail of media production (millions of people producing content that they think is cool) is actually a powerful force. \r\n\r\nThis is really a fascinating concept - almost like a million monkeys writing shakespeare, except that we can see it at work. The problem is that while it\'s an amazing social phenomenon, I don\'t see how it works to kill the hit in media (or journalism - or basically any field where people are paid for their time). \r\n\r\nTo play fast and loose with Marx: where is the money? In a capitalistic society, understanding anything starts with following the transactions. \r\n\r\nThe Long Tail works by reducing the cost of offering something to practically (or literally) zero. For retailers, this is an obvious thing - if you can offer each person in the world a chance to buy a product, and it costs you absolutely nothing to do so, then if it sells <em>just once, ever</em>, then it\'s worth carrying. \r\n\r\nFor something like a movie or a comedy sketch or a newspaper, even if it costs next to nothing to offer it to someone (floating around a P2P network on the internet), it <em>still costs money to make</em>. The one commodity that is not dropping to zero with new technology is talent. I hope it never will. \r\n\r\nThe Lonely Island Crew is a success story because they went from the fringe to the tip, from a miss to a hit, from a freebie to something that could be monetized. \r\n\r\nFrom a business standpoint, the long tail is useless unless it can pay itself back. In the long tail, producing a short film is never going to pay for itself. Producing a shitload of long tail shot films is just going to not pay for itself on a larger scale. \r\n\r\nOnly when a short film crosses over into the territory of the hit will it begin to pay for itself. So it looks like we\'re still trapped in the same system we started with (as far as business is concerned) until we can make the long tail pay for the time people put into it. \r\n','The premature death of hits',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-premature-death-of-hits','','\nhttp://www.longtail.com/the_long_tail/2006/07/guy_kawasaki_an.html#trackback','2006-07-20 17:18:13','2006-07-21 00:18:13','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/20/the-premature-death-of-hits/',0,'post','',0),
	(759,1,'2006-07-20 15:51:02','2006-07-20 22:51:02','No idea how long it\'ll be up (it says public domain?) but a full hour of one of my favorite comedians can be found on Google Video:\r\n\r\n<center><embed style=\"width:400px; height:326px;\" id=\"VideoPlayback\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" src=\"http://video.google.com/googleplayer.swf?docId=-3337098556772269799\"> </embed></center>\r\n        ','George Carlin On Campus 1984',0,'','publish','open','open','','george-carlin-on-campus-1984','','','2006-07-20 15:51:02','2006-07-20 22:51:02','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/20/george-carlin-on-campus-1984/',0,'post','',0),
	(760,1,'2006-07-20 17:16:53','2006-07-21 00:16:53','I guess the main point of <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/20/the-premature-death-of-hits/\">my earlier thoughts</a> is that in everything except retail, The Long Tail is a great way to outsource the development of hits, and not much more. ','Outsource the hit',0,'','publish','open','open','','outsource-the-hit','','','2006-07-20 17:16:53','2006-07-21 00:16:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/20/outsource-the-hit/',0,'post','',0),
	(761,1,'2006-07-21 16:34:54','2006-07-21 23:34:54','Our house phone line has been out for three days now, and if it isn\'t fixed today it probably won\'t be fixed unil monday. \r\n\r\nTen years ago this would have been a pretty unacceptable outtage. I mean, without a phone there used to be very options for getting hold of people. \r\n\r\nNow we all have cell phones, and who knows how long the line was out before we noticed ;)','The new age of cell phones',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-new-age-of-cell-phones','','','2006-07-21 16:34:54','2006-07-21 23:34:54','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/21/the-new-age-of-cell-phones/',0,'post','',0),
	(762,1,'2006-07-22 13:31:58','2006-07-22 20:31:58','In case you didn\'t hear about the <a href=\"http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2003144181_kirkfire22e.html\">story in Kirkland</a>, it turns out the massive house fire was arson, the fire set by a neighbor who claims he woke up in pools of blood next to the deceased. \r\n\r\nApparently, he showered himself off then torched the house hoping to hide the evidence. \r\n\r\nWhat\'s really interesting is that the Judge wouldn\'t let the Seattle Times photograph the suspect\'s face, so the Times grabbed his headshot from MySpace. \r\n\r\nHmmm. ','Deaths in kirkland',0,'','publish','open','open','','deaths-in-kirkland','','','2006-07-22 13:31:58','2006-07-22 20:31:58','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/22/deaths-in-kirkland/',0,'post','',0),
	(763,1,'2006-07-23 16:23:05','2006-07-23 23:23:05','I can\'t usually tell people what I do at work. This is largely because I don\'t think that what I do is very easy to classify; half the time <em>I\'m</em> not even sure what I\'m doing. \r\n\r\nBetween what I do to pay the bills (<a href=\"http://www.blogbusinesssummit.com\">Blog Business Summit</a>) and what I do for fun to try to pay the bills (<a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net\">Flicker Gaming</a>), I end up spending a lot of time doing things that I never thought I\'d know how to do in the first place. \r\n\r\nWhen I first bought the Flicker Gaming domain, I had no idea how to install a <acronym title=\"Content Management System\">CMS</acronym> of any kind, not even a blog. I hired a friend to install e107 and tweak it so I liked how it looked. It worked OK, but I\'m a control freak and I like to keep tweaking things, and that wasn\'t really going to work for both of us, so I set out to teach myself Wordpress. \r\n\r\nI think I\'ve done a decent job. Decent enough that I get paid at work to develop sites in Wordpress, and that\'s pretty impressive. \r\n\r\nBut as I keep going I find that the things I end up doing are getting ever more technical. I look at sites like <a href=\"http://www.mikeindustries.com/blog/\">Mike Davidson\'s blog</a> and I realize that there\'s a certain level of complexity I don\'t think I ever really want to understand. \r\n\r\nI\'m far more interested with the superficial tweaks, and frankly, I\'d rather come up with an image file and have someone else do all the crazy coding that needs to happen behind it. \r\n\r\nAt the same time, every step of the way I see things that look hopelessly complex, and then three months later I\'m actually trying to <em>do</em> them. I never wanted to be a crazy techie webmaster, but I keep finding myself further down that road. \r\n\r\nI just hope I keep doing other stuff as well. Eventually, I\'d like to hand off the webmaster work at Flicker to someone who really knows what they\'re doing, and move to blogging and, for lack of a better term, managing things. But that\'s gotta wait until we have revenue. ','Some things I don\'t want to learn',0,'','publish','open','open','','some-things-i-dont-want-to-learn','','','2006-07-23 16:23:05','2006-07-23 23:23:05','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/23/some-things-i-dont-want-to-learn/',0,'post','',0),
	(764,1,'2006-07-23 18:09:04','2006-07-24 01:09:04','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image765\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/intellitext.png\" alt=\"Intellitext\" />Does anyone else think that this IntelliTXT style advertising that keeps showing up (on a lot of gaming sites, incidentally) is extremely annoying? The last thing I want to see while I\'m scrolling through an article is for an advertisement bubble to pop up over what I\'m reading. \r\n\r\nOne of the keys to successful advertising is <em>not being annoying</em>.\r\nThis is true for the product being advertised, and it\'s true for the site the advertising is on. I\'m going to steal a quote I <a href=\"http://www.veen.com/jeff/archives/000901.html\">saw recently</a> to underline this:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>To predict the behavior of ordinary people in advance, you only have to assume that they will always try to escape a disagreeable situation with the smallest possible expenditure of intelligence.\r\n\r\n<p><em> - <a href=\"http://history.enotes.com/famous-quotes/to-predict-the-behavior-of-ordinary-people-in\">Friedrich Nietzsche</a></em></blockquote>\r\n\r\nIf your site is annoying because of your ads, I\'m going to visit it less often (unfortunately true for the Dilbert Blog, one of my favorites). I\'m simply trying to escape a disagreeable situation. \r\n\r\nps. I\'m downloading a file at 780 KB/sec! I remember when that was impossible, so this still impresses me. \r\n\r\npps. I need to find a good desktop blog client - that means a desktop app for composition that allows me to save and retrive drafts from my server. This is the second time today my browser has randomly deleted a half-finished post. ','IntelliTXT is annoying',0,'','publish','open','open','','intellitxt-is-annoying','','','2006-07-23 18:09:04','2006-07-24 01:09:04','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/23/intellitxt-is-annoying/',0,'post','',2),
	(765,1,'2006-07-23 18:08:48','2006-07-24 01:08:48','','Intellitext',0,'','inherit','open','open','','intellitext','','','2006-07-23 18:08:48','2006-07-24 01:08:48','',764,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/intellitext.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(766,1,'2006-07-23 20:00:12','2006-07-24 03:00:12','It\'s funny that Seth Godin is posting a few throwback posts while I\'m reading The Long Tail. In a thought apparently from 2004, <a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2006/07/factor_l.html\">Seth said</a>:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Every time you launch a product or service, every time you apply for a job or start a nonprofit, you\'re either going to hit or not. If you get lucky, you\'re entitled to deny that luck had anything to do with it.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nSo I guess now we should say you\'re either going to hit or you\'re going to be in the Long Tail. Although I\'m still not convinced we\'ve figured out how to produce for the Long Tail yet. ','Hit or Miss',0,'','publish','open','open','','hit-or-miss','','\nhttp://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2006/07/factor_l.html#trackback\nhttp://www.typepad.com/t/trackback/5490759','2006-07-24 13:21:27','2006-07-24 20:21:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/23/hit-or-miss/',0,'post','',0),
	(767,1,'2006-07-24 15:20:13','2006-07-24 22:20:13','When I hit the randomize button, I want it to randomize the order of play, not screw up the order in which I\'ve listed my songs. ','Dear iTunes',0,'','publish','open','open','','dear-itunes','','','2006-07-24 15:20:13','2006-07-24 22:20:13','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/24/dear-itunes/',0,'post','',1),
	(768,1,'0000-00-00 00:00:00','0000-00-00 00:00:00','','New metrics, old metrics, blogs and hybrid media',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','0000-00-00 00:00:00','0000-00-00 00:00:00','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=768',0,'post','',0),
	(769,1,'2006-07-24 15:55:21','2006-07-24 22:55:21','Between my desktop and my laptop, I use about four or five different browsers every day, on at least two different computers. \r\n\r\nGenerally speaking, Google Broswer Sync is godlike, and keeps all my passwords and login info synchronized between my computers, and has really narrowed my browser use down to Firefox and IE (for the rare occasion that something isn\'t compatible). \r\n\r\nBut I am never logged in to Google or Yahoo!. \r\n\r\nIt used to be that whenever I opened up my browser I was logged in to Yahoo! (when it was my homepage) or Google (it is still my homepage, but it\'s worthless because I\'m never logged in). \r\n\r\nI <em>want</em> Google to remember me, so that I don\'t have to log in just to check my customized home page. It\'s worthless as a home page if it\'s, well, not really my home page. \r\n\r\nSo we need a new system where Google can remember what browsers and what computers I\'m logged in on, and then make it so I don\'t go to their annoyingly generic splash page everytime my browser loads. ','We need a new cookie system',0,'','publish','open','open','','we-need-a-new-cookie-system','','','2006-07-24 15:55:21','2006-07-24 22:55:21','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/24/we-need-a-new-cookie-system/',0,'post','',0),
	(770,1,'2006-07-24 21:15:32','2006-07-25 04:15:32','It seems that no matter what I do, <a href=\"http://www.bluehost.com\">BlueHost</a> keeps dropping Flicker down. Now that we\'ve moved server boxes, our site is down and the other sites I know on BH are up. Guh. \r\n\r\nAs soon as our prepaid year\'s hosting is up we\'re moving to <a href=\"http://www.dreamhost.com\">dreamhost</a>. ','no hope for bluehost',0,'','publish','open','open','','no-hope-for-bluehost','','','2006-07-24 21:17:30','2006-07-25 04:17:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/24/no-hope-for-bluehost/',0,'post','',1),
	(771,1,'2006-07-25 11:41:26','2006-07-25 18:41:26','I wish I had the readership and resources to outsource my business problems to the blogosphere like Mark Cuban does. Is this another example of pulling the long tail <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/20/the-premature-death-of-hits/\">into the hit structure</a>?\r\n\r\nI think the best idea is <a href=\"http://www.blogmaverick.com/entry/1234000960073808/#c708096\">this one</a>. \r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.blogmaverick.com/entry/1234000960073808/#c708143\">This</a> is another almost great idea. I think it needs to be \"before release.\"\r\n\r\nLastly, I like the ideas in <a href=\"http://www.blogmaverick.com/entry/1234000960073808/#c708239\">this comment</a>. \r\n\r\nIt\'ll be interesting to see what happens. ','Outsourcing creativity (to the long tail?)',0,'','publish','open','open','','771','','','2006-07-25 11:46:53','2006-07-25 18:46:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/25/771/',0,'post','',0),
	(773,1,'2006-07-27 13:59:17','2006-07-27 20:59:17','I\'m glad some people are contesting ridiculous lawsuits (or rather, going to court instead of settling):\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://wired.com/wired/archive/14.08/start.html?pg=3\">From Wired</a>:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Last November, Shawn Hogan received an unsettling call: A lawyer representing Universal Pictures and the Motion Picture Association of America informed the 30-year-old software developer that they were suing him for downloading Meet the Fockers over BitTorrent. Hogan was baffled. Not only does he deny the accusation, he says he already owned the film on DVD. The attorney said they would settle for $2,500. Hogan declined.</blockquote>\r\n\r\n','Hooray Shawn Hogan',0,'','publish','open','open','','hooray-shawn-hogan','','','2006-07-27 13:59:17','2006-07-27 20:59:17','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/27/hooray-shawn-hogan/',0,'post','',0),
	(772,1,'2006-07-26 14:09:27','2006-07-26 21:09:27','If anyone knows the magic secret to getting bunches of people to realize that your site is up online and is cool, now would be the time to let me in ;)\r\n\r\nI\'ve gone over the data a zillion times and I\'ve made countless numbers of minor tweaks (remove a link here, add one there, change \"blog\" to \"home,\" make a \"related posts\" box, change the height of page elements) to hopefully make the site friendlier to folks of all stripes, but it seems like the big problem is just having people think of Flicker when they think of the web sites they visit.\r\n\r\nExposure takes money. Pure and simple. We\'re back to <a title=\"The Megaphone Problem\" href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/05/04/how-big-is-your-megaphone/\">the megaphone problem</a>, and unfortunately my megaphone is still quite small.','Magic traffic secrets',0,'','publish','open','open','','magic-traffic-secrets','','','2006-07-26 14:09:27','2006-07-26 21:09:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/26/magic-traffic-secrets/',0,'post','',0),
	(774,1,'2006-07-27 18:35:07','2006-07-28 01:35:07','Part of what I do during all my sleepless nights (hey, I\'m young) is try to think logically about how people browse about the internet (hey, I\'m a geek). <br /><br />To this end, I spend a lot of time thinking about what my browsing habits are, what I see other people doing online, and staring at boring-ass statistics like pages most frequently visited and yaddda yadda. Incidentally, the most popular thing I\'ve ever written is a <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net/index.php/2006/05/15/how-to-run-tie-fighter-collectors-cd-edition-in-windows-xp/\">guide</a> to making the classic video game TIE Fighter work on modern Windows XP machines. <br /><br />When you break it down, people are usually online for only a couple of reasons. If you toss out shopping (because that doesn\'t apply to me), then people mostly look at sites for three different types of content, and my guess is that it\'s in this order:<br /><ul><li>Reference (how do I...)</li><li>News (what happened today in rhode island...)<br /></li><li>Editorial (what does Friedman think about what happened today in rhode island...)</li></ul>So if you\'re looking to grab traffic, you should probably focus heavily on those three types of content - in that order. <br /><br />This post is basically a cheap trick to fit into the first category (how do I get more site traffic). What\'s annoying about posts like this is that while they <i>do</i> offer good advice, the irony is often too much for me to bear. <br /><br />Regardless, this is a strategy I\'m going to start working on at Flicker, and we\'ll see how well it does. Today we have two new editorial pieces up - I wrote about <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net/index.php/2006/07/27/debunking-the-ps3-ipod-comparison/\">debunking the PS3 - iPod comparison</a>, and our excellent blogger Corvus shared <a href=\"http://www.flickergaming.net/index.php/2006/07/27/ads-please/\">his thoughts on in-game advertising</a>. <br /><br />Now we need to work on having more how-to content. <br />','Tips on how to grow traffic with the right content',0,'','publish','open','open','','tips-on-how-to-grow-traffic-with-the-right-content','','','2006-07-27 18:55:45','2006-07-28 01:55:45','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/27/tips-on-how-to-grow-traffic-with-the-right-content/',0,'post','',0),
	(775,1,'2006-07-27 19:23:28','2006-07-28 02:23:28','I just realized that in my little bookmark folder of blogs that I visit basically every day, I\'ve been skipping basically all the typepad blogs because they have the same favicon. <br /><br />My eye gravitates to unique icons, for some reason. Anyways, apparently it\'s a good idea to have your own icon. <br />','The importance of favicons',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-importance-of-favicons','','','2006-07-27 19:23:30','2006-07-28 02:23:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/27/the-importance-of-favicons/',0,'post','',0),
	(776,1,'2006-07-29 15:10:57','2006-07-29 22:10:57','There is such a thing as free marketing online. <a href=\"http://www.passthepress.com/?p=635\">Some people think</a> Viral Marketing is past it\'s prime. It probably is, so long as it\'s marketing. \r\n\r\nWhat blogs have done is allowed content to market itself. We\'ve moved from Word of Mouth to Word of Blog. \r\n\r\nUnfortunately WOB marketing isn\'t really marketing - you just have to make good stuff and hope people care enough to share it. \r\n','WOB marketing',0,'','publish','open','open','','wob-marketing','','','2006-07-29 15:10:57','2006-07-29 22:10:57','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/29/wob-marketing/',0,'post','',0),
	(777,1,'2006-07-29 17:03:22','2006-07-30 00:03:22','Recently I\'ve been getting a bunch of incoming links listed from <a href=\"http://www.technorati.com\">Technorati</a> that don\'t seem to exist. I regularly check out blogs that link in (either here or at Flicker) to see what people are saying, but recently \r\n\r\nI\'ve been shuttled to posts that are not only completely irrelevant but also absolutely devoid of linkage. What gives, Technorati?','Phantom linkage from Technorati',0,'','publish','open','open','','phantom-linkage-from-technorati','','','2006-07-29 17:03:22','2006-07-30 00:03:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/29/phantom-linkage-from-technorati/',0,'post','',0),
	(778,1,'2006-07-30 13:52:44','2006-07-30 20:52:44','Reading the comics section of the paper this morning, I realized why Dilbert is such an amazingly popular comic: it\'s the only one that\'s funny. \r\n\r\nSo it seems the secret to succcess is to be mildly entertaining in a world of mediocrity. Hurrah!','I\'ve discovered the secret to Dilbert\'s success',0,'','publish','open','open','','ive-discovered-the-secret-to-dilberts-success','','','2006-07-30 13:52:44','2006-07-30 20:52:44','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/30/ive-discovered-the-secret-to-dilberts-success/',0,'post','',2),
	(779,1,'2006-07-31 11:38:45','2006-07-31 18:38:45','','newsvine wall',0,'','inherit','open','open','','newsvine-wall','','','2006-07-31 11:38:45','2006-07-31 18:38:45','',780,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/newsvine_wall.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(780,1,'2006-07-31 11:39:03','2006-07-31 18:39:03','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image779\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/07/newsvine_wall.jpg\" alt=\"newsvine wall\" />I\'m a perfect example of why blogs actually do work. This summer, simply because of people\'s blogs, I have:\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Bought a book</li>\r\n	<li>Subscribed to a magazine, and</li>\r\n	<li>Signed up for three web services</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nThe latest of these is <a href=\"http://www.newsvine.com\">Newsvine</a>. After running across Mike Davidson\'s blog (CEO Newsvine), I wound up poking around the Newsvine site a little bit. I never really took the time to figure out how it worked before. \r\n\r\nSo far it\'s pretty neat, and it lets me find things like this:\r\n\r\n<blockquote><a href=\"http://www.newsvine.com/_news/2006/07/29/304406-man-accused-of-biting-off-roosters-head\">Man Accused of Biting Off Rooster\'s Head</a>\r\n\r\nNEW YORK â€” A man accused of biting the head off his pet rooster was arrested Friday and faces up to a year in prison if convicted, an animal protection spokesman said.\r\n\r\nA neighbor had complained about a dead rooster near his Manhattan apartment and agents found the body of the beheaded rooster on a fire escape, said Joe Pentangelo, spokesman for the American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals. The rooster\'s head was not located.</blockquote>','Discovering Newsvine',0,'','publish','open','open','','discovering-newsvine','','','2006-07-31 11:39:03','2006-07-31 18:39:03','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/31/discovering-newsvine/',0,'post','',0),
	(781,1,'2006-08-01 13:30:21','2006-08-01 20:30:21','The whole time I\'ve been reading through <a href=\"http://www.thelongtail.com\">The Long Tail</a>, I\'ve been trying to grasp how it applies to content <em>production</em> (or more specifically, my blogs). \r\n\r\nThere\'s the obvious route of having large numbers of niche blogs (Weblogs Inc), but while for some reason that system has worked (probably because the hits support the tail), it\'s pretty hard to figure a way that each niche blog couldsupport itself all the way down the tail. \r\n\r\nLast night, around 2:30am, I figured it out: the long tail forms with <em>blog archives</em>. Once produced (and blogs, unlike most mainstream media, have really accessible archives), these archives cost literally nothing to keep around (reduce cost to zero), and their subject matter often subdivides into whatever extent of the long tail you\'d like. \r\n\r\nI\'ve got no numbers to graph, but my guess is that if you listed every blog post from my blogs and laid them out in terms of how many visits each permalink gets, you\'d end up with a rough equivalent of the long tail. \r\n\r\nHuzzah!\r\n\r\nAnd as an added bonus, you get to enjoy this YouTube video now:\r\n\r\n<center><object width=\"425\" height=\"350\"><param name=\"movie\" value=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/7xAA71Ssids\"></param><embed src=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/7xAA71Ssids\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"425\" height=\"350\"></embed></object></center>','I get it! (the long tail)',0,'','publish','open','open','','i-get-it-the-long-tail','','','2008-07-30 10:40:30','2008-07-30 17:40:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/01/i-get-it-the-long-tail/',0,'post','',0),
	(782,1,'2006-08-01 13:58:22','2006-08-01 20:58:22','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image783\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/feed-icon-128x128.png\" alt=\"feed icon grayscale\" />Has anyone else noticed that with it\'s latest revision, technorati has become a feedreader as well as a blog search engine?\r\n\r\nThey encourage you to \"favorite\" blogs, and then they track the latest posts from your favorite blogs and post them on the front page. You can click through to the article\'s text within the Technorati frame.\r\n\r\nEveryone\'s gone all crazy about RSS. Yes, it\'s a great technology, but <em>it should be invisible</em>. \r\n\r\nMore and more I find myself using bookmarks for everything but homepage widgets. I actually <em>like</em> going to individual sites - they all have a distinct footprint that is part of what makes the blog unique and individual (like Fred Wilson\'s <a href=\"http://avc.blogs.com/a_vc/2006/07/new_blog_bling.html\">blog bling</a> - you can hate the load times, but that\'s part of who he is!).\r\n\r\nSooner or later people are going to figure out that they still like web sites. I hate reading a bunch of text in a little dialog-box rss reader, and I love checking out new sites. RSS is good for notification and bookmarking, not for reading. Figure it out guys. ','Technorati becomes a feedreader, I want my web sites back',0,'','publish','open','open','','technorati-becomes-a-feedreader-i-want-my-web-sites-back','','','2006-08-01 13:58:22','2006-08-01 20:58:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/01/technorati-becomes-a-feedreader-i-want-my-web-sites-back/',0,'post','',0),
	(783,1,'2006-08-01 13:58:05','2006-08-01 20:58:05','','feed icon grayscale',0,'','inherit','open','open','','feed-icon-grayscale','','','2006-08-01 13:58:05','2006-08-01 20:58:05','',782,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/feed-icon-128x128.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(784,1,'2006-08-01 14:52:33','2006-08-01 21:52:33','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image785\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/cd.jpg\" alt=\"cd\" />I just installed a new lightscribe DVD burner in the computer I\'m building to be a DVR for my projector. It\'s a sweet new drive that\'s supposed to be able to burn dual-layer DVDs (either format) and laser-print a label onto the top of the DVD.\r\n\r\nIt can also print the top of CDs. \r\n\r\nSo my question is: from a consumer standpoint, what is the major difference between a disc printed in my drive and a disc printed professionally?\r\n\r\nFor DVDs it\'s copy protection. For CDs...I can\'t think of anything. \r\n\r\nSo why can\'t my digital music shopping at Amazon work like this:\r\n\r\nI go to Amazon and find the CD I\'d like to buy. Amazon offers me a \"disc image\" option, which then allows me to download a pre-packaged file which will take the blank CD I put into my drive and burn/label it identically to a CD I could find in a store. \r\n\r\nThen I get to download and store a pdf (or some format) copy of the CD\'s accompanying leaflet thing. \r\n\r\nDone. I\'ve got my CD. I\'ve got my track list. No shipping. \r\n\r\nLet\'s do it. ','Buying digital',0,'','publish','open','open','','buying-digital','','','2006-08-01 14:56:22','2006-08-01 21:56:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/01/buying-digital/',0,'post','',1),
	(785,1,'2006-08-01 14:56:09','2006-08-01 21:56:09','','cd',0,'','inherit','open','open','','cd','','','2006-08-01 14:56:09','2006-08-01 21:56:09','',784,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/cd.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(787,1,'2006-08-02 12:57:42','2006-08-02 19:57:42','<img align=\"left\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image788\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/books.jpg\" alt=\"books\" />Seth Godin posted a <a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2006/08/advice_for_auth.html\">list of helpful tidbits</a> for wannabe authors like myself, and it\'s a pretty awesome little list. \r\n\r\nSince publishing a short story or a book is one of the things on <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/03/28/my-101-list/\">my 101 list</a>, it\'s something that I\'m usually either thinking about doing or working towards doing. \r\n\r\nSince starting Flicker Gaming, I\'ve literally learned at least three new things each day about blogs, blogging as a business, and running a startup. The best part is that I find most of what I learn to be absolutely fascinating. \r\n\r\nI\'ve been working for a while on writing them up into blog-length pieces of advice, ranging from tips on redirecting traffic to your site (things like <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/07/27/tips-on-how-to-grow-traffic-with-the-right-content/\">growing traffic with the right content</a>) to things you might or might now know about running a small business. This is the first time around for me, and I haven\'t been to business school, so some of it is going to be fairly obvious and some of it is going to be more obscure (this is an obscure field of business, after all). \r\n\r\nRegardless, I think it\'s the type of thing that would be pretty helpful to anyone else who wants to start a blog and make money off of it. \r\n\r\nI plan to post a good twenty peices or so online - maybe here, maybe on another blog - and make a larger grand total available in book format. We\'ll see how I do at finishing what I\'ve started this time around (I have a bad record with that). \r\n\r\nI hope I get it done. I <em>want</em> to write a book.','Writing a book (or two)',0,'','publish','open','open','','writing-a-book-or-two','','\nhttp://www.typepad.com/t/trackback/5567455','2006-08-02 12:57:42','2006-08-02 19:57:42','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/02/writing-a-book-or-two/',0,'post','',0),
	(786,1,'2006-08-02 03:45:57','2006-08-02 10:45:57','There\'s no time like 4am to do a site redesign. \r\n\r\nI finally convinced myself to draw up what I wanted in Photoshop and *then* go tinkering in the CSS. Doing it this way is <em>so</em> much nicer. \r\n\r\nI changed the site to look like this in literally 10 minutes. I\'ll spend another ten tomorrow changing the color of the header text to black and picking colors for the menu highlights, and then...done. \r\n\r\nHooray I have a site again. ','90 percent live',0,'','publish','open','open','','90-percent-live','','','2006-08-02 03:45:57','2006-08-02 10:45:57','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/02/90-percent-live/',0,'post','',0),
	(788,1,'2006-08-02 12:57:17','2006-08-02 19:57:17','','books',0,'','inherit','open','open','','books','','','2006-08-02 12:57:17','2006-08-02 19:57:17','',787,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/books.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(789,1,'2006-08-02 16:08:19','2006-08-02 23:08:19','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" id=\"image790\" src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/digglogo.jpg\" alt=\"digg lego logo\" />I never commented <a href=\"http://www.calacanis.com/2006/07/18/everyones-gotta-eat-or-1-000-a-month-for-doing-what-youre/\">on this</a> because I thought it would die out pretty quickly, but the blogosphere is all about beating a dead horse, so I\'m gonna drag the carcass in here for two quick cents:\r\n<ol>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.netscape.com\">New Netscape</a> has always been centered around mixing editorial content with community content. How can paying people to, like an editor, select stories for \"print\" <em>possibly</em> be an unexpected or unplanned or even unreasonable extension of the concept?</li>\r\n<li>If paying someone to do more of something they do well invalidates that work, then the whole basis for our capitalistic society falls apart. Why are we paying athletes? So Jason spotted people doing something that, if doing it paid the bills, they could do <em>even better</em>? Gee, what a horrible idea it is to pay them.</li></ol>\r\n\r\nI\'m doing some more cool stuff with regards to Netscape and Digg right now. I\'ll post on it in a few days when I\'ve got my thoughts together. ','Two cents on paying social bookmarkers',0,'','publish','open','open','','two-cents-on-paying-social-bookmarkers','','','2006-08-02 16:08:19','2006-08-02 23:08:19','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/02/two-cents-on-paying-social-bookmarkers/',0,'post','',0),
	(790,1,'2006-08-02 16:07:14','2006-08-02 23:07:14','','digg lego logo',0,'','inherit','open','open','','digg-lego-logo','','','2006-08-02 16:07:14','2006-08-02 23:07:14','',789,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2006/08/digglogo.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(791,1,'2006-08-03 15:27:49','2006-08-03 22:27:49','I just spotted another OK GO video on YouTube. For those of you who missed out on A Million Ways, you can find it <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6dJEa44Wgtk&search=million%20ways\">here</a>. I think it\'s maybe a little better than the new one.\r\n\r\n<object width=\"425\" height=\"350\"><param name=\"movie\" value=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/pv5zWaTEVkI\"></param><embed src=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/pv5zWaTEVkI\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"425\" height=\"350\"></embed></object>\r\n\r\nThese have to be some of the most creative music videos I\'ve seen in a long time, and they\'re being distributed for free on YouTube and Google Video, just like anyone else in the world can. \r\n\r\nThere is a lot of talk about how YouTube is flawed because it promotes copyright infringement. I think it\'s great that there are content owners out there who realize how YouTube can be an incredible way to get your stuff out there. \r\n\r\nThe real problem with YouTube, if any, is that you have to cover the bandwidth and hosting cost for videos that aren\'t always played on your site and supported by your ads. ','OK GO: Who needs MTV?',0,'','publish','open','open','','ok-go-who-needs-mtv','','','2006-08-03 15:27:49','2006-08-03 22:27:49','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/03/ok-go-who-needs-mtv/',0,'post','',0),
	(792,1,'2006-08-03 16:11:01','2006-08-03 23:11:01','Looking at articles in Business Week like <a href=\"http://www.businessweek.com/magazine/content/06_33/b3997001.htm\">this one</a> covering Kevin Rose (<a href=\"http://www.digg.com\">Digg.com</a>) makes me realize several things about being an entrepreneur on the web today:<br /><ul><li>You either need to be able to do something yourself, or have the cash to actually hire someone who can. It sounds obvious, but you\'d be surprised how easily you can talk yourself into \"I\'ll find someone who knows how to do this, and we\'ll split the profits.\"</li><li>A great way to offer a successful service is to find out something that web users are doing already or want to be doing, and come up with a better way for them to do it. (In the case of Digg, that \"something\" was \"find and share cool links with other people\"). </li><li>In the increasinly long-tail digital world, there\'s as much money or more to be made in successfully aggregating&nbsp; content (being a filter) as there is in creating it. Practically every link on Digg sends you to another site, and it\'s almost as popular as the <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com\">New York Times</a>. </li></ul>','Entrepreneuring on the web',0,'','publish','open','open','','entrepreneuring-on-the-web','','','2006-08-03 16:11:03','2006-08-03 23:11:03','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/08/03/entrepreneuring-on-the-web/',0,'post','',0);
