	(1375,1,'2007-08-09 11:50:22','0000-00-00 00:00:00','I don\'t think there\'s any higher recommendation for a product or service than to enjoy using it. I\'ve been a bit of a plugin junkie over the past few years and I\'ve tried out literally dozens of plug-ins on this site. Some of them have stayed, many of them have gone. \r\n\r\nI\'m going to keep this post as an active list of the wordpress plug-ins I am currently using and roughly what they do. Over the time that I\'ve been using them, I\'ve gotten so used to relying on them that I think I\'d have trouble running this blog without them. \r\n\r\nIn no particular order, these are the plug-ins I\'m currently using:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.richardkmiller.com/blog/wordpress-plugin-what-would-seth-godin-do/\">What Would Seth Godin Do</a> - ','The list of Wordpress plugins that I use on this site',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2007-08-09 11:50:22','2007-08-09 18:50:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1375',0,'post','',0),
	(1330,1,'2007-07-25 17:21:35','2007-07-26 00:21:35','<object width=\"425\" height=\"350\"><param name=\"movie\" value=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/d_o07JSf1z8\"></param><param name=\"wmode\" value=\"transparent\"></param><embed src=\"http://www.youtube.com/v/d_o07JSf1z8\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" wmode=\"transparent\" width=\"425\" height=\"350\"></embed></object>','A little more practice is needed...',0,'','publish','open','open','','a-little-more-practice-is-needed','','','2007-07-25 17:21:35','2007-07-26 00:21:35','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/25/a-little-more-practice-is-needed/',0,'post','',0),
	(1291,1,'2007-07-05 15:14:01','2007-07-05 22:14:01','I know it\'s a little bit early to get all excited about Half-Life 2 Episode 2, but I\'m excited anyway. I\'ve been a Valve fanboy for years, ever since my friend tricked me into playing one of those games with \"graphics that look like triangles,\" which turned out to be Half-Life. \r\n\r\nSince then I\'ve bought and played through the sequels, the expansions, and I still play Counter-Strike. I met Gabe Newell, who has a charming habit of needing to prop his glasses back up on his nose. And now I want The Orange Box (for PC of course):\r\n\r\n<img width=\"450\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/orangeboxpc.jpg\' alt=\'The Orange Box\' />','The Orange Box',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-orange-box','','','2007-07-12 21:58:09','2007-07-13 04:58:09','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/05/the-orange-box/',0,'post','',2),
	(1292,1,'2007-07-05 15:09:41','2007-07-05 22:09:41','','The Orange Box',0,'','inherit','open','open','','the-orange-box','','','2007-07-05 15:09:41','2007-07-05 22:09:41','',18446744072525878513,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/orangeboxpc.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1293,1,'2007-07-06 17:32:42','2007-07-07 00:32:42','I\'ve <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/02/20/twitter-is-not-for-me/\">posted before</a> about not understanding why people like <a href=\"http://twitter.com/jasonp107\">Twitter</a>. I thought it was a little bit trendy and a little bit overblown. \r\n\r\nI think I was right about both of those things, because you don\'t hear nearly as much about it now, but I was wrong about it not being fun. It\'s fun because you get to talk to people, which is the whole point of blogging, web whatever-point-oh, social networking, and generally being human. \r\n\r\nSome RSS feed of what you\'re doing isn\'t really that great in and of itself. But when you realize that it\'s a big shared aggregate of people watching what other people are saying, then getting to respond...well that\'s pretty cool. ','What\'s fun about Twitter is what\'s fun about blogging',0,'','publish','open','open','','whats-fun-about-twitter-is-whats-fun-about-blogging','','\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/02/20/twitter-is-not-for-me/','2007-07-06 17:32:42','2007-07-07 00:32:42','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/06/whats-fun-about-twitter-is-whats-fun-about-blogging/',0,'post','',0),
	(1294,1,'2007-07-08 11:05:53','0000-00-00 00:00:00','I was reading a little bit of Communications Magazine (no link because I can\'t find their stuff online) earlier today. They have a huge cover-story on \"the Science of Games,\" which, as far as I can tell, is a compilation of several studies and reports from research groups that are looking in to game development as a science. \n\nIt\'','Serious games research',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2007-07-08 11:05:53','2007-07-08 18:05:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1294',0,'post','',0),
	(1295,1,'2007-07-08 17:33:51','2007-07-09 00:33:51','I realized a few days ago that the HDTV channels I had listed in MCE mysteriously disappeared sometime in the past weeks. The channels from basic cable are still in the program guide, and they all still work, but Vista won\'t let me add any channel that isn\'t numbered between 2 and 62.\r\n\r\nI\'ve got an ATI HDTV Wonder for the antenna and an older Hauppauge WinTV350 for the old cable. They both work, because the HDTV worked earlier, and now the cable works. \r\n\r\nBut the bizarre part is that I can\'t find ANYTHING about it online. Maybe I\'m just suffering from massive internet failure, but I can\'t find anything in Google, on <a href=\"http://thegreenbutton.com/default.aspx\">The Green Button</a> forums, or Yahoo, or wherever else I can think of, that actually talks about anything like this. \r\n\r\nI\'ve heard somewhere that you\'re not supposed to be able to use multiple tuners in Vista unless they are the same model of tuner (mine are not), but I can\'t find that anywhere online. Nor can I find a setting in the Vista MCE interface that lets me configure my tuners. It just gives me the option to tell them whether I get my TV from an \"antenna\" or from \"cable.\" What gives?','Vista MCE won\'t let me list both HDTV and Cable channels at the same time',0,'','publish','open','open','','vista-mce-wont-let-me-list-both-hdtv-and-cable-channels-at-the-same-time','','','2007-07-08 17:33:51','2007-07-09 00:33:51','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/08/vista-mce-wont-let-me-list-both-hdtv-and-cable-channels-at-the-same-time/',0,'post','',0),
	(1296,1,'2007-07-08 23:25:16','2007-07-09 06:25:16','Ok, so that\'s not quite the way Sony phrased it, but <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/09/business/09sony.html?ref=business\">according to the New York Times</a> Sony has finally decided that $600 is way more than people are willing to spend for a console that has one decent game and looks like it belongs in a restaurant. They\'re dropping it to $500. \r\n\r\nApparently the motivation is Microsoft\'s recent widespread trouble with 360s breaking all over the place. I know the rumors are true because <a href=\"http://www.rabidspacedog.com\">my friend Ethan\'s</a> xbox, already sent in for repairs once, has just busted again. Poor guy has to pay to send it in to Microsoft so that can give him something back that might work for more than 6 months at a time. \r\n\r\nBut the kicker? Sony\'s not willing to give up on the $600 price tag altogether. The 60gb consoles are going to drop in price on Thursday, but soon (August) buyers will find the shelves chock-full of 80gb, $600 paperweights in their place. Why? Because nobody in their right mind would pay $100 for a 20gb hard drive. ','Sony (finally) drops the price on the PS3 trainwreck, decides to introduce a new expensive model \"just in case\"',0,'','publish','open','open','','sony-finally-drops-the-price-on-the-ps3-trainwreck-decides-to-introduce-a-new-expensive-model-just-in-case','','','2007-07-08 23:27:27','2007-07-09 06:27:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/08/sony-finally-drops-the-price-on-the-ps3-trainwreck-decides-to-introduce-a-new-expensive-model-just-in-case/',0,'post','',0),
	(1297,1,'2007-07-09 11:22:22','2007-07-09 18:22:22','I am a huge sucker for sparsity. Have you ever been in one of those retail stores that has only two shelves, and yet still manages to stock everything you\'re looking for? \r\n\r\nBy now my room is a hopeless wreck, with about fifteen years of accumulated crap filling the corners and the shelves, hiding everything I actually want, which is inconvenient (like my copy of Supreme Commander, which mysteriously disappeared last week and hasn\'t resurfaced). But when I move in another month or so I\'m going to sort of \"reset\" everything and see how that goes. \r\n\r\nThis also extends to my computers. I like clean desktops. I\'m upset that when I installed BootCamp on my MacBookPro it made a second icon for my Windows HDD. That\'s one too many icons. \r\n\r\nIf I absolutely have to get something read, or processed, or somehow done, I\'ll put it on my desktop, because it will bug me until I can put it somewhere else. \r\n\r\nAnd so it is also with e-mail signatures. I think the vast majority of e-mail signatures are cumbersome. How could they not be? Let\'s look at all the stuff you want in a signature, ideally:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Name</li>\r\n<li>Company</li>\r\n<li>Company URL</li>\r\n<li>Personal URL</li>\r\n<li>AIM/Skype</li>\r\n<li>Job Title</li>\r\n<li>Email address</li>\r\n<li>Phone number</li>\r\n<li>Fax number</li>\r\n<li>Confidentiality statement</li></ul>\r\n\r\nThat\'s a lot of stuff to fit elegantly at the bottom of an e-mail. I want to craft a signature for myself, but before I can have one I need to figure out how to compact all of that into something that looks clean. \r\n\r\nI\'m thinking I can probably get rid of <em>Name</em> and <em>Email address</em> right off the bat, because I usually sign my name to e-mails and my address is, well, right there in the \"From\" bar. I also don\'t yet have a Fax Machine, so that\'s a problem I can put off until later. \r\n\r\nThat still leaves me with Company/URL/URL/AIM/Job Title/Phone number/Statement. \r\n\r\nMaybe I could simply put in a link to a full profile on my web site. Any sweet ideas?\r\n\r\nYou may now proceed to ignore most of my post and tell me in the comments that I must like the Apple store. Which I do. ','Email signatures',0,'','publish','open','open','','email-signatures','','','2007-07-09 11:22:22','2007-07-09 18:22:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/09/email-signatures/',0,'post','',2),
	(1298,1,'2007-07-10 12:10:38','2007-07-10 19:10:38','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/blogtips.png\' alt=\'BlogTips Pic\' />Just yesterday we Blog Business Summit folks launched our little <a href=\"http://www.facebook.com/apps/application.php?id=2465293443\">BlogTips Facebook Application</a>, which, although the development hasn\'t been very hands-on for me, has been fun to have going. It\'s also fun to watch people sign up - we\'re up to 41 members already, which is excellent news. \r\n\r\n\"What does it do?\" What a silly question - it provides blogging tips! The cool part is that the tips are all user-submitted. Add the application and submit as many blogging tips as you like, and every day we select a new tip to be displayed on the application widget. \r\n\r\nAnd just for fun, because we like people, before the conference we\'re having our speakers review all the submitted tips to pick the best one, and whoever submitted that tip gets a free 3-day pass to the Chicago BBS this September. \r\n\r\nThink you got the blogging chops? Then go win a free conference pass. ','Everybody do the \"BlogTip,\" or something less cheezy but equally useful',0,'','publish','open','open','','everybody-do-the-blogtip-or-something-less-cheezy-but-equally-useful','','','2007-07-10 12:11:39','2007-07-10 19:11:39','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/10/everybody-do-the-blogtip-or-something-less-cheezy-but-equally-useful/',0,'post','',0),
	(1299,1,'2007-07-10 12:09:59','2007-07-10 19:09:59','','BlogTips Pic',0,'','inherit','open','open','','blogtips-pic','','','2007-07-10 12:09:59','2007-07-10 19:09:59','',18446744072525458584,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/blogtips.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(1300,1,'2007-07-11 13:40:25','2007-07-11 20:40:25','I realize that the reason I cant find an online version of the trailer for The Dark is Rising is probably because the trailer was just made. But on the other hand, it\'s showing at the beginning of Harry Potter in the theaters, so why can\'t they stream it online?\r\n\r\nI missed the trailer last night, and thought, \"no problem, I\'ll just check it out online.\" The Dark is Rising Sequence by Susan Cooper has been one of my favorite stories for years, and it could make an awesome set of movies. \r\n\r\nHowever, it seems like Fox is (as usual) being retarded, and not providing the trailer online anywhere. A trailer is free advertising, it builds hype, people want to see them and share them---why limit yourself? ','Any movie without an online trailer is shooting itself in the foot',0,'','publish','open','open','','any-movie-without-an-online-trailer-is-shooting-itself-in-the-foot','','','2007-07-11 13:40:25','2007-07-11 20:40:25','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/11/any-movie-without-an-online-trailer-is-shooting-itself-in-the-foot/',0,'post','',3),
	(1301,1,'2007-07-11 16:06:10','2007-07-11 23:06:10','Everybody hates surveys, which is why we\'re putting everyone who takes this survey in a drawing to get a $200 discount for our Chicago conference. \r\n\r\nWe need to gather some information on how bloggers go about gathering information (fun!). I just took the survey myself, it\'s about four pages with one or two questions on each, with nice, big, easy to see buttons all over the place. \r\n\r\n<strong><a href=\"http://blogbusinesssummit.com/infogatheringsurvey\">Here is the survey link.</a></strong>\r\n\r\nGratsi\r\n','Take a survey and get in a drawing for $200 off our conference',0,'','publish','open','open','','take-a-survey-and-get-in-a-drawing-for-200-off-our-conference','','','2007-07-11 16:06:10','2007-07-11 23:06:10','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/11/take-a-survey-and-get-in-a-drawing-for-200-off-our-conference/',0,'post','',0),
	(1302,1,'2007-07-11 18:40:46','2007-07-12 01:40:46','<blockquote>[Asassin\'s Creed] was ridiculously good! Like POWPOW to the nth degree. So was crysis and bio shock. And a few other awedome games. Oh the new tony hawk is good too.</blockquote>\r\n\r\n...the fifth text-message update just buzzed my phone from my friend who is currently down in LA, at E3. I\'ve never had the opportunity to attend myself. And yet...\r\n\r\nI\'m not sure how much I\'m missing this year. The big downsize has <a href=\"http://www.clickz.com/showPage.html?page=3626334\">removed a lot of the distractions</a> from the floor, I\'m sure, but the distractions are why I\'ve wanted to go in the past. In my mind, you go to E3 for the same reason you go to Vegas. \r\n\r\nI think the ESA might be trying to force the industry into \"growing up.\" Which is silly, because frankly, it\'s the <em>video game</em> industry. It\'s entertainment. Sure, video games aren\'t just for kids, but video games <em>are</em> for fun. Of course, the ESA could also just be trying to save game companies from spending gazillions of dollars on the displays. \r\n\r\nAs nice as it would be to get some play time in with games that I\'ve been drooling over like Crysis, I\'m <a href=\"http://www.penny-arcade.com/2007/07/11\">like Tycho</a> in my cynical approach to marketing materials. What in this video is truly representative of the game? What is pre-rendered? What is the campaign <em>actually like</em>?\r\n\r\nI feel like for an event like this, which is mainly a big marketing venue, I\'m almost better off just picking through the coverage online. \r\n\r\nStill, it would be fun to go. ','Am I better off not being at E3?',0,'','publish','open','open','','am-i-better-off-not-being-at-e3','','','2007-07-11 23:30:55','2007-07-12 06:30:55','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/11/am-i-better-off-not-being-at-e3/',0,'post','',1),
	(1303,1,'2007-07-11 23:47:35','2007-07-12 06:47:35','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/sonyhome.jpg\' alt=\'sonyhome.jpg\' />I\'ve been in the habit, over the past year, of bashing Sony for practically every decision their gaming division has made. The PSP has been pretty lackluster from the get-go, and let\'s not go anywhere near the PS3 launch. \r\n\r\nTo their credit, however, the Sony press conference <a href=\"http://games.gearlive.com/playfeed/article/q107-e3-2007-sonys-press-conference-recap-and-impressions/\">coverage at PlayFeed</a> suggests that Sony is trying to put some new life into its gaming machines the only real way they can: creating sweet games and dropping them in an innovative interface. \r\n\r\nThey\'re crazy new <a href=\"http://kotaku.com/gaming/gdc07/gdc07-sony-unveils-home-242332.php\">HOME system</a> which I hear tons of good things about, is actually a development on an idea that I\'ve heard tossed around the blogosphere for some time. A while ago <a href=\"http://scobleizer.com/2006/03/27/second-life-is-an-os/\">Scoble mused</a> about making an Operating System based on SecondLife, which is more or less what this sounds like. \r\n\r\nAnd then I read things like this:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>PSPâ€™s turn, as a plethora of trailers are shown for various games, including SOCOM Tactical Strike, NBA 08, Castlevania: The Dracula X Chronicles, The Sims 2: Castaway, Silent Hill Origins, God of War: Chains of Olympus, Patapon, SWAT: Target Liberty, and more.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nWhich is great news for a handheld system that is largely dead. If Sony keeps at it, they may yet push some real life back into their games department. Of course they need to drop the $60 pricetag on the games, though - I\'ll just buy the PC version for $10 less. ','Can Sony revive the PSP? The PS3?',0,'','publish','open','open','','can-sony-revive-the-psp-the-ps3','','\nhttp://scobleizer.com/2006/03/27/second-life-is-an-os/','2007-07-11 23:47:35','2007-07-12 06:47:35','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/11/can-sony-revive-the-psp-the-ps3/',0,'post','',0),
	(1304,1,'2007-07-11 23:47:01','2007-07-12 06:47:01','','sonyhome.jpg',0,'','inherit','open','open','','sonyhomejpg','','','2007-07-11 23:47:01','2007-07-12 06:47:01','',1303,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/sonyhome.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1305,1,'2007-07-12 13:25:39','2007-07-12 20:25:39','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/dsbaseballsm.jpg\' alt=\'DS baseball sm\' />The <a href=\"http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/businesstechnology/2003785404_interactivefans12.html\">article in the Seattle Times</a> notes how \"little fanfare\" has been made of the feature. I went to a Mariners game two weeks ago and I had no idea this even existed:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>The innovative program is called the Nintendo Fan Network. For a fee, the network uploads a program onto the user\'s DS Lite and allows fans to order food and drinks, watch the live television feed of the game, access stats and scores and play trivia, all from the comfort of their seat â€” whether it\'s a premium seat behind home plate or in the top row of the stadium.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nThe fee mentioned is $5 for one game, or $30 for 10 games. It irks me a little bit that they think they can charge you to make it more convenient for you to <em>buy food</em>, but then I realize that they\'re also Wi-Fi-ing you a live TV stream of the game and providing stats and trivia. \r\n\r\nSince I tend to sit in the nosebleeds at Mariners games, that could be really freaking cool. I think I\'ll head out to a game sometime soon with my trusty DS to give this system a try. I\'ll let you know what I think. \r\n\r\n','Nintendo (continues) to think outside the box: use your DS Lite at a Mariners game',0,'','publish','open','open','','nintendo-continues-to-think-outside-the-box-use-your-ds-lite-at-a-mariners-game','','','2007-07-12 13:25:39','2007-07-12 20:25:39','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/12/nintendo-continues-to-think-outside-the-box-use-your-ds-lite-at-a-mariners-game/',0,'post','',0),
	(1306,1,'2007-07-12 13:24:41','2007-07-12 20:24:41','','DS baseball sm',0,'','inherit','open','open','','ds-baseball-sm','','','2007-07-12 13:24:41','2007-07-12 20:24:41','',18446744072525280324,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/dsbaseballsm.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1307,1,'2007-07-12 15:24:19','2007-07-12 22:24:19','According to some stranger with funny graphics, rounded corners, and a quick quiz:\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://mingle2.com/blog-addiction\" style=\"color: #D64B32; text-decoration: none; display: block; width: 286px; height: 128px; padding-top: 50px; padding-left: 17px; background: url(http://mingle2.com/img/bb/blog_addiction/badge.jpg) no-repeat; font-family: Times New Roman, sans-serif; font-size: 30px;\">82%<span style=\"display: none;\">How Addicted to Blogging Are You?</span></a>','I\'m 82% addicted to blogging',0,'','publish','open','open','','im-82-addicted-to-blogging','','','2007-07-12 15:24:53','2007-07-12 22:24:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/12/im-82-addicted-to-blogging/',0,'post','',0),
	(1308,1,'2007-07-12 19:58:05','2007-07-13 02:58:05','If you actually read my blog in a web browser, please bear with the changing visuals. Just playing around. ','Mucking about with themes',0,'','publish','open','open','','mucking-about-with-themes','','','2007-07-12 19:58:05','2007-07-13 02:58:05','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/12/mucking-about-with-themes/',0,'post','',0),
	(1310,1,'2007-07-17 10:06:41','2007-07-17 17:06:41','I have one particularly eloquent thing to add to <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/17/business/media/17dow.html?_r=1&amp;th&amp;emc=th&amp;oref=slogin\">this article</a> in the NYTimes:\n\nNooooooooooooooooooo\n\nMurdoch already owns everything from the Times of London to Fox News Channel--meaning that the chances of you picking up media in the western world that isn\'t controlled by either Murdoch or Ted Turner are smaller than You-Know-Who\'s You-Know What. \n\nNot only that, but this is the man who is notorious for meddling in \"news\" articles, slanting information and somehow managing to cater to the most misinformed citizenry in the United States despite the fact that his cable news is \"fair and balanced.\" Inexplicable. \n\nI think it\'s a bad idea for a couple, basic reasons:\n<ol><li>Newspapers, especially the WSJ, aren\'t dying yet and don\'t need a lifeline.</li><li>Having two or three people controlling 90% of the information we as people receive is just a <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">bad idea</span>. Did you see <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Wag the Dog</span>? If Turner and Murdoch decided to fake a freaking War, we\'d have no idea they made it up. \n</li></ol>So please, Bancroft family, don\'t sell!\n<p style=\"text-align: right; font-size: 8px\">Blogged with <a href=\"http://www.flock.com/blogged-with-flock\" title=\"Flock\" target=\"_new\">Flock</a></p><!-- technorati tags begin --><p style=\"font-size:10px;text-align:right;\">Tags: <a href=\"http://technorati.com/tag/bancroft\" rel=\"tag\">bancroft</a>, <a href=\"http://technorati.com/tag/wsj\" rel=\"tag\">wsj</a>, <a href=\"http://technorati.com/tag/%20wall%20street%20journal\" rel=\"tag\"> wall street journal</a>, <a href=\"http://technorati.com/tag/%20murdoch\" rel=\"tag\"> murdoch</a>, <a href=\"http://technorati.com/tag/%20newscorp\" rel=\"tag\"> newscorp</a></p><!-- technorati tags end -->','Dow Jones &quot;tentatively agreed\" to Murdoch\'s offer',0,'','publish','open','open','','dow-jones-tentatively-agreed-to-murdochs-offer','','\nArray','2007-07-17 10:06:41','2007-07-17 17:06:41','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/17/dow-jones-tentatively-agreed-to-murdochs-offer/',0,'post','',0),
	(1311,1,'2007-07-17 15:14:20','2007-07-17 22:14:20','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/supcombox.jpg\' alt=\'Supreme Commander\' />The motherboard for my crazy <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/05/03/my-new-beastie/\">self-built new gaming rig</a> came with a copy of Supreme Commander, which was very handy because every review tells me that game is <em>sweet</em>. It was also handy because in the new <a href=\"http://www.gamesforwindows.com/en-US/Games/Pages/SupremeCommander.aspx\">Games For Windows</a> system, Supreme Commander recommends a computer that ranks a \"5.0\" in vista. Mine ranks a 5.6 ;)\r\n\r\nThe game came in a flimsy little paper sleeve, however, so I diligently wrote the CD key down in an excel file on my hard drive, so I wouldn\'t lose it in case I ever needed to install it again. Unfortunately for me, Fate likes a good laugh, so I lost the game disc. I only played the game once. \r\n\r\nToday Valve announced that Supreme Commander, among other games, will be available on Steam:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>The starting line up of THQ titles heading to Steam starting today spans a wide range of genres and hails from some of the most prestigious development studios in the gaming industry, such as THQ\'s Relic Entertainment, Gas Powered Games, and Pandemic. Specifically, the titles are:\r\n\r\nSupreme Commander - (Strategy; developed by Gas Powered Games) \r\nS.T.A.L.K.E.R.: Shadow of Chernobyl - (Action; developed by GSC Game World)\r\nCompany of Heroes - (Strategy; developed by Relic Entertainment)\r\nTitan Quest  - (Role playing; developed by Lore Entertainment)\r\nTitan Quest: Immortal Throne - (Role Playing; developed by Lore Entertainment)\r\nFull Spectrum Warrior -  (Action; developed by Pandemic)\r\nFull Spectrum Warrior: Ten Hammers -  (Action; developed by Pandemic)\r\nWarhammer 40,000: Dawn of War Gold Edition - (Strategy; developed by Relic Entertainment)\r\nWarhammer 40,000: Dawn of War: Dark Crusade - (Strategy; developed by Relic Entertainment)\r\nWarhammer 40,000: Dawn of War Platinum Edition - (Strategy; developed by Relic Entertainment)\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\nThis is wonderful news, because now I can type in my CD Key and play the game through Steam regardless of where that nefarious disc has run itself off to. Also, I\'ll never have to find that silly Dawn of War disc again. Hooray for the disc-less future!\r\n','Steam returns Supreme Commander from the grave',0,'','publish','open','open','','steam-returns-supreme-commander-from-the-grave','','\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/05/03/my-new-beastie/','2007-07-17 15:14:20','2007-07-17 22:14:20','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/17/steam-returns-supreme-commander-from-the-grave/',0,'post','',1),
	(1312,1,'2007-07-17 15:13:36','2007-07-17 22:13:36','','Supreme Commander',0,'','inherit','open','open','','supreme-commander','','','2007-07-17 15:13:36','2007-07-17 22:13:36','',1311,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/supcombox.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1314,1,'2007-07-18 16:47:33','2007-07-18 23:47:33','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/vista_logo.jpg\' alt=\'Vista Logo\' />I\'m listening to the PC Gamer Podcast and hearing the little jabs at Vista that are almost inevitable when you decide to charge gamers another couple hundred dollars to upgrade to a new operating system. I\'ve heard a lot of jabs about Vista in the past few months, and I\'ve <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/06/25/missing-hard-drive-space-in-windows-vista/\">had my own problems</a> with the OS, but overall I\'m pretty happy with it. \r\n\r\nMy Vista machine exists only for gaming. I\'ve got a MacBookPro that I use for work, email, blogging, video editing, photoshop...literally everything else. On my desktop: Vista and games. \r\n\r\nAnd I like it. \r\n\r\nI use Steam the most, which is great because it\'s worked flawlessly with my computer since I booted it up. Vista also has a self-populating \"Games\" folder, which grabs a little picture of the game box and some brief info on the game, such as the game\'s recommended performance rating--\r\n\r\nOh yeah, <em>performance ratings</em>. What a cool idea. \r\n\r\nThat\'s one of my favorite features, because it makes sense on so many levels. Computers have always suffered from needless complications. Gamers, especially console gamers (read: the mass market consumer), don\'t like having to figure out what the difference between AMD and Intel is, which one they have, and whether or not they should open the nonreturnable box in their hands. \r\n\r\nWhat\'s the solution? Rank you computer with a number system. Rank the games with the same number system. Numbers match? Game work. So simple, even cavemen can get offended. \r\n\r\nAlso, and for a gamer most importantly, almost all of my games have worked. I have a few more to install (if I ever find those damn CDs) like DeusEx, or I\'m not even sure I\'m going to try <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/03/how-to-run-tie-fighter-collectors-cd-edition-on-windows-xp/\">TIE Fighter</a> - but so far all the Steam games work, C&C Generals works, Civ IV works after a minor patch from their site. The only game that hasn\'t worked yet is Dawn of War (!!) - but the last time I checked for patches was about a month ago. \r\n\r\nI know there aren\'t any DX10 games out yet, and I know that Vista takes a chunk of memory to run (although it\'s a lot more <em>efficient</em> with it than XP - you\'ll practically never have to reboot), but there <em>will</em> be DX10 games, and as to memory--use <a href=\"http://www.microsoft.com/windows/products/windowsvista/features/details/readyboost.mspx\">ReadyBoost</a>. Yes, it actually works. \r\n\r\nThe bottom line is: I\'m happy with Vista. In fact I like it better than XP.','Vista for video games: compatibility and features',0,'','publish','open','open','','vista-for-video-games-compatibility-and-features','','http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/06/25/missing-hard-drive-space-in-windows-vista/\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/03/how-to-run-tie-fighter-collectors-cd-edition-on-windows-xp/','2007-07-18 16:47:33','2007-07-18 23:47:33','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/18/vista-for-video-games-compatibility-and-features/',0,'post','',0),
	(1315,1,'2007-07-18 16:41:58','2007-07-18 23:41:58','','Vista Logo',0,'','inherit','open','open','','vista-logo','','','2007-07-18 16:41:58','2007-07-18 23:41:58','',1314,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2007/07/vista_logo.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1316,1,'2007-07-18 17:35:18','2007-07-19 00:35:18','It irks me that in the past year Jason Preston Gore has supplanted my Google spot. \r\n\r\nIt should probably annoy me more that some people probably come across this site and assume I am him (I do not have a marc jacobs tatoo), but really I\'m just annoyed that if you <a href=\"http://www.google.com/search?source=ig&hl=en&q=%22Jason+Preston%22&btnG=Google+Search\">search for \"Jason Preston,\"</a> only three of the top 10 links actually refer to me, when I used to have something like 7/10. \r\n\r\nI\'m not even the first hit! It\'s his Friendster profile! Blah. ','Jason Preston Gore is all over my Google',0,'','publish','open','open','','jason-preston-gore-is-all-over-my-google','','','2007-07-18 17:35:18','2007-07-19 00:35:18','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2007/07/18/jason-preston-gore-is-all-over-my-google/',0,'post','',5),
