	(1652,1,'2008-02-14 12:54:08','2008-02-14 19:54:08','Hooray!\r\n\r\nI suffer from no illusions that my <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/05/how-important-is-rss-to-blogging/\">big long whiny post</a> about cutting it off had anything to do with his decision to let the tap flow once more, but I can now read full-length posts from <a href=\"http://dilbertblog.typepad.com\">The Dilbert Blog</a> in my RSS reader. \r\n\r\nGlory. Sweet Glory.','The Dilbert Blog full RSS feed is back',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-dilbert-blog-full-rss-feed-is-back','','\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/05/how-important-is-rss-to-blogging/','2008-02-14 12:54:08','2008-02-14 19:54:08','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/14/the-dilbert-blog-full-rss-feed-is-back/',0,'post','',0),
	(1653,1,'2008-02-14 19:06:58','2008-02-15 02:06:58','In case you\'ve ever wanted to know what Ethan Ambabo\'s list is, and it is an interesting list, you can check it out over on <a href=\"http://rabidspacedog.com/2008/02/15/men-i-would-go-gay-for/\">RabidSpaceDog</a>.\r\n\r\nIt is a list of the men he would go gay for. \r\n\r\nIt has been posted in honor of Valentine\'s Day, which is today. \r\n\r\nToday is also the first Valentine\'s Day I have ever, in my life, gotten to spend with a girlfriend. \r\n\r\nThat is what I call totally swick. \r\n\r\nSwick = Sweet + Sick. ','Today is what?',0,'','publish','open','open','','today-is-what','','\nhttp://rabidspacedog.com/2008/02/15/men-i-would-go-gay-for/','2008-02-14 19:06:58','2008-02-15 02:06:58','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/14/today-is-what/',0,'post','',0),
	(1654,1,'2008-02-15 14:44:01','2008-02-15 21:44:01','In case you weren\'t sure by now that newspapers were, by and large, in Big Trouble, the NYT has announced that they\'re going to <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/reuters/business/business-newyorktimes-layoffs.html?scp=1&sq=nyt+100+newsroom&st=nyt\">drop 100 newsroom staff</a> over the next year. \r\n\r\nIf you\'re a newspaper facing the next fifteen years, you have to be feeling pretty grim. \r\n\r\nI think you can attribute most of the problems newspapers are having today to three things:\r\n\r\n<ol><li><strong>The New York Times launched for free on the internet</strong>, effectively setting the price point for news online going forwards. Largely because of this, consumers expect that their online news should be free. If you want to charge for it, you\'re going to have to find something that the average reader <em>feels right</em> paying for.</li>\r\n<li><strong>Craigslist</strong>. I think this one is just gone. Localized listings are no longer a market that newspapers can really control. People don\'t want to have to check many places for classifieds. Even if one newspaper manages to beat out Craigslist, that doesn\'t help the industry, it just helps one paper.</li>\r\n<li><strong>The Free Rider problem</strong>. This is the big one. I don\'t have the statistics in front of me and I\'m too lazy to look them up (which proves my point), but newspapers account for at least 90% of all reporting in the freaking world. \r\n\r\nWrap your head around this concept: without newspapers, <em>there would not be news</em>. Slowly, magazines, tabloids, radio, and TV stations have figured out that they can read the newspapers and \"report\" on what they\'re reporting. \r\n\r\nI\'m exaggerating for effect of course, but the point is basically accurate. The work that newspapers produce (the reporting of news) is being distributed and redistributed and monetized worldwide, and newspapers are not getting a cut of the secondary, tertiary revenue. \r\n\r\nThe trick in the internet age is to put your content EVERYWHERE POSSIBLE and monetize it in every instance. \r\n\r\nThink about Revver. Any Revver video can be embedded anywhere (content everywhere). Wherever it is embedded, it includes a post-roll ad that pays back to the original content creator (monetize in every instance). \r\n\r\nIt\'s a tough nut to crack.</li></ol>','How can newspapers make money?',0,'','publish','open','open','','how-can-newspapers-make-money','','','2008-02-15 17:59:24','2008-02-16 00:59:24','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/15/how-can-newspapers-make-money/',0,'post','',0),
	(1655,1,'2008-02-18 00:13:21','2008-02-18 07:13:21','For a while now I\'ve thought it would be a fun project to set up a magazine based in Seattle that is like <a href=\"http://www.newyorker.com\">The New Yorker</a>. \r\n\r\nThe New Yorker is kind of an elitist publication, but often deservedly so. I think that we in Seattle often think highly enough of ourselves that there\'s a market for a self-congratulatory literary publication. \r\n\r\nThe New Yorker and the Atlantic Monthly pretty much have the national stage nailed. I\'m not talking about competing with them. I\'m thinking of something that has an ultimate end-goal print run of 10,000. Realistically, it will all be online in 10 years anyway. \r\n\r\nI think that rather than having a literary magazine <em>about</em> Seattle I should start one that\'s about being <em>written by</em> Seattleites. \r\n\r\nIn other words, if you want to know who\'s who among Seattle thinkers and writers, or for some reason you want to know <em>what</em> they think and write, this is the local magazine to pick up. \r\n\r\nSounds like fun. But I need a name.','The New Seattler (or something)',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-new-seattler-or-something','','','2008-02-18 00:13:21','2008-02-18 07:13:21','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/18/the-new-seattler-or-something/',0,'post','',0),
	(1656,1,'2008-02-18 14:57:46','2008-02-18 21:57:46','From Umair at Bubblegeneration. <a href=\"http://www.bubblegeneration.com/?a=a&resource=musicrisk1\">Check it out</a>:\r\n\r\n<blockquote> The point is this: the net offers listeners insurance against the music industry itself. File-sharing isn\'t simply theft. Rather, file-sharing is risk-sharing - against an industry with the freedom to undertake hidden action in the extreme, and not live up to the contract it has written. Remember, the contract said that labels would assume the risk in exchange for dollars from listeners - so when moral hazard lets labels try and push risk to listeners, is it any surprise that listeners try and minimize it by parceling it out? </blockquote> ','Some genius-level thinking on the music industry',0,'','publish','open','open','','some-genius-level-thinking-on-the-music-industry','','','2008-02-18 14:57:46','2008-02-18 21:57:46','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/18/some-genius-level-thinking-on-the-music-industry/',0,'post','',0),
	(1657,1,'2008-02-18 19:00:56','2008-02-19 02:00:56','I\'ve been enjoying a lot of stat padding recently because I am the <a href=\"http://www.google.com/search?hl=en&q=tv+links+replacement&btnG=Google+Search\">number 1 search result</a> for tv links replacement on Google. This is because mere hours after finding out about TV links\' demise, I posted a short list of sites people could use now that the old classic was offline, AND nobody more popular than me posted about it, either. \r\n\r\nI know that this is a temporary blessing. That sooner or later people will stop searching for replacement sites since they\'ll know what they are already. \r\n\r\nI can only hope that a few of the people cruising through will add <a href=\"http://feeds.feedburner.com/jasonpreston\">my RSS feed</a> to their reader or decide that my blog is cool enough to bookmark and check back later. \r\n\r\nIn the meantime, though, I\'m going to just enjoy the stat padding:\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/02/search-phrases.png\' alt=\'search phrases\' />\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/02/visits-pageviews.png\' alt=\'visits and pageviews\' />\r\n\r\nAnd if there were any doubts that Google pwnes the search space, let those doubts be put to rest:\r\n<img src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/02/search-pie.png\' alt=\'search pie\' />','Stat padding with Google search results',0,'','publish','open','open','','stat-padding-with-google-search-results','','','2008-02-18 20:01:22','2008-02-19 03:01:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/18/stat-padding-with-google-search-results/',0,'post','',0),
