	(1661,1,'2008-02-19 01:02:07','2008-02-19 08:02:07','I\'ve had <a href=\"http://twitter.com/jasonp107\">my Tweets</a> (from Twitter) running through my sidebar for some time now. \r\n\r\nThere are some awesome benefits to this system. Most importantly, I think they look cool over there. Also, it\'s  cool to have more of my \"lifestream\" aggregated to this blog. \r\n\r\nBut there are some downsides, too. Sometimes the page hangs when the Twitter servers are being slow. They\'re a bit out of the way, and a lot of people probably don\'t notice them. \r\n\r\nI recently installed <a href=\"http://alexking.org/blog/2007/05/07/twitter-tools-10\">Twitter Tools</a> on this blog, which lets me do some new, cool things. One thing is that all my blog posts get published automatically as Tweets through my Twitter account. \r\n\r\nAnother option I get is to import my tweets as blog posts. I think it would, most importantly, look less cool than the tweets in the sidebar. \r\n\r\nBut it will also pull them more legitimately into my \"lifestream,\" which is what I want this blog to be, and it will let people leave comments on the tweets (I\'d prefer they just @tweet me back, of course, but I can\'t have everything...). \r\n\r\nIt will also solve the issue with page load times as the tweets will be housed in my own database. And finally, for the same reasons, it will be an archive of my Tweets that is fully under my control.\r\n\r\nSo I\'m going to give that a try. I think it will work out well. ','After much deliberation, I have decided that Tweets will be posts',0,'','publish','open','open','','after-much-deliberation-i-have-decided-that-tweets-will-be-posts','','','2008-02-18 22:27:30','2008-02-19 05:27:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/19/after-much-deliberation-i-have-decided-that-tweets-will-be-posts/',0,'post','',0),
	(1662,1,'2008-02-18 19:53:45','2008-02-19 02:53:45','Making sure I follow everyone who follows me. Created a giant &quot;follow deficit.&quot; Now it\'s backwards. Who\'s not following me?? ;)','Making sure I follow everyone ...',0,'','publish','open','open','','making-sure-i-follow-everyone','','','2008-02-18 19:53:45','2008-02-19 02:53:45','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/18/making-sure-i-follow-everyone/',0,'post','',0),
	(1663,1,'2008-02-19 18:16:59','2008-02-20 01:16:59','<img align=\"right\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"4\" src=\'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/02/iphonegames.jpg\' alt=\'iphone games\' />Since I <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/01/19/my-new-iphone/\">got myself an iPhone</a> about a month ago, I\'ve been waiting anxiously to play games on it. \r\n\r\nI take it with me almost everywhere, unlike my Nintendo DS (which I wish I took everywhere). The battery life is exceptional, the screen is gorgeous, and the touch-screen is responsive and accurate. If that doesn\'t spell \"b-e-s-t.  m-o-b-i-l-e.  g-a-m-i-n-g.  d-e-v-i-c-e.  e-v-e-r,\" I don\'t know what does. \r\n\r\nUnfortunately, I\'m too much of a ninny to jailbreak my iPhone and install any of the games that are currently on the market, so I\'m waiting for the official SDK and \"legal\" 3rd party software. \r\n\r\nHere are my tips for those developers when the time comes:\r\n\r\n<ol><li><strong>Don\'t price yourself out of the market</strong> - legal or not, the free market will still exist (and thrive) for iPhone customers. Don\'t overcharge for games that are basically available for free.</li>\r\n<li><strong>Remember that the iPhone abandons all traditional input methods, and you should too</strong> - stop yourself from making touchscreen recreations of d-pads and A-B-Y-Z buttons.</li>\r\n<li><strong>The best iPhone games will be ones that can be \"insta-paused.\"</strong> In other words, people will be playing games for minutes (or possibly seconds) at a time, so the most successful games will probably be like Myst, where you can keep going in small chunks, or like Snood, where quitting doesn\'t really mean abandoning anything.</li>\r\n<li><strong>Don\'t exceed the \"system requirements.\"</strong> One of the reasons the iPhone feels like such a great phone is that Apple has been very careful not to let the CPU feel underpowered. At most I have to wait a second or two for a screen to load. Laggy gameplay will make the iPhone <em>and</em> your game feel clunky.</li>\r\n<li><strong>Battery life is king</strong> - you could have the greatest game in the world, but if it kills my battery life, i\'m not likely to play it. While I recognize that gaming is far more important than communicating with the outside world, <em>the iPhone is still my phone</em>. If it won\'t last me a full day, what\'s the use?</li></ol>','5 tips for iPhone game developers',0,'','publish','open','open','','5-tips-for-iphone-game-developers','','\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/01/19/my-new-iphone/','2008-02-19 18:18:48','2008-02-20 01:18:48','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2008/02/19/5-tips-for-iphone-game-developers/',0,'post','',2),
