	(1829,1,'2008-09-16 08:42:11','2008-09-16 15:42:11','John McCain.\r\n\r\nFind out more by reading my <a href=\"http://teresacentric.com/2008/09/why-talking-about-sarah-palin-is-a-big-big-mistake-for-democrats/\">guest post</a> on TeresaCentric.','Top of the ticket: Sarah Palin or John McCain?',0,'','publish','open','open','','top-of-the-ticket-sarah-palin-or-john-mccain','','\nhttp://teresacentric.com/2008/09/why-talking-about-sarah-palin-is-a-big-big-mistake-for-democrats/','2008-09-16 08:43:06','2008-09-16 15:43:06','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1829',0,'post','',0),
	(1830,1,'2008-09-11 17:53:09','0000-00-00 00:00:00','I\'m as guilty as the rest of us. I want to cash in on the Google search terms and the attention frenzy that has followed John McCain\'s VP pick almost nonstop since it was announced. \r\n\r\nBut I\'ll tell you all this now: the one sure-fire way for Democrats to lose the election this year is to talk about Sarah Palin.  \r\n\r\nThere\'s a scene in the movie Thank You For Smoking where Nick Naylor sits with his son Joey in California, eating ice cream. This is where Joey gets a quick tutorial in winning arguments:\r\n\r\n\"So you think chocolate is the end all and be all of ice cream, do you?\" asks Nick.\r\n\r\n\"It\'s the best ice cream. I wouldn\'t order any other,\" says Joey\r\n\r\n\"Oh, so it\'s all chocolate for you, is it?\" asks Nick.\r\n\r\n\"Yes, chocolate is all I need,\" says Joey.\r\n\r\n\"Well, I need more than chocolate,\" Nick launches into a speech. \"And for that matter, I need more than vanilla. I believe we need freedom and choice when it comes to our ice cream and that, Joey Naylor. That is the definition of liberty.\"\r\n\r\n\"But you didn\'t prove that vanilla was the best,\" Joey protests. \r\n\r\n\"I didn\'t have to. I proved that you\'re wrong. And if you\'re wrong, I\'m right,\" Nick answers.\r\n\r\nWinning an election is like winning a court case or an argument about ice cream. It\'s about controlling the conversation and making sure that people aren\'t thinking about the right things when they make their decision. \r\n\r\nThere\'s been a lot of speculation about the political tactics behind picking Palin. I\'ll tell you what it is. Palin is designed to attract two groups of voters:\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>The socially conservative Republican base</li>\r\n<li>People who don\'t figure out that she\'s the Vice Presidential candidate</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\nSo far, the Republican strategy is succeeding brilliantly. They\'ve snagged the single-issue conservative voters by bringing in a candidate that aligns strongly with their views. They\'ve at least temporarily reframed the entire election by getting people to choose between <em>Palin</em> and Obama, instead of <em>McCain</em> and Obama. \r\n\r\nAnd they\'ve pulled the rest of the conservative base back in line by making them forget that voting in this election is as simple as asking yourself that one, all-important question: are you better off than you were 8 years ago?\r\n\r\nFor most Americans, the answer is a resounding no. And that includes the Republican base. \r\n\r\n<strong>About the author</strong>: <em>Jason writes about newspapers and journalism at <a href=\"http://eatsleeppublish.com\">Eat Sleep Publish</a>. To learn more about the future of publishing, <a href=\"http://feeds.feedburner.com/EatSleepPublish\">subscribe</a> to his blog.</em>','Why talking about Sarah Palin is a big big mistake (for Democrats)',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2008-09-11 17:53:09','2008-09-12 00:53:09','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1830',0,'post','',0),
	(1831,1,'2008-09-09 17:51:07','2008-09-10 00:51:07','Found <a href=\"http://www.wired.com/gaming/gamingreviews/commentary/games/2008/09/gamesfrontiers_0908\">this</a> on Wired today. Basically it explains how playing videogames nonstop from middle school through college made me so damn smart:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>This led Steinkuehler to a fascinating and provocative conclusion: Videogames are becoming the new hotbed of scientific thinking for kids today.\r\n\r\nThis makes sense if you think about it for a second. After all, what is science? It\'s a technique for uncovering the hidden rules that govern the world. And videogames are simulated worlds that kids are constantly trying to master. Lineage and World of Warcraft aren\'t \"real\" world, of course, but they are consistent -- the behavior of the environment and the creatures in it are governed by hidden and generally unchanging rules, encoded by the game designers. In the process of learning a game, gamers try to deduce those rules. </blockquote>\r\n\r\nGo team videogames. ','Video Games teach the Scientific Method',0,'','publish','open','open','','video-games-teach-the-scientific-method','','','2008-09-09 17:51:07','2008-09-10 00:51:07','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1831',0,'post','',0),
	(1832,1,'2008-09-16 11:18:21','2008-09-16 18:18:21','<blockquote>(Washington, DC) â€” The first national survey of its kind finds that virtually all American teens play computer, console, or cell phone games and that the gaming experience is rich and varied, with a significant amount of social interaction and potential for civic engagement.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nHere is the <a href=\"http://www.macfound.org/site/apps/nlnet/content2.aspx?c=lkLXJ8MQKrH&b=4192109&content_id={CF9B933A-8261-4FE5-B9AD-AD751CDEEFC6}&notoc=1\">survey link</a>.\r\n\r\nOh, SNAP. Suck it, haters. ','Sweet video game validation',0,'','publish','open','open','','sweet-video-game-validation','','','2008-09-16 11:18:21','2008-09-16 18:18:21','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1832',0,'post','',0),
	(1833,1,'2008-09-23 09:24:42','2008-09-23 16:24:42','<span class=\"drop_cap\">I</span> sent out the first e-mail on the <a href=\"http://eatsleeppublish.com/lists/?p=subscribe&id=1\">Official Eat Sleep Publish E-mail List</a> today, which means that everyone on that list just got a full copy of the recording I made at The Pitch last week, but it also means that I managed to get PHPlist installed and working on my GoDaddy Virtual Dedicated server. \r\n\r\nUnlike other hosting solutions I\'ve used, GoDaddy goes a bit out of the way to make sure that you don\'t set up a massive spam machine on their servers (this is probably a good thing). I haven\'t gotten anywhere near to hitting it yet, but there\'s a 1,000 e-mail \"transaction\" limit per day, and scripts aren\'t allowed to run for more than about 360 seconds. \r\n\r\nAdditionally, GoDaddy disables things like the PHP send mail function and doesn\'t let PHP make directories writeable (which means you can\'t upload huge lists of e-mails, you have to enter them manually). \r\n\r\n<!--more-->That means that if you\'re sending large batches, you\'d be better off just using a paid system.\r\n\r\nBut if you\'re doing small potatoes like I am, PHPList will work just fine on GoDaddy. You just need to tell it to send e-mail via SMTP instead of the PHP send mail function. \r\n\r\nTo do that, just find the following code in your config.php file:\r\n\r\n<blockquote><code># To use a SMTP please give your server hostname here, leave it blank to use the standard\r\n# PHP mail() command.\r\ndefine(\"PHPMAILERHOST\",\'\');</code>\r\n\r\n<code># if you want to use smtp authentication when sending the email uncomment the following\r\n# two lines and set the username and password to be the correct ones\r\n#$phpmailer_smtpuser = \'smtpuser\';\r\n#$phpmailer_smtppassword = \'smtppassword\'; </code></blockquote>\r\n\r\nand put in your SMTP info. This is generally just the regular login info for any one of your SMTP e-mail accounts. \r\n\r\nSo your mailerhost is probably something like \"mail.domain.com\" and your user is \"user@domain.com\" and I have no idea what your password is.\r\n\r\nAfter that, PHPList should start sending mail just fine. It did for me.','PHPList doesn\'t work with GoDaddy? Try using SMTP',0,'','publish','open','open','','phplist-doesnt-work-with-godaddy-try-using-smtp','','','2008-09-23 09:24:42','2008-09-23 16:24:42','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1833',0,'post','',4),
	(1834,1,'2008-09-23 14:47:12','2008-09-23 21:47:12','Seth Godin is, as usual, 100% correct about the best way to use statistics as a marketer. Changing the statistics is a lot easier than getting people to admit that their worldview is wrong:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>If you, as a marketer, can package data in a way that people with a certain worldview can accept, you move the conversation forward far more quickly than if you merely dismiss the non-customers or the doubters as stupid.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nCheck out the <a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2008/09/probably-not-st.html\">full post</a>. ','Seth Godin on statistics',0,'','publish','open','open','','seth-godin-on-statistics','','','2008-09-23 14:47:12','2008-09-23 21:47:12','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1834',0,'post','',0),
	(1835,1,'2008-10-01 12:38:32','2008-10-01 19:38:32','<blockquote>\r\nGive people a break.\r\nThe break you probably deserve yourself.\r\nPeople are out to do good, 99% of the time.\r\nYou probably are too.\r\nSay thanks out loud and a lot.\r\nTry making someoneâ€™s day.\r\nChances are theyâ€™ll make yours in return.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\nThere\'s more here on the <a href=\"http://blogs.squidoo.com/squidblog/?p=276\">Squidblog</a>.','Brilliant advice on making the web a better place',0,'','publish','open','open','','brilliant-advice-on-making-the-web-a-better-place','','\nhttp://blogs.squidoo.com/squidblog/?p=276','2008-10-01 12:38:32','2008-10-01 19:38:32','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1835',0,'post','',0),
	(1836,1,'2008-10-23 10:47:52','0000-00-00 00:00:00','<h3>Story</h3>\r\n\r\nPeople don\'t want a big elaborate story. They want <em>gaps</em>.\r\n\r\n<h3>Freedom</h3>\r\n\r\nPeople want complete freedom? No, they want structure. Rebellion. \r\n\r\n<h3>Graphics</h3>\r\n\r\nGraphics don\'t matter? Bullshit!','The top three myths about video game design',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2008-10-23 10:47:52','2008-10-23 17:47:52','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1836',0,'post','',0),
	(1837,1,'2008-11-03 11:50:37','2008-11-03 18:50:37','Remember that they (we?) didn\'t turn out to keep the drinking age at 18 in the 1970s. ','To all those who think the youth vote turnout is likely to change',0,'','publish','open','open','','to-all-those-who-think-the-youth-vote-turnout-is-likely-to-change','','','2008-11-03 11:50:37','2008-11-03 18:50:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1837',0,'post','',1),
	(1838,1,'2008-11-06 15:22:59','2008-11-06 22:22:59','<img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/11/picture-1.png\" alt=\"\" title=\"submit ideas\" width=\"229\" height=\"231\" class=\"alignright size-full wp-image-1839 frame\" />I have to say that this little widget is one of the coolest things I\'ve found on <a href=\"http://www.change.gov/\">change.gov</a>. When Obama says that he wants the people to participate in their government, he apparently means it. \r\n\r\nPeople have always been free to submit their own ideas to the government in power, of course, but I don\'t think I\'ve ever seen it so freely <em>welcomed</em> and made so easy to do. \r\n\r\nThis is going to be cool. ','Of the people, by the people',0,'','publish','open','open','','of-the-people-by-the-people','','','2008-11-06 15:22:59','2008-11-06 22:22:59','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1838',0,'post','',1),
	(1839,1,'2008-11-06 15:19:38','2008-11-06 22:19:38','','submit ideas',0,'','inherit','open','open','','picture-1','','','2008-11-06 15:19:38','2008-11-06 22:19:38','',1838,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/11/picture-1.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),
	(1840,1,'2008-11-11 17:49:39','2008-11-12 00:49:39','I have no idea if this even exists. I tried some random searching and I haven\'t seen anything like it yet, but it\'s pretty straightforward. \r\n\r\nI want a device that works like a wireless phone system, but for speakers. \r\n\r\n1 \"base\" unit that has a USB cord for my computer.\r\n\r\n1, 2, 3, 4... units that all have stereo audio out & digital audio out that I can plug into a basic amp or receiver. \r\n\r\nThen a little selector on my computer that says what speakers I want to use. \r\n\r\n[Update: You can sort of do this by buying a bunch of Apple AirPorts, but I was hoping for a solution that costs less than $100 per room. ]','The home stereo tool I want the most',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-home-stereo-tool-i-want-the-most','','','2008-11-11 17:49:39','2008-11-12 00:49:39','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1840',0,'post','',0),
	(1841,1,'2008-11-13 13:32:20','2008-11-13 20:32:20','I thought this was a funny juxtaposition in my inbox today:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Hello,\r\n\r\nOnline safety is more important than even. The reason is simple:  The threats faced by minors are more serious than ever.    Cyberbullying tops the list.\r\n\r\nAccording to a study by the Pew Internet & American Life Project, one-third of teens claim to have experienced cyberbullying.  It can take on extreme and dangerous dimensions, such as the case in which an adult masqueraded as a young boy on MySpace to bully a 13-year-old girl. The young girl eventually committed suicide. The mother of one of her classmates will stand trial in Los Angeles on charges related to the incident. She faces a maximum sentence of 20 years in prison.\r\n \r\n\r\nHow can parents and others teach children to experience the positive side of the Internet while avoiding the negative?\r\n\r\nThatâ€™s what Internet safety experts Linda Criddle and Tori Case will discuss on a special panel at CES.  <strong>â€œInternet Safety Without the Scare Tacticsâ€ will be held on January 9th</strong></blockquote>\r\n\r\nAnd what were the previous three paragraphs?','Without the scare tactics? Really?',0,'','publish','open','open','','without-the-scare-tactics-really','','','2008-11-13 13:32:20','2008-11-13 20:32:20','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1841',0,'post','',0),
	(1842,1,'2008-12-04 17:32:17','2008-12-05 00:32:17','I recently bought a Zune pass, which is a pretty sweet deal, and I think it\'s one of the few things Microsoft is really kicking ass at right now ($15 per month, unlimited music access, plus you keep ten songs per month). \r\n\r\nThis is a great solution for home stereo setups because I can just attach a computer to whatever system I want playing music. Right now I have the media center hooked up to the obvious stereo system, and I have an old laptop that half-runs the Zune software to power the system in my room. \r\n\r\nI also bought the Orb Live iPhone application, which means I can access the songs I download through my iPhone. Better than nothing, but I\'d still rather stream the full Zune library. \r\n\r\nI cannot, however, run Zune on my MacBook Pro. Which makes me very angry. I also can\'t emulated (or I can\'t figure out <em>how</em> to emulate the absolutely heavenly listening experience on Pandora: pick a song or an artist, and the software composes a playlist for you on the fly. \r\n\r\nIf I were running Zune, I\'d tackle those two issues immediately. Building a lightweight Mac client shouldn\'t take much work, and you\'re looking to make money on the music subscriptions, not the PC sales. Plus it will get mac users to buy Zune devices. \r\n\r\nAnd nobody needs to tell you how many people love Pandora. Just copy the idea. ','Suggestions for Zune',0,'','publish','open','open','','suggestions-for-zune','','','2008-12-04 17:32:37','2008-12-05 00:32:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1842',0,'post','',0),
	(1843,1,'2008-12-17 12:36:59','2008-12-17 19:36:59','This week\'s <a href=\"http://www.apple.com/pr/library/2008/12/16macworld.html\">announcement</a> that MacWorld Expo 2009 will be Apple\'s last appearance has sparked a lot of speculation about the future of the event and, of course, Steve Jobs\' health. \r\n\r\nI\'m not one of the people who thinks that Jobs is headed down the tubes, although I know many people find it the most likely explanation. \r\n\r\nTo me, though, it sounds like <a href=\"http://www.macworld.com/article/137596/2008/12/apple_kills_expo_reax.html\">Jason Snell nailed it</a>, talking about when Apple decided to ditch MacWorld Boston:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>At the same time, Apple was toning its product-announcement muscles in other areas. The keynote address at Appleâ€™s once-sleepy developer conference suddenly became a hot ticket. And Apple began making more and more major announcements at Apple-controlled media events, not only at the Apple campus, but at Moscone West in San Francisco, at the Yerba Buena Theater in San Francisco, and even at the California Theater in San Jose.\r\n\r\nThose events were timed by Apple, controlled by Apple, and attended only by Appleâ€™s invited guestsâ€”VIPs, members of the media, analysts, and Apple employees. The public couldnâ€™t get in, and there was no intermediary like IDG World Expo to get in the way. But I think most important was the timingâ€”Apple could announce products when it damn well wanted to, rather than being forced to adhere to a trade-show calendar thatâ€™s usually set years in advance.</blockquote>\r\n\r\n','The end of the Stevenote?',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-end-of-the-stevenote','','','2008-12-17 12:36:59','2008-12-17 19:36:59','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1843',0,'post','',0),
	(1844,1,'2008-12-17 12:39:59','0000-00-00 00:00:00','','Eat Sleep Publish gets snarky',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2008-12-17 12:39:59','2008-12-17 19:39:59','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1844',0,'post','',0),
	(1845,1,'2008-12-23 13:11:42','2008-12-23 20:11:42','I\'m in North Conway, New Hampshire right now, where they\'re apparently all used to this \"snow\" stuff that\'s got Seattle all screwed up right now. \r\n\r\nOn the one hand, it\'s awesome to be out here and see a foot and a half dump from the sky instead of the puny couple of inches that have been dropping in Seattle. My Dad e-mailed me some pictures from around the house, and it looks like a lot of snow to me.\r\n\r\nOn the other hand though, snow is so expected out here that it\'s not anywhere near as much fun. Being in Seattle last Thursday was a bit like going to the opening midnight show of a much-anticipated movie---there\'s a kind of atmosphere to it. People bundle up, take a break from life, and get into it. \r\n\r\nThat said, we certainly don\'t get icicles like this in Seattle:\r\n\r\n<img class=\"frame\" src=\"http://img.photobucket.com/albums/v294/jasonp107/blog-icicle.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"Photobucket\">\r\n','Snow and Ice',0,'','publish','open','open','','snow-and-ice','','','2008-12-23 13:11:42','2008-12-23 20:11:42','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1845',0,'post','',3),
	(1846,1,'2009-01-02 17:16:22','2009-01-03 00:16:22','Of all the \"issues\" that face my generation (I tend to hate generational distinctions, but everyone else loves them), gay rights strikes me as the most drop-dead simple to me. Gay people are (drumroll please...) <em>people</em>, so it strikes me as unsurprising that they want, you know, rights and stuff. \r\n\r\nLike the right to get married. If you haven\'t seen <a href=\"http://www.funnyordie.com/videos/c0cf508ff8/prop-8-the-musical-starring-jack-black-john-c-reilly-and-many-more-from-fod-team-jack-black-craig-robinson-john-c-reilly-and-rashida-jones\">Prop 8: The Musical</a>, it\'s a good thing to check out. Especially since they pointed out the very thing that Massachusetts is figuring out, <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2008/07/16/us/16gay.html?_r=1&th&emc=th\">from the New York Times</a>:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>State officials said they expected a multimillion-dollar benefit in weddings and tourism, especially from people who live in New York. A just-released study commissioned by the State of Massachusetts concludes that in the next three years about 32,200 couples would travel here to get married, creating 330 permanent jobs and adding $111 million to the economy, not including spending by wedding guests and tourist activities the weddings might generate.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nIt makes me a little happy that Massachusetts can make a hundred million bucks just because they live near a bunch of idiots who don\'t believe in equal rights for all people.  ','The easy way to make $100 million',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-easy-way-to-make-100-million','','','2009-01-02 17:16:22','2009-01-03 00:16:22','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1846',0,'post','',1),
	(1847,1,'2009-01-05 15:40:14','0000-00-00 00:00:00','Several months ago, and I\'m not exactly sure when it was, MÃ³nica and I decided to watch the entirety of Star Trek: The Next Generation from the first episode through to the last. Given that I\'m a gigantic geek who keeps company with other gigantic geeks, I felt like I had a huge cultural gap to close, and anyway everyone says it\'s the best Star Trek Series. \r\n\r\nBut we missed an incredible blogportunity in the process: we failed to start a \"watching TNG from start to finish\" blog. Which makes me sad, because it\'s been so fun to watch the characters and, hell, the show itself, develop from season one all the way through season four (we\'re on the second to last disc now), and I\'m sure it will continue all the way through season seven. \r\n\r\nI\'m willing to bet there are nuances to this series that are only really noticed when you watch the show from start to finish, entirely in sequence. And I think they\'d make great blog posts. \r\n\r\nMore importantly, to hear these impressions from someone who is watching Star Trek <em>for the first time</em> (that\'s me), has got to provide an interesting perspective. An entirely different viewpoint from the traditional do-you-know-how-many-episodes-Data-appeared-in Trekkie\'s take. \r\n\r\nAnd so to make up for the failed opportunity, I think I\'ll post my thoughts about TNG episodes here as they occur to me. You\'ll get over it, I promise. ','Lost blogportunity - Star Trek: The Next Generation',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2009-01-05 15:40:14','2009-01-05 22:40:14','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1847',0,'post','',0),
	(1848,1,'2009-01-05 18:49:37','2009-01-06 01:49:37','I read recently that people have more active (read: smarter) brains if they get regular exercise. I believe it because I tend to do a lot of thinking while I work out.\n\nSo it occurs to me that I ought to do more blogging while I work out.\n\nThis blog has suffered the attrition of both <a href=\"http://eatsleeppublish.com\">Eat Sleep Publish</a> and <a href=\"http://Twitter.com/jasonp107\">Twitter</a> over the past year, plus I haven\'t felt like writing as much as I have in the past.\n\nWhich is really fine, because this blog has never been as much about catering to a crowd or a topic as much as it has been about putting my thoughts out there on these thar internets. That other people happen to read it is entirely accidental.\n\nBut over the past few weeks I\'ve been devouring the work of my new second-favorite sci fi author and blogger <a href=\"http://whatever.scalzi.com\">John Scalzi</a> (my favorite being Timothy Zahn), which has had the effect of making me want to spend more time on my middle school hobby: writing sci fi.\n\nAnd so I wrote a three-page sci fi short over my winter vacation that I\'m actually fairly proud of, and that I\'m going to submit to Analog on the off chance thy they want to publish it.\n\nWe\'ll see what happens. I\'m pretty sure you\'ll hear about it if they say yes.','Creativity and treadmills',0,'','publish','open','open','','creativity-and-treadmills','','','2009-01-05 19:03:37','2009-01-06 02:03:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/01/05/creativity-and-treadmills/',0,'post','',0),
	(1849,1,'2009-01-06 15:56:10','2009-01-06 22:56:10','I think my more recent enthusiasm for apple products (as opposed to, say, three years ago) can be attributed in part to a larger trend in consumer electronics: features are becoming commodities. \r\n\r\nPretty much any mp3 player on the market will play music. They\'ll play mp3 files and they\'ll probably play some proprietary format that someone dreamed up in some dark room. So assuming you aren\'t incredibly tied in to one format or another (and most people aren\'t), then how do you decide what music player you get?\r\n\r\nSimple: which one has the interface you like best. \r\n\r\nWhen features are commodities, it\'s time to start making consumer choices based on other criteria, like ease of use. \r\n\r\nThis is why Apple is leaping ahead of other companies in all areas of technology---because suddenly, starting in the past year or so, \"pretty\" actually matters. It\'s a major factor in consumer buying decisions. \r\n\r\nI used to want computers and a network in my house that ran a lot like the Millennium Falcon: only if you have a toolkit handy every time you want to turn something on. And there\'s still a part of me that really enjoys opening things up and tinkering, but increasingly I want <em>elegant</em> solutions. \r\n\r\nIf you\'re in the technology space, it\'s time to start focusing on differentiating your product with design and user interface. Aside from Apple, the only other company I\'ve noticed really going after that angle is Sony. \r\n\r\nIt\'ll be interesting to see if anyone else is following suit this year at CES. ','Features are commodities',0,'','publish','open','open','','features-are-commodities','','','2009-01-06 15:56:10','2009-01-06 22:56:10','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1849',0,'post','',0),
	(1850,1,'2009-01-12 00:03:34','2009-01-12 07:03:34','','p-640-480-f8a11d89-e499-458e-9e8b-804bb6a7ff20.jpeg',0,'','inherit','open','open','','p-640-480-f8a11d89-e499-458e-9e8b-804bb6a7ff20jpeg','','','2009-01-12 00:03:34','2009-01-12 07:03:34','',1851,'http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/01/p-640-480-f8a11d89-e499-458e-9e8b-804bb6a7ff20.jpeg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
	(1851,1,'2009-01-11 23:42:23','2009-01-12 06:42:23','Every year my company, the Parnassus Group, puts on the <a href=\"http://itwontstayinvegas.com\">It Won\'t Stay in Vegas</a> blogger party at CES, and every year it gets a little more cool.\n\nThis year we had over 500 people RSVP (you can see the star-studded list over at the site), and I think at least half of them showed up.\n\nI think everyone enjoyed the Sony gaming lounge--how could you not--and the Lovesac furniture surrounding the consoles provided some really serious lounging cred to the whole outfit.\n\nI know AMD had no shortage of people interested in their new tech, and Sonos provided a really awesome home entertainment system to some lucky bugger.\n\nBut I think I might be the one who came away with the real win: Levar Burton signed my laptop. \n\nYes, that Levar Burton. Yes, my laptop. The incriminating evidence is at the bottom of this post.\n\n<a href=\"http://wilwheaton.typepad.com\">Wil Wheaton</a>, I\'m coming for you next! Even if I have to do it at PAX. I know, that\'s cheating, but STILL.\n\n<p><a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/01/p-640-480-f8a11d89-e499-458e-9e8b-804bb6a7ff20.jpeg\"><img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/01/p-640-480-f8a11d89-e499-458e-9e8b-804bb6a7ff20.jpeg\" alt=\"\" width=\"225\" height=\"300\" class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-364\" /></a></p>','It wont stay in vegas this year, either',0,'','publish','open','open','','it-wont-stay-in-vegas-this-year-either','','','2009-01-11 23:42:23','2009-01-12 06:42:23','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/01/11/it-wont-stay-in-vegas-this-year-either/',0,'post','',3),
	(1852,1,'2009-01-12 18:30:53','2009-01-13 01:30:53','I have a habit of understimating my favorite author, which is probably one of the reasons he <em>is</em> my favorite author. Pick up any book by Timothy Zahn and, if you are a fan of sci-fi, I guarantee you will be pleased. \r\n\r\nHe has a knack for taking ridiculously kooky-sounding concepts and turning them into remarkably engaging novels. The difference between the back of the book and the book is huge. \r\n\r\nI\'ve been putting off one of the only books he wrote that I <em>haven\'t</em> yet read for precisely that reason: by all external accounts, the book just isn\'t something I\'m interested in. It\'s called <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Triplet-Timothy-Zahn/dp/0671653415\">Triplet</a>. The cover is none too promising, either. \r\n\r\nBut crack the book open and sure enough - genius pours out. Just makes you wonder, what if the description on the back of the book sounded <em>good</em>?','Underestimating Timothy Zahn',0,'','publish','open','open','','underestimating-timothy-zahn','','','2009-01-12 18:30:53','2009-01-13 01:30:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1852',0,'post','',0),
	(1853,1,'2009-01-13 16:56:12','2009-01-13 23:56:12','I know I\'m a geek because I get Laptop Envy pretty easily. My boss has a brand new MacBook Pro on the way, and my roommate Jacob has a new ultraportable (or at least he will if he can manage to be home for the scheduled delivery time).\r\n\r\nCouple this with the fact that I just got my laptop signed by Levar Burton...it makes me really want to stow this thing in a display case and use it as an excuse to blow WAY too much money on a new laptop. \r\n\r\nOK, now that I\'ve said it, I think I can go back to ignoring my impulses. Thanks for indulging. ','Laptop Envy',0,'','publish','open','open','','laptop-envy','','','2009-01-13 16:56:12','2009-01-13 23:56:12','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1853',0,'post','',3),
	(1854,1,'2009-01-14 23:05:36','2009-01-15 06:05:36','I\'m pretty sure that\'s actually the log-line from the AT&T commercial for these internet cards. I picked one up (from AT&T) the other day because I got incredibly tired of <em>wanting</em> on all the time but not actually <em>having</em> one. \r\n\r\nThis is perfect timing because it\'s just before I\'m headed for Washington DC and the inauguration. It means I will be totally wired in to my addiction the entire time that I\'m traveling, and it also means that YOU, good reader, will benefit by hearing/seeing more of my blather/artsy photos. \r\n\r\nThe reason I went with the AT&T stick is actually because it\'s the best option, not just because I\'m on AT&T. Sprint is super expensive, and has bandwidth limits. Clearwire is cheap, awesome, and doesn\'t work on the Mac. Verizon is kinda expensive, and has ridiculous bandwidth limits (try 5mb per month on the \"cheap\" plan). \r\n\r\nThe one I got has no bandwidth limit and is the same price as the \"cheap\" Verizon one. Seems like some simple math to me. \r\n\r\nAnyways, I can now get online practically anywhere. In the long run, this is probably going to be a bad thing. ','The internet can\'t hide anymore',0,'','publish','open','open','','the-internet-cant-hide-anymore','','','2009-01-14 23:05:36','2009-01-15 06:05:36','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1854',0,'post','',0),
	(1855,1,'2009-01-15 15:51:47','2009-01-15 22:51:47','I\'ve never been a big fan of following rules that strike me as silly. I got into plenty of trouble in college because I spent almost as much time arguing with my professors about why their formatting rules were bogus as I did actually, you know, doing the work.\r\n\r\nBut that\'s just how I\'m wired. Which is why I\'m actually pretty surprised that in pursuit of one of the items on <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/03/28/my-101-list/\">my 101 list</a> (number 4: publish a short story), I actually wrote, printed out, and mailed my story in to <a href=\"http://www.analogsf.com/0902/issue_02.shtml\">Analog science fiction and fact</a>---complete with a totally pointless self-addressed & stamped envelope.  \r\n\r\nI know, however, that the only kind of news I\'m going to get in that SASE is bad news. Honestly, I included both my phone number and my e-mail on the story\'s cover page, and if you\'re an editor who wants to publish my story, there\'s <em>no way</em> that you\'re going to write me a letter about it. \r\n\r\n<blockquote>Hi Jason,\r\n\r\nThanks for your short story submission. I think it\'s great! I would love to publish it for our industry standard rate of [practically nothing] per word. \r\n\r\nPlease write me back at your earliest convenience,\r\n\r\nSincerely,\r\n   Editor\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\nNo, if you want my story you\'re probably going to pick up the phone and give me a call. Or maybe you\'d e-mail me. So I can only assume that the SASE is part of the submission guidelines to add some extra friction to the process and make sure that the people doing the submitting actually <em>mean business</em> about their work. \r\n\r\nWhich is why I\'m playing by the rules. I <em>mean business</em>, people. I\'ll be expecting that phone call...','I can\'t believe I\'m playing by the rules',0,'','publish','open','open','','i-cant-believe-im-playing-by-the-rules','','\nhttp://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2006/03/28/my-101-list/','2009-01-15 15:51:47','2009-01-15 22:51:47','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1855',0,'post','',0),
	(1856,1,'2009-01-15 21:41:07','2009-01-16 04:41:07','I can\'t stand broken clocks. There\'s one in the workout room at the Belltown Court condo complex in (drumroll) Belltown, and every time I get on the treadmill down there I look at the clock, and, because it\'s \"broken\" at about 6:45, and even better, that\'s usually about the time I make it down there to work out, it means I think it\'s <em>accurate</em>.\r\n\r\nAt which point I begin the longest workout session of my life...until I remember that the effing clock is broken. \r\n\r\nStupid broken clocks. ','Broken clocks',0,'','publish','open','open','','broken-clocks','','','2009-01-15 21:41:07','2009-01-16 04:41:07','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1856',0,'post','',0),
	(1857,1,'2009-01-16 12:43:15','2009-01-16 19:43:15','It\'s really too bad that taking red eye flights on Friday nights are a great way to get to the East Coast on a Saturday without missing any work, because I cannot sleep on planes, and I\'ve been taking a lot of these incredibly painful flights recently. \r\n\r\nOnly once have I slept on a plane, and then I only slept about four or five hours. I was coming back from England (to Seattle), and I\'m frankly a little amazed I even made it on the plane. Here\'s the scenario: My flight left England on Thursday morning (GMT), so I got up early on Wednesday morning to take a trip with friends to <a href=\"http://www.beachyhead.org.uk/about_the_area.html\">Beachy Head</a>, where I had been once before at the beginning of my study abroad program. \r\n\r\nI had an incredibly great day, good pub lunch, went out in the evening to the pubs (as you do), and stayed out until around 5am, at which point I was locked out of my friend\'s flat, where I was then sleeping for my last few days in town. \r\n\r\nSo, locked out, tired, and knowing that I needed to be awake in about two hours so that I could get a ride to the bus stop that would then take me to Heathrow, and knowing that I was not the only person leaving in the morning so people were just bound to come outside sooner or later, I balled up my jacket and laid down on the pavement right outside the door. I tried unsuccessfully to sleep. \r\n\r\nSure enough, someone popped their head out a few hours later, and I went inside to pack my stuff. Got to the bus. Stayed awake long enough to get on the plane. Got into my seat and...woke up somewhere over the Atlantic. \r\n\r\nMy point being, if I\'m going to sleep on a plane, I have to be literally dying of exhaustion, which I will not be <em>tonight</em>, as I board the flight that will take me to Washington DC, and next Tuesday\'s inauguration. ','Sleeping (or rather, not sleeping) on planes',0,'','publish','open','open','','sleeping-or-rather-not-sleeping-on-planes','','','2009-01-16 12:43:15','2009-01-16 19:43:15','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1857',0,'post','',1),
	(1858,1,'2009-01-17 06:22:04','2009-01-17 13:22:04','MÃ³nica and I are waiting the last few minutes before boarding our flight from JFK to Richmond, VA, which will then let us catch a train ride to Washington DC (proper), where we will then call her brother to find out exactly which confusing set of subway trains we need to take in order to finally collapse into a heap and sleep for 14 hours. \r\n\r\nAt least that\'s my plan. True to form I didn\'t catch any real sleep on the flight out of Seattle that left last night...\r\n\r\nThe good news: the free wi-fi at JFK is speedy speedy and I\'ve downloaded a full HD episode of Battlestar Galactica from iTunes, which MÃ³nica and I will watch on the flight. \r\n\r\nThe bad news: or right, there is no bad news. ','So the trip begins',0,'','publish','open','open','','so-the-trip-begins','','','2009-01-17 06:22:27','2009-01-17 13:22:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1858',0,'post','',3),
	(1859,1,'2009-01-18 10:12:09','2009-01-18 17:12:09','I\'m really lazy when it comes to actually <em>using</em> pictures that I\'ve taken. I could go on and on about that but I won\'t. \r\n\r\nInstead I\'m just going to say that on my old laptop I had a little built-in SD-card slot, so that if I wanted to I could carry my tiny little camera around, snap a picture, pop out the SD card, stick it in the laptop, copy it over, and use it in a blog post. \r\n\r\nIt was really REALLY easy. But Macs are \"too cool\" for that sort of convenience. So now I hardly ever have pictures for my blog posts, even when it\'s relevant, because I also have to carry around a card reader and a specialized USB cord, and it\'s just a pain in the ass. \r\n\r\nSo I wish that my MacBook Pro had an SD card reader, too.','Laziness and pictures',0,'','publish','open','open','','laziness-and-pictures','','','2009-01-18 10:12:09','2009-01-18 17:12:09','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1859',0,'post','',2),
	(1860,1,'2009-01-22 15:26:18','2009-01-22 22:26:18','but you probably have. What do you think? \r\n\r\nI get the sense that this year had a bit of a lackluster selection of films to begin with.','I haven\'t taken a close look at the Oscar nominations yet...',0,'','publish','open','open','','i-havent-taken-a-close-look-at-the-oscar-nominations-yet','','','2009-01-22 15:26:18','2009-01-22 22:26:18','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1860',0,'post','',0),
	(1861,1,'2009-01-26 15:32:48','2009-01-26 22:32:48','As anyone who pays attention to my <a href=\"http://twitter.com/jasonp107\">rampant tweeting</a> already knows, while I was finally making the trip back from Washington DC on Thursday, one day and a couple hundred dollars later than we expected, I managed to leave my camera bag on the Amtrak when debarking at Richmond. \r\n\r\nIt took me a grand total of 10 minutes to realize it was missing, but that\'s more than enough time for a train to leave the station (and it did). As a result, I spent last Thursday, Friday, Saturday, and Sunday convinced that I\'d be moving forward in life without my wonderful Canon Rebel XTi, or, for that matter, my photos from the inauguration. \r\n\r\nBut this morning at ass o clock in the morning (OK, 7am PST), I got a call from Amtrak letting me know that...they found my camera. \r\n\r\nI don\'t know which particular brand of good luck I\'m wallowing in at the moment, but that\'s pretty freaking incredible. Looks like Amtrak has some honest employees (or maybe everyone is drunk on Obama-do-good-ness), but one way or other, my camera is now on the way home:\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/01/track-camera.png\" alt=\"\" title=\"track-camera\" width=\"446\" height=\"149\" class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-1864 frame\" />','My camera is on the road again',0,'','publish','open','open','','my-camera-is-on-the-road-again','','','2009-01-26 15:32:48','2009-01-26 22:32:48','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1861',0,'post','',0),
	(1862,1,'2009-01-25 15:44:37','2009-01-25 22:44:37','<img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/01/street_fighter_ii_snes_hadoken.jpg\" alt=\"\" title=\"street_fighter_ii_snes_hadoken\" width=\"455\" height=\"341\" class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-1863 frame\" />\r\n\r\nI remember playing the crap out of Street Fighter II Turbo when the Super Nintendo was the coolest toy in the world. (As a side note, who could have expected the \"Super\" Nintendo after the NES? Imagine putting \"super\" in front of other things, and you\'ll realize how ridiculous it sounds). \r\n\r\nBut I haven\'t play SFII in years, which is probably going to bite me in the ass, because tonight, from 5-8pm, I\'m going to playing with the awesome <a href=\"http://gearlive.com\">GearLive</a> crew in the <a href=\"http://www.xbox.com/en-US/community/calendar/playdate/2009/0125-gearlive.htm\">GearLive Community Playdate</a> against...people who are massively, massively better than me. Especially since I just downloaded the game this afternoon. \r\n\r\nThat\'s right, you are ALL quaking in your boots. Because everyone wears boots.','Preparing for Street Fighter II destruction',0,'','publish','open','open','','preparing-for-street-fighter-ii-destruction','','','2009-01-25 15:44:37','2009-01-25 22:44:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1862',0,'post','',0),
