	(1977,1,'2009-03-20 11:12:30','2009-03-20 18:12:30','Who wants to work at AIG?\r\n\r\nSeriously. The company is nationally (maybe internationally) hated. It\'s 80% owned by the government, you\'ll be the subject of protests all over the nation, and you\'ll get <a href=\"http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/nationworld/2008891913_aigstaff20.html\">death threats</a> just because of who you\'re employer is. \r\n\r\nSounds like a fun job, right? \r\n\r\nHow about this: what if AIG offered you about half as much money as every other firm you have job offers from? And then the government decided to <a href=\"http://www.bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601087&sid=a5JHdEyAaec4&refer=home\">tax your bonuses at 90%</a>?\r\n\r\nSounds like a sweet deal, huh?\r\n\r\nAll of us taxpayers (snide note: not all Americans) now have a stake in AIG, and I for one, want my investment back. Which means <em>I want smart people working at AIG</em>. \r\n\r\nPlus, the people that <em>really</em> deserve blame are already gone (from the Seattle Times article above):\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Pasciucco said nearly all the troublesome sectors of the business — the risky credit derivatives written on mortgage-backed securities — are out of the equation, as are the people who worked on them. That leaves a small number of workers to untangle the remaining trades.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nIt\'s starting to piss me off that we the American public seem to think throwing fit over $165 million is worth putting our $170 billion at additional risk. Because the more we hate AIG, the more of our own money we\'re flushing down the toilet.\r\n\r\nIt\'s kind of like buying a stock and then cheering as it drops off the charts. It\'s against our interest. \r\n\r\n','The American public: Throwing away $170 billion',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','1972-revision-5','','','2009-03-20 11:12:30','2009-03-20 18:12:30','',1972,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/03/20/1972-revision-5/',0,'revision','',0),
	(1978,1,'2009-03-20 11:14:26','2009-03-20 18:14:26','Who wants to work at AIG?\r\n\r\nSeriously. The company is nationally (maybe internationally) hated. It\'s 80% owned by the government, you\'ll be the subject of protests all over the nation, and you\'ll get <a href=\"http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/nationworld/2008891913_aigstaff20.html\">death threats</a> just because of who you\'re employer is. \r\n\r\nSounds like a fun job, right? \r\n\r\nHow about this: what if AIG offered you about half as much money as every other firm you have job offers from? And then the government decided to <a href=\"http://www.bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601087&sid=a5JHdEyAaec4&refer=home\">tax your bonuses at 90%</a>?\r\n\r\nSounds like a sweet deal, huh?\r\n\r\nAll of us taxpayers (snide note: not all Americans) now have a stake in AIG, and I for one, want my investment back. Which means <em>I want smart people working at AIG</em>. \r\n\r\nPlus, the people that <em>really</em> deserve blame are already gone (from the Seattle Times article above):\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Pasciucco said nearly all the troublesome sectors of the business — the risky credit derivatives written on mortgage-backed securities — are out of the equation, as are the people who worked on them. That leaves a small number of workers to untangle the remaining trades.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nIt\'s starting to piss me off that we the American public seem to think throwing fit over $165 million is worth putting our $170 billion at additional risk. Because the more we hate AIG, the more of our own money we\'re flushing down the toilet.\r\n\r\nIt\'s kind of like buying a stock and then cheering as it drops off the charts. It\'s against our interest. \r\n\r\n','The American public: Throwing away $170 billion',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','1972-revision-6','','','2009-03-20 11:14:26','2009-03-20 18:14:26','',1972,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/03/20/1972-revision-6/',0,'revision','',0),
	(1979,1,'2009-03-24 15:46:20','0000-00-00 00:00:00','Between the <a href=\"http://blogbusinesssummit.com\">Blog Business Summit</a>, the <a href=\"http://webcommunityforum.com\">Web Community Forum</a>, <a href=\"http://nonewsisbadnews.org\">No News is Bad News</a>, <a href=\"http://eatsleeppublish.com/the-pitch\">The Pitch</a>, and now <a href=\"http://parnassusgroup.com/twitterconference\">140 | The Twitter Conference</a>, I\'ve spent at least a little bit of time putting together events. \r\n\r\nI\'ll admit right away, if you haven\'t been paying a lot of attention to Twitter, or if you\'re really tuned in to Twitter just as a user, it could be easy to dismiss our conference as <a href=\"http://friendfeed.com/e/bd04bdc1-1bb7-4c78-b453-0ed1b675dd1c/Wow-I-just-got-an-invite-to-a-Twitter-Conference/\">laughable</a> or <a href=\"http://twitter.com/MaryHodder/status/1378837156\">uninteresting</a>. \r\n\r\nAt the same time, I\'m a little surprised about the reaction we\'ve gotten from a few of the people we\'ve invited to the conference:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\ngot invited to \'140 | The Twitter Conf\' Seriously. 2d. WTF? why? this is utterly ridiculous. didn\'t recession squish this type of thng?\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\nMary: I\'m a little hurt that you think our conferences go in the same boat as, say, crooked real-estate brokers. I work really hard on this stuff, and I\'d like to think that if you\'re not interested, you can at least respect the fact that there is something here to be interested in. \r\n\r\nIt\'s probably our fault, frankly, for not communicating well what the conference is about. If you take a look at the <a href=\"http://parnassusgroup.com/twitterconference/sessions/\">session grid</a> as we have it so far, you\'ll see that we\'re focusing on, essentially, two broad topics: developer practices and business practices. \r\n\r\nCall it stupid if you want, but I think there\'s a reason why <a href=\"http://www.atebits.com/software/tweetie/\">Tweetie</a> dominates the reviews for iPhone Twitter clients, and I\'m looking forward to finding out, since <a href=\"http://parnassusgroup.com/twitterconference/speakers/#lorenbrichter\">Loren is speaking at our event</a>. \r\n\r\nThere\'s more to Twitter development than just \"can you put a piece of code together?\" There\'s also, say, \"What are the best ways to monetize my app?\"\r\n\r\nI can also understand why you\'d think that business use of Twitter is a lame theme. It sounds a little lame. But the fact of the matter is that Twitter is a very powerful community building tool, and simply tweeting is just the beginning of the story. \r\n\r\nIf you think that Twitter use in business is so simple it isn\'t worth talking about, then you probably haven\'t thought about how a company might want to experiment: does it make sense to dig into the API yourself? what tools are out there to help brand managers? How can you effectively visualize Twitter data? How do you assign weight to the data you get? \r\n\r\nBelieve it or not, the signups <em>are</em> rolling in quickly. We\'re getting low on the early-bird $199 seats. The community is here...and I hope you\'ll join us. ','No Joke: 140 | The Twitter Conference',0,'','draft','closed','open','','','','','2009-03-24 15:46:20','2009-03-24 22:46:20','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=1979',0,'post','',0),
	(1980,1,'2009-03-24 14:55:06','2009-03-24 21:55:06','Between the <a href=\"http://blogbusinesssummit.com\">Blog Business Summit</a>, the <a href=\"http://webcommunityforum.com\">Web Community Forum</a>, <a href=\"http://nonewsisbadnews.org\">No News is Bad News</a>, and <a href=\"http://eatsleeppublish.com/the-pitch\">The Pitch</a>, and now <a href=\"http://parnassusgroup.com/twitterconference\">140 | The Twitter Conference</a>, I\'ve spent at least a little bit of time putting together events. \n\nI\'ll admit right away, if you haven\'t been paying a lot of attention to Twitter, or if you\'re really tuned in to Twitter just as a user, it could be easy to dismiss our conference as trivial, laughable, or <a href=\"http://twitter.com/MaryHodder/status/1378837156\">uninteresting</a>. ','No Joke: 140 | The Twitter Conference',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','1979-revision','','','2009-03-24 14:55:06','2009-03-24 21:55:06','',1979,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/03/24/1979-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
