	(2083,1,'2009-10-09 17:56:28','0000-00-00 00:00:00','Anyone who is a professional user interface designer knows how hard it is to build a good, usable interface. \r\n\r\nI\'m no design professional, and nothing in this post is likely to be new for anyone who is.  But for the past several months I\'ve been working on a not-super-secret project that will hopefully celebrate a working alpha launch sometime in the next two months, and (as a result) one of the topics that has been devouring my head is usability. \r\n\r\nI think that a lot of poor interfaces are borne of an over-reliance on functionality. In other words, engineers and designers will often decide to include a button, option, or switch, when it <em>could</em> be left out. \r\n\r\nThe theory? The more options a user has, the more they will be able to do, and the more the product does, the happier and more numerous your customers. \r\n\r\nBut there\'s a point at which new options crowd out useful options, and massive <em>functionality</em> can destroy an interface\'s <em>usability</em>. Again - I don\'t think there\'s anything controversial here. In fact, I think that most designers believe they are accomplishing this pretty well. \r\n\r\nThe real difficulty, and the magic of good design, is in understanding exactly where that line is. Knowing which features should be included and which features are better skipped. \r\n\r\nI hope we get it right.','Usability not functionality',0,'','draft','closed','open','','','','','2009-10-09 17:56:28','2009-10-10 00:56:28','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2083',0,'post','',0),
	(2084,1,'2009-10-09 17:56:00','2009-10-10 00:56:00','Anyone who is a professional user interface designer knows how hard it is to build a good, usable interface. \n\nI\'m no design professional, and nothing in this post is likely to be new for anyone who is.  But for the past several months I\'ve been working on a not-super-secret project that will hopefully celebrate a working alpha launch sometime in the next two months, and (as a result) one of the topics that has been devouring my head is usability. \n\nI think that a lot of poor interfaces are borne of an over-reliance on functionality. In other words, engineers and designers will often decide to include a button, option, or switch, when it <em>could</em> be left out. \n\nThe theory? The more options a user has, the more they will be able to do, and the more the product does, the happier and more numerous your customers. \n\nBut there\'s a point at which new options crowd out useful options, and massive <em>functionality</em> can destroy an interface\'s <em>usability</em>. Again - I don\'t think there\'s anything controversial here. In fact, I think that most designers believe they are accomplishing this pretty well. \n\nThe real difficulty, and the magic of good design, is in understanding exactly where that line is. Knowing which features should be included and which features are better skipped. ','Usability not functionality',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2083-revision','','','2009-10-09 17:56:00','2009-10-10 00:56:00','',2083,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/09/2083-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2085,1,'2009-10-09 17:56:23','2009-10-10 00:56:23','Anyone who is a professional user interface designer knows how hard it is to build a good, usable interface. \r\n\r\nI\'m no design professional, and nothing in this post is likely to be new for anyone who is.  But for the past several months I\'ve been working on a not-super-secret project that will hopefully celebrate a working alpha launch sometime in the next two months, and (as a result) one of the topics that has been devouring my head is usability. \r\n\r\nI think that a lot of poor interfaces are borne of an over-reliance on functionality. In other words, engineers and designers will often decide to include a button, option, or switch, when it <em>could</em> be left out. \r\n\r\nThe theory? The more options a user has, the more they will be able to do, and the more the product does, the happier and more numerous your customers. \r\n\r\nBut there\'s a point at which new options crowd out useful options, and massive <em>functionality</em> can destroy an interface\'s <em>usability</em>. Again - I don\'t think there\'s anything controversial here. In fact, I think that most designers believe they are accomplishing this pretty well. \r\n\r\nThe real difficulty, and the magic of good design, is in understanding exactly where that line is. Knowing which features should be included and which features are better skipped. \r\n\r\nI hope we get it right.','Usability not functionality',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2083-revision-2','','','2009-10-09 17:56:23','2009-10-10 00:56:23','',2083,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/09/2083-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2086,1,'2009-10-16 12:05:18','0000-00-00 00:00:00','About a year ago I abandoned by RSS reader for a set of Firefox bookmarks, mostly because I got tired of staring at boring-old Google Reader. I like a little visual spice in my blog reading, and I\'ll be the first to admit there are several good blogs I ignore because they\'re flat out ugly. \n\nJust for fun, here\'s a list of ten blogs with designs that I can only dream about achieving. Bear in mind that I am not, never was, and probably never will be a professional designer. \n\n<h3>5. Copyblogger<h3>\n\n<h3>4. Shawn Blanc<h3>\n\n<h3>3. StopDesign<h3>\n\n<h3>2. <h3>\n\n<h3>1. Daring Fireball</h3>\n\nA staple of good design, Daring Fireball is classic, classy, and edgy at the same time. I love the way it marries the simple (no extraneous shit) with the complete (doesn\'t feel like space is left empty, or unprofessional). \n\nOne of the things I struggle with when building a blog design is in making the site feel \"complete\" or \"professional\" without resorting to an overabundance of images. In this, DF succeeds brilliantly. \n\nUnless you\'re blind, you can probably see the influence of that site on my own. \n\n\n\n','Top Ten: Favorite blog designs',0,'','draft','closed','open','','','','','2009-10-16 12:05:18','2009-10-16 19:05:18','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2086',0,'post','',0),
