	(2092,1,'2009-10-16 12:05:15','2009-10-16 19:05:15','About a year ago I abandoned by RSS reader for a set of Firefox bookmarks, mostly because I got tired of staring at boring-old Google Reader. I like a little visual spice in my blog reading, and I\'ll be the first to admit there are several good blogs I ignore because they\'re flat out ugly. \n\nJust for fun, here\'s a list of ten blogs with designs that I can only dream about achieving. Bear in mind that I am not, never was, and probably never will be a professional designer. \n\n<h3>5. Copyblogger<h3>\n\n<h3>4. Shawn Blanc<h3>\n\n<h3>3. StopDesign<h3>\n\n<h3>2. <h3>\n\n<h3>1. Daring Fireball</h3>\n\nA staple of good design, Daring Fireball is classic, classy, and edgy at the same time. I love the way it marries the simple (no extraneous shit) with the complete (doesn\'t feel like space is left empty, or unprofessional). \n\nOne of the things I struggle with when building a blog design is in making the site feel \"complete\" or \"professional\" without resorting to an overabundance of images. In this, DF succeeds brilliantly. \n\nUnless you\'re blind, you can probably see the influence of that site on my own. \n\n\n\n','Top Ten: Favorite blog designs',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2086-revision-6','','','2009-10-16 12:05:15','2009-10-16 19:05:15','',2086,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/16/2086-revision-6/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2093,1,'2009-10-16 12:28:32','2009-10-16 19:28:32','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://shawnblanc.net/2008/01/marsedit-review/\">MarsEdit: Helping the Personal Publishing Revolution</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"You may not have thought about the fact that when you write a post from your CMS’s ‘Write a Post’ browser interface you are typing into a text field. All the tags are black and blend right in with all the text (also black). There are no shortcut keys for custom tags, and you’re subject to the speed of your internet connection and availability of your server. (Have you ever tried posting an update or edit to an article while on Digg’s homepage?)\"</blockquote>\n\nOn Shawn Blanc\'s recommendation, I am giving MarsEdit a try. If this makes \"linked list\" items like this easy to do, it could kill the momentum on my <a href=\"http://jasonp107.com\">Posterous</a>. \n\n','MarsEdit: Helping the Personal Publishing Revolution – Shawn Blanc',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','marsedit-helping-the-personal-publishing-revolution-%e2%80%93-shawn-blanc','','','2009-10-16 12:28:35','2009-10-16 19:28:35','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2093',0,'post','',0),
	(2094,1,'2009-10-16 12:28:32','2009-10-16 19:28:32','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://shawnblanc.net/2008/01/marsedit-review/\">MarsEdit: Helping the Personal Publishing Revolution</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"You may not have thought about the fact that when you write a post from your CMS’s ‘Write a Post’ browser interface you are typing into a text field. All the tags are black and blend right in with all the text (also black). There are no shortcut keys for custom tags, and you’re subject to the speed of your internet connection and availability of your server. (Have you ever tried posting an update or edit to an article while on Digg’s homepage?)\"</blockquote>\n\nOn Shawn Blanc\'s recommendation, I am giving MarsEdit a try. If this makes \"linked list\" items like this easy to do, it could kill the momentum on my <a href=\"http://jasonp107.com\">Posterous</a>. \n\n','MarsEdit: Helping the Personal Publishing Revolution – Shawn Blanc',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2093-revision','','','2009-10-16 12:28:32','2009-10-16 19:28:32','',2093,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/16/2093-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2095,1,'2009-10-16 12:58:26','2009-10-16 19:58:26','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com/articles/burnout/\">Burnout</a></div>\n\nGreat article on <a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com\">A List Apart</a> about burnout in life and work. One suggestion for combating it is to work when you\'re naturally inclined to work:\n\n<blockquote>\"It’s not unusual for creative types to do their best work at the same time every day. By this I mean that it’s important to follow our own circadian rhythms. Hemingway began writing every morning at dawn and explained his choice this way: ‘There is no one to disturb you and it is cool or cold and you come to work and warm as you write. You read what you have written and, as you always stop when you know what is going to happen next, you go on from there.’\"</blockquote>\n\n','A List Apart: Articles: Burnout',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','a-list-apart-articles-burnout','','','2009-10-16 12:58:59','2009-10-16 19:58:59','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2095',0,'post','',0),
	(2096,1,'2009-10-16 12:58:26','2009-10-16 19:58:26','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com/articles/burnout/\">Burnout</a></div>\n\nGreat article on <a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com\">A List Apart</a> about burnout in life and work. One suggestion for combating it is to work when you\'re naturally inclined to work:\n\n<blockquote>\"It’s not unusual for creative types to do their best work at the same time every day. By this I mean that it’s important to follow our own circadian rhythms. Hemingway began writing every morning at dawn and explained his choice this way: ‘There is no one to disturb you and it is cool or cold and you come to work and warm as you write. You read what you have written and, as you always stop when you know what is going to happen next, you go on from there.’\"</blockquote>\n\n','A List Apart: Articles: Burnout',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2095-revision','','','2009-10-16 12:58:26','2009-10-16 19:58:26','',2095,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/16/2095-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2097,1,'2009-10-16 12:58:29','2009-10-16 19:58:29','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com/articles/burnout/\">Burnout</a></div>\n\nGreat article on <a href=\"http://www.alistapart.com\">A List Apart</a> about burnout in life and work. One suggestion for combating it is to work when you\'re naturally inclined to work:\n\n<blockquote>\"It’s not unusual for creative types to do their best work at the same time every day. By this I mean that it’s important to follow our own circadian rhythms. Hemingway began writing every morning at dawn and explained his choice this way: ‘There is no one to disturb you and it is cool or cold and you come to work and warm as you write. You read what you have written and, as you always stop when you know what is going to happen next, you go on from there.’\"</blockquote>\n\n','A List Apart: Articles: Burnout',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2095-revision-2','','','2009-10-16 12:58:29','2009-10-16 19:58:29','',2095,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/16/2095-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
