	(2111,1,'2009-10-20 17:41:38','2009-10-21 00:41:38','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://2dboy.com/2009/10/19/birthday-sale-results/\">2D Boy: Pay-What-You-Want Birthday Sale Results</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"Steam sales rose 40% relative to the previous week. Our Steam sales tend to fluctuate and it’s not unheard of for there to be a 25% difference from one week to the next (up or down) but the 40% increase came after a week that saw a 25% increase.  It has been several months since we’ve seen this number of sales in a single week on Steam.\"</blockquote>\n\nFascinating example of a pay-what-you-want video game sales promotion having a positive impact on sales in other channels.\n\n','Pay-What-You-Want Birthday Sale Results',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','pay-what-you-want-birthday-sale-results','','\nhttp://2dboy.com/2009/10/19/birthday-sale-results/','2009-10-20 17:41:41','2009-10-21 00:41:41','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2111',0,'post','',0),
	(2112,1,'2009-10-20 17:41:38','2009-10-21 00:41:38','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://2dboy.com/2009/10/19/birthday-sale-results/\">2D Boy: Pay-What-You-Want Birthday Sale Results</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"Steam sales rose 40% relative to the previous week. Our Steam sales tend to fluctuate and it’s not unheard of for there to be a 25% difference from one week to the next (up or down) but the 40% increase came after a week that saw a 25% increase.  It has been several months since we’ve seen this number of sales in a single week on Steam.\"</blockquote>\n\nFascinating example of a pay-what-you-want video game sales promotion having a positive impact on sales in other channels.\n\n','Pay-What-You-Want Birthday Sale Results',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2111-revision','','','2009-10-20 17:41:38','2009-10-21 00:41:38','',2111,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/20/2111-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2113,1,'2009-10-20 18:12:43','2009-10-21 01:12:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://stevenf.tumblr.com/post/218293148/a-couple-people-have-asked-me-to-post-an-update\">Why there are two App stores</a></div>\n\nSteven Frank makes a lot of sense, explaining why there seem to be <a href=\"http://www.marco.org/208454730\">two app stores</a> for the iPhone:\n\n<blockquote>The iPhone/iPod Touch, being available for both Mac and Windows, has a single source of software in the app store. That Mac AND Windows thing is key. The app store is not just a software market for Mac users. This is why it blows the minds of indie Mac developers like myself. It’s because it follows the rules of the general software market, not just the Mac software microcosm that we Mac indies enjoy.</blockquote>\n\nFound via <a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2009/10/20/stevenf-iphone\">Daring Fireball</a>, of course.\n\n','stevenf.com - A couple people have asked me to post an update...',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','stevenf-com-a-couple-people-have-asked-me-to-post-an-update','','','2009-10-20 18:12:45','2009-10-21 01:12:45','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2113',0,'post','',0),
	(2114,1,'2009-10-20 18:12:43','2009-10-21 01:12:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://stevenf.tumblr.com/post/218293148/a-couple-people-have-asked-me-to-post-an-update\">Why there are two App stores</a></div>\n\nSteven Frank makes a lot of sense, explaining why there seem to be <a href=\"http://www.marco.org/208454730\">two app stores</a> for the iPhone:\n\n<blockquote>The iPhone/iPod Touch, being available for both Mac and Windows, has a single source of software in the app store. That Mac AND Windows thing is key. The app store is not just a software market for Mac users. This is why it blows the minds of indie Mac developers like myself. It’s because it follows the rules of the general software market, not just the Mac software microcosm that we Mac indies enjoy.</blockquote>\n\nFound via <a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2009/10/20/stevenf-iphone\">Daring Fireball</a>, of course.\n\n','stevenf.com - A couple people have asked me to post an update...',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2113-revision','','','2009-10-20 18:12:43','2009-10-21 01:12:43','',2113,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/20/2113-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2115,1,'2009-10-20 18:29:25','2009-10-21 01:29:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://gamersnexus.net/component/content/article/47-newsfront1/226-dice-devs-side-with-pcgamers1\">DICE on why Bad Company 2 gives PC gamers extra features</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Now, the reasons the PC platform are getting seemingly “better” features in the game are a result of DICE ignoring the PC user base for the past 3-4 years and this is a homage to the PC platform, and also because it’s a lot of things that PC users simply deserve. </blockquote>\n\nThere are die-hards for PCs and die-hards for Consoles, but in the end, it\'s really about making each version of the game fit the platform that it\'s built for. ','DICE: PC Gamers Get \"The Shaft\" Too Often',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','dice-pc-gamers-get-the-shaft-too-often','','','2009-10-20 18:29:27','2009-10-21 01:29:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2115',0,'post','',0),
	(2116,1,'2009-10-20 18:29:25','2009-10-21 01:29:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://gamersnexus.net/component/content/article/47-newsfront1/226-dice-devs-side-with-pcgamers1\">DICE on why Bad Company 2 gives PC gamers extra features</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Now, the reasons the PC platform are getting seemingly “better” features in the game are a result of DICE ignoring the PC user base for the past 3-4 years and this is a homage to the PC platform, and also because it’s a lot of things that PC users simply deserve. </blockquote>\n\nThere are die-hards for PCs and die-hards for Consoles, but in the end, it\'s really about making each version of the game fit the platform that it\'s built for. ','DICE: PC Gamers Get \"The Shaft\" Too Often',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2115-revision','','','2009-10-20 18:29:25','2009-10-21 01:29:25','',2115,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/20/2115-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2117,1,'2009-10-21 18:37:02','2009-10-22 01:37:02','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.kk.org/thetechnium/archives/2009/06/why_technology.php\">The Technium: Why Technology Can\'t Fulfill</a></div>\n\nAs usual, a fantastic piece from Kevin Kelly on the role of technology in personal fulfillment. Amish are among the happiest people in the US, and yet:\n\n<blockquote>The Amish are a little sensitive about this, but their self reliant lifestyle as it is currently practiced is heavily dependent on the greater technium that surrounds their enclaves. They do not mine the metal they build their mowers from. They do not drill or process the kerosene they use. They don\'t manufacture the solar panels on their roofs.</blockquote>\n\n','The Technium: Why Technology Can\'t Fulfill',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','the-technium-why-technology-cant-fulfill','','','2009-10-21 18:37:05','2009-10-22 01:37:05','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2117',0,'post','',0),
	(2118,1,'2009-10-21 18:37:02','2009-10-22 01:37:02','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.kk.org/thetechnium/archives/2009/06/why_technology.php\">The Technium: Why Technology Can\'t Fulfill</a></div>\n\nAs usual, a fantastic piece from Kevin Kelly on the role of technology in personal fulfillment. Amish are among the happiest people in the US, and yet:\n\n<blockquote>The Amish are a little sensitive about this, but their self reliant lifestyle as it is currently practiced is heavily dependent on the greater technium that surrounds their enclaves. They do not mine the metal they build their mowers from. They do not drill or process the kerosene they use. They don\'t manufacture the solar panels on their roofs.</blockquote>\n\n','The Technium: Why Technology Can\'t Fulfill',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2117-revision','','','2009-10-21 18:37:02','2009-10-22 01:37:02','',2117,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/21/2117-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2119,1,'2009-10-23 13:57:50','2009-10-23 20:57:50','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.mediapost.com/publications/?fa=Articles.showArticle&art_aid=116050\">The Video Game Hype Machine</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>If you recall \"Spore\"\'s promotional campaign, it was similar -- tons of gameplay video, the Creature Creator available months before release, and a huge amount of hype surrounding the game. For Triple-A titles, when it comes to releasing content, the rule is simple: Less isn\'t more, more is more -- often to a parodic extent.\n\nContrast this with the approach movie promotion often takes. From \"Jaws\" to \"Cloverfield,\" movie promotions often try to avoid showing the monster -- often until the theatergoers are in the seats and the lights start to dim. Spoilers are tightly controlled, because the theory goes that if people know who Kaiser Soze is or what happens to Dumbledore, nobody\'s going to pay to see the film. </blockquote>\n\nOf course, game makers try very hard to maintain secrecy around game plot spoilers. What gets circulated endlessly is gameplay footage, and plenty of gameplay mechanics - which really works as pre-game-release <em>training</em> as well. \n\nTrailers serve the same purpose with movies, there\'s just a lot less non-spoiler material to work with. \n\n','The Video Game Hype Machine',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','mediapost-publications-the-video-game-hype-machine-10232009','','','2009-10-23 13:58:24','2009-10-23 20:58:24','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2119',0,'post','',0),
	(2120,1,'2009-10-23 13:57:50','2009-10-23 20:57:50','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.mediapost.com/publications/?fa=Articles.showArticle&art_aid=116050\">MediaPost Publications The Video Game Hype Machine</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>If you recall \"Spore\"\'s promotional campaign, it was similar -- tons of gameplay video, the Creature Creator available months before release, and a huge amount of hype surrounding the game. For Triple-A titles, when it comes to releasing content, the rule is simple: Less isn\'t more, more is more -- often to a parodic extent.\n\nContrast this with the approach movie promotion often takes. From \"Jaws\" to \"Cloverfield,\" movie promotions often try to avoid showing the monster -- often until the theatergoers are in the seats and the lights start to dim. Spoilers are tightly controlled, because the theory goes that if people know who Kaiser Soze is or what happens to Dumbledore, nobody\'s going to pay to see the film. </blockquote>\n\nOf course, game makers try very hard to maintain secrecy around game plot spoilers. What gets circulated endlessly is gameplay footage, and plenty of gameplay mechanics - which really works as pre-game-release <em>training</em> as well. \n\nTrailers serve the same purpose with movies, there\'s just a lot less non-spoiler material to work with. \n\n','MediaPost Publications The Video Game Hype Machine 10/23/2009',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2119-revision','','','2009-10-23 13:57:50','2009-10-23 20:57:50','',2119,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/10/23/2119-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
