	(2192,1,'2010-02-01 14:50:53','2010-02-01 21:50:53','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://furbo.org/2010/01/29/ipad-liberation/\">furbo.org · iPad liberation</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>An important observation that I’ve made with the iPhone is that it’s perfect for “relaxed” computing. I use it while lying in bed, watching TV, waiting for my wife to try on a new outfit, driving through the middle of the desert, and a myriad of other situations where I’m not working.\n\nThere’s an inherent benefit to only doing one thing at a time: the load of worrying about other tasks is lifted. Knowing that there isn’t anything else competing for your attention is quite liberating.\n\n</blockquote>\n\n','furbo.org · iPad liberation',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','furbo-org-%c2%b7-ipad-liberation','','','2010-02-01 14:50:56','2010-02-01 21:50:56','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2192',0,'post','',0),
	(2193,1,'2010-02-01 14:50:53','2010-02-01 21:50:53','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://furbo.org/2010/01/29/ipad-liberation/\">furbo.org · iPad liberation</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>An important observation that I’ve made with the iPhone is that it’s perfect for “relaxed” computing. I use it while lying in bed, watching TV, waiting for my wife to try on a new outfit, driving through the middle of the desert, and a myriad of other situations where I’m not working.\n\nThere’s an inherent benefit to only doing one thing at a time: the load of worrying about other tasks is lifted. Knowing that there isn’t anything else competing for your attention is quite liberating.\n\n</blockquote>\n\n','furbo.org · iPad liberation',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2192-revision','','','2010-02-01 14:50:53','2010-02-01 21:50:53','',2192,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/02/01/2192-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2194,1,'2010-02-01 16:50:25','2010-02-01 23:50:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2010/02/modern-procrastination.html\">Modern Procrastination</a></div>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>I\'d like to posit that for idea workers, misusing Twitter, Facebook and various forms of digital networking are the ultimate expression of procrastination. You can be busy, very busy, forever. The more you do, the longer the queue gets. The bigger your circle, the more connections are available. </blockquote>\r\n\r\nHow could you not quote Seth Godin.','Modern Procrastination',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','seths-blog','','','2010-02-01 16:51:48','2010-02-01 23:51:48','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2194',0,'post','',0),
