	(2233,1,'2010-03-04 12:54:31','2010-03-04 19:54:31','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2010/03/04/facebook-twitter-valuations/\">Report lists Facebook\'s value at $11.5 billion</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>SharesPost, however, is a company that lets owners of shares in private companies sell them to prospective buyers, and they’ve just released an index <a href=\"http://www.bizjournals.com/sanjose/stories/2010/03/01/daily84.html\">detailing valuations</a> of some of the most prominent companies in the social media space.</blockquote>\n\nI find it fascinating (and frankly, bad) that private companies are finding ways to become publicly traded without the restrictions and regulations imposed by the government on pubilc companies. ','Report lists Facebook\'s value at $11.5 billion',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','report-lists-facebooks-value-at-11-5-billion','','\nhttp://mashable.com/2010/03/04/facebook-twitter-valuations/','2010-03-04 12:54:33','2010-03-04 19:54:33','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2233',0,'post','',0),
	(2234,1,'2010-03-04 12:54:31','2010-03-04 19:54:31','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2010/03/04/facebook-twitter-valuations/\">Report lists Facebook\'s value at $11.5 billion</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>SharesPost, however, is a company that lets owners of shares in private companies sell them to prospective buyers, and they’ve just released an index <a href=\"http://www.bizjournals.com/sanjose/stories/2010/03/01/daily84.html\">detailing valuations</a> of some of the most prominent companies in the social media space.</blockquote>\n\nI find it fascinating (and frankly, bad) that private companies are finding ways to become publicly traded without the restrictions and regulations imposed by the government on pubilc companies. ','Report lists Facebook\'s value at $11.5 billion',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2233-revision','','','2010-03-04 12:54:31','2010-03-04 19:54:31','',2233,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/04/2233-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2235,1,'2010-03-04 15:01:44','2010-03-04 22:01:44','<div class=\'posterous_autopost\'><div class=\"posterous_bookmarklet_entry\"> <blockquote class=\"posterous_long_quote\">Doug Lombardi, <a href=\"http://valvesoftware.com\">Valve</a>\'s VP of developer marketing, <a href=\"http://www.macnn.com/articles/10/03/03/teaser.hints.at.portal.team.fortress.2/\">confirmed to MacNewsNetwork</a> that the company is planning to port some of its most popular games to the Mac. And while details remain thin, most in the gaming community view this as a positive step.</blockquote>    <div class=\"posterous_quote_citation\">via <a href=\"http://news.cnet.com/8301-13846_3-10463650-62.html\">news.cnet.com</a></div> <p><img src=\"http://posterous.com/getfile/files.posterous.com/jasonp107/ctsEgBuFeukjhFbjJugHtcqvivdEeBFFdeoApFmAfCiCAvpjkAchxyeenkph/media_httpiicomcomcnw_Cddjg.jpg.scaled500.jpg\" width=\"500\" height=\"333\"/> </p></div>      <p style=\"font-size: 10px;\">  <a href=\"http://posterous.com\">Posted via web</a>   from <a href=\"http://jasonp107.com/valve-games-coming-soon-to-a-mac-near-you-sof\">Jason Preston\'s posterous</a>  </p>  </div>','Valve games coming soon to a Mac near you | Software, Interrupted - CNET News',0,'','publish','closed','open','','valve-games-coming-soon-to-a-mac-near-you-software-interrupted-cnet-news','','','2010-03-04 15:01:44','2010-03-04 22:01:44','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/04/valve-games-coming-soon-to-a-mac-near-you-software-interrupted-cnet-news/',0,'post','',0),
	(2236,1,'2010-03-05 11:42:32','2010-03-05 18:42:32','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.mediapost.com/publications/?fa=Articles.showArticle&art_aid=123774#comments\">Non-games</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"Heavy Rain\" is one of the increasingly popular \"non-games\" like \"Flower\" and \"fl0w\" that set up hoops for the player to jump through as they see fit, but offer no significant win or lose conditions. There is just \"progression.\" </blockquote>\n\n','Non-games',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','non-games','','','2010-03-05 11:42:35','2010-03-05 18:42:35','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2236',0,'post','',0),
	(2237,1,'2010-03-05 11:42:32','2010-03-05 18:42:32','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.mediapost.com/publications/?fa=Articles.showArticle&art_aid=123774#comments\">Non-games</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"Heavy Rain\" is one of the increasingly popular \"non-games\" like \"Flower\" and \"fl0w\" that set up hoops for the player to jump through as they see fit, but offer no significant win or lose conditions. There is just \"progression.\" </blockquote>\n\n','Non-games',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2236-revision','','','2010-03-05 11:42:32','2010-03-05 18:42:32','',2236,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/05/2236-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2238,1,'2010-03-06 12:36:35','2010-03-06 19:36:35','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.businessinsider.com/how-facebook-was-founded-2010-3\">How Facebook Was Founded</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>New information uncovered by Silicon Alley Insider suggests that some of the complaints against Mark Zuckerberg are valid.  It also suggests that, on at least one occasion in 2004, Mark used private login data taken from Facebook\'s servers to break into Facebook members\' private email accounts and read their emails--at best, a gross misuse of private information. Lastly, it suggests that Mark hacked into the competing company\'s systems and changed some user information with the aim of making the site less useful.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s all irrelevant, because none of this is going to hurt Facebook at this stage. Critical mass. ','How Facebook Was Founded',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','how-facebook-was-founded','','','2010-03-06 12:36:37','2010-03-06 19:36:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2238',0,'post','',0),
	(2239,1,'2010-03-06 12:36:35','2010-03-06 19:36:35','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.businessinsider.com/how-facebook-was-founded-2010-3\">How Facebook Was Founded</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>New information uncovered by Silicon Alley Insider suggests that some of the complaints against Mark Zuckerberg are valid.  It also suggests that, on at least one occasion in 2004, Mark used private login data taken from Facebook\'s servers to break into Facebook members\' private email accounts and read their emails--at best, a gross misuse of private information. Lastly, it suggests that Mark hacked into the competing company\'s systems and changed some user information with the aim of making the site less useful.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s all irrelevant, because none of this is going to hurt Facebook at this stage. Critical mass. ','How Facebook Was Founded',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2238-revision','','','2010-03-06 12:36:35','2010-03-06 19:36:35','',2238,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/06/2238-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2240,1,'2010-03-14 08:11:04','2010-03-14 15:11:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://search.twitter.com/search?q=%23processjournalism\"></a>RT @Editer: @Moniguzman is essentially dictating...</div>\n\n<blockquote>Editer: .@Moniguzman is essentially dictating the manual on how to report in real time - story of response to cop shootings. #processjournalism</blockquote>\n\nApparently \"Something is technically wrong\" with my Twitter account, which means I can\'t tweet. Here\'s to seeing if I can tweet via my blog. ','RT @Editer: @Moniguzman is essentially dictating...',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','rt-editer-moniguzman-is-essentially-dictating','','','2010-03-14 08:11:06','2010-03-14 15:11:06','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2240',0,'post','',0),
	(2241,1,'2010-03-14 08:11:04','2010-03-14 15:11:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://search.twitter.com/search?q=%23processjournalism\"></a>RT @Editer: @Moniguzman is essentially dictating...</div>\n\n<blockquote>Editer: .@Moniguzman is essentially dictating the manual on how to report in real time - story of response to cop shootings. #processjournalism</blockquote>\n\nApparently \"Something is technically wrong\" with my Twitter account, which means I can\'t tweet. Here\'s to seeing if I can tweet via my blog. ','RT @Editer: @Moniguzman is essentially dictating...',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2240-revision','','','2010-03-14 08:11:04','2010-03-14 15:11:04','',2240,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/14/2240-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2242,1,'2010-03-19 22:50:13','2010-03-20 05:50:13','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.thenation.com/doc/20100322/galbraith/single\">In Defense of Deficits</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>For ordinary people, public budget deficits, despite their bad reputation, are much better than private loans. Deficits put money in private pockets. Private households get more cash. They own that cash free and clear, and they can spend it as they like. If they wish, they can also convert it into interest-earning government bonds or they can repay their debts. This is called an increase in \"net financial wealth.\" Ordinary people benefit, but there is nothing in it for banks. </blockquote>\n\nFascinating','In Defense of Deficits',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','in-defense-of-deficits','','','2010-03-19 22:50:16','2010-03-20 05:50:16','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2242',0,'post','',0),
	(2243,1,'2010-03-19 22:50:13','2010-03-20 05:50:13','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.thenation.com/doc/20100322/galbraith/single\">In Defense of Deficits</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>For ordinary people, public budget deficits, despite their bad reputation, are much better than private loans. Deficits put money in private pockets. Private households get more cash. They own that cash free and clear, and they can spend it as they like. If they wish, they can also convert it into interest-earning government bonds or they can repay their debts. This is called an increase in \"net financial wealth.\" Ordinary people benefit, but there is nothing in it for banks. </blockquote>\n\nFascinating','In Defense of Deficits',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2242-revision','','','2010-03-19 22:50:13','2010-03-20 05:50:13','',2242,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/19/2242-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2244,1,'2010-03-22 10:33:36','2010-03-22 17:33:36','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/21/opinion/21Benkler.html\">$33 per month</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>IMAGINE that for $33 a month you could buy Internet service twice as fast as what you get from Verizon or Comcast, bundled with digital high-definition television, unlimited long distance and international calling to 70 countries and wireless Internet connectivity for your laptop or smartphone throughout much of the country.\n\nThat\'s what you can buy in France, and similar speeds and prices are available in other countries with competitive markets. </blockquote>','$33 per month',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','33-per-month','','','2010-03-22 10:33:38','2010-03-22 17:33:38','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2244',0,'post','',0),
	(2245,1,'2010-03-22 10:33:36','2010-03-22 17:33:36','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/21/opinion/21Benkler.html\">$33 per month</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>IMAGINE that for $33 a month you could buy Internet service twice as fast as what you get from Verizon or Comcast, bundled with digital high-definition television, unlimited long distance and international calling to 70 countries and wireless Internet connectivity for your laptop or smartphone throughout much of the country.\n\nThat\'s what you can buy in France, and similar speeds and prices are available in other countries with competitive markets. </blockquote>','$33 per month',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2244-revision','','','2010-03-22 10:33:36','2010-03-22 17:33:36','',2244,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/22/2244-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2246,1,'2009-12-13 23:51:12','2009-12-14 06:51:12','This page is basically a rolling list of everything I come across that looks cool and that I think I might want.\r\n\r\nI\'ve decided to bold the things that I know I want, and leave everything else in the \"I wouldn\'t mind having this\" category in normal text.\r\n\r\nSo, (friends and family I assume), here\'s the list of everything I think I might want. Naturally, there\'s nothing I particularly need to have, but lists are always helpful.\r\n\r\nAnyways, here goes:\r\n\r\n<h3>The List</h3>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Feel free to get me another year of any of the following addictions: Zune, Netflix, Cell phone, or PC Gamer...</li>\r\n        <li><strong><a href=\"http://braid-game.com/\">Braid</a> - (Video game)</strong></li>\r\n	<li>Subscription to Fast Company, Wired, Esquire, or GQ</li>\r\n        <li>Down comforter for the bed</li>\r\n        <li>USB CF card reader with no cord, <a href=\"http://www.google.com/products/catalog?hl=en&source=hp&q=usb+cf+card+reader&um=1&ie=UTF-8&cid=5552213667371327134&ei=IeAlS53XGY3UsQPz38ngDg&sa=X&oi=product_catalog_result&ct=image&resnum=2&ved=0CDQQ8gIwAQ#\">like this one</a></li>\r\n        <li>Mixing bowls, <a href=\"http://www.surlatable.com/product/kitchen+%26+bar+tools/cooks+tools/mixing+%26+prep+bowls/glass+grid+bowls%2C+set+of+10.do?sortby=ourPicks\">this kind</a>, but not necessarily a set or from this store.</li>\r\n        <li>Cutting board, <a href=\"http://www.surlatable.com/product/epicurean+cutting+surfaces.do?keyword=cutting+board&sortby=ourPicks\">this kind</a>, maybe 15\"x11\"</li>\r\n	<li>Clothes: Brown or Tan Corduroys, New pair of jeans, brown or blue jacket, surprises... </li>\r\n        <li>iPhone apps: Simplenote, a good Starmap, etc</li>\r\n        <li>Not sure if it will be out for sale yet, but when it is: God of War III</li>\r\n        <li>Blu-Ray copy of <em>Up</em></li>\r\n        <li><strong>Program for the mac called <a href=\"http://www.red-sweater.com/marsedit/\">MarsEdit</a>, a stand-alone blog editor that makes it easier to write blog posts</strong> </li>\r\n	<li>Running shoes (I like asics) - men\'s 10.5</li>\r\n        <li>A standing coat rack, something <a href=\"http://www.coatrackshack.com/steel-wood-coat-rack.htm\">like this</a></li>\r\n	<li>A record player</li>\r\n	<li>A relatively cheap IP-print server</li>\r\n	<li>Good books</li>\r\n	<li>Extra power cable for my MacBook Air</li>\r\n	<li>Can\'t say I\'d complain about a Nintendo Wii</li>\r\n	<li>A cool looking decanter (you know, that thing for scotch with a stopper in the top?)</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Surprises</strong>!</li>\r\n\r\n\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Wishes for the future</h3>\r\n\r\nThat is, things I want, but that it doesn\'t make sense for me to have now:\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>A nice, comfy leather (or, theoretically, not leather, if it\'s comfy enough) couch that\'s just the right size and shape to sleep on all day.</li>\r\n	<li>Houseplants - maybe fake</li>\r\n	<li>Set of wireless speakers that can be connected to a standard amp via speaker cable or headphone jack.</li>\r\n         <li>Queen size mattress and bed frame</li>\r\n\r\n</ul>','Jason\'s Wish List',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','1998-revision-12','','','2009-12-13 23:51:12','2009-12-14 06:51:12','',1998,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2009/12/13/1998-revision-12/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2249,1,'2010-03-31 15:45:22','2010-03-31 22:45:22','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/30/opinion/30brooks.html\">Personal happiness</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>If you have a successful marriage, it doesn’t matter how many professional setbacks you endure, you will be reasonably happy. If you have an unsuccessful marriage, it doesn’t matter how many career triumphs you record, you will remain significantly unfulfilled.</blockquote>\n\n','Personal happiness',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','personal-happiness','','','2010-03-31 15:45:25','2010-03-31 22:45:25','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2249',0,'post','',0),
	(2250,1,'2010-03-31 15:45:22','2010-03-31 22:45:22','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/03/30/opinion/30brooks.html\">Personal happiness</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>If you have a successful marriage, it doesn’t matter how many professional setbacks you endure, you will be reasonably happy. If you have an unsuccessful marriage, it doesn’t matter how many career triumphs you record, you will remain significantly unfulfilled.</blockquote>\n\n','Personal happiness',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2249-revision','','','2010-03-31 15:45:22','2010-03-31 22:45:22','',2249,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/03/31/2249-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2251,1,'2010-04-04 00:56:42','2010-04-04 07:56:42','A few quick thoughts about the iPad (unimaginatively titled) before I go to sleep:\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>It feels like the future. The screen is crazy crisp and gorgeous, the iPad is as responsive as everyone says it is</li>\r\n<li>iPhone apps on the iPad are ok but not great. I\'m going to use a dedicated app pretty much whenever possible.</li>\r\n<li>Netlfix on the iPad is brilliant but basically the web UI</li>\r\n<li>The larger screen really does make the web browsing experience unbelievable. The speed of the processor means that i can basically touch type on the digital widescreen keyboard.</li>\r\n<li>The Wall Street Journal app (you need a WSJ subscription to use it) is one of the most innovative presentations of news I\'ve seen so far.</li>\r\n<li>I wish it had a camera.</li>\r\n<li>It can\'t replace a laptop because there\'s no real file management (i.e. I can\'t upload a picture to this blog post via the web interface)</li>\r\n</ul>','iPad first impressions',0,'','publish','closed','open','','ipad-first-impressions','','','2010-04-04 00:56:42','2010-04-04 07:56:42','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2251',0,'post','',0),
	(2252,1,'2010-04-04 00:50:49','2010-04-04 07:50:49','A few quick thoughts about the iPad (unimaginatively titled) before I go to sleep:\n\n<ul>\n<li>It feels like the future. The screen is crazy crisp and gorgeous, the iPad is as responsive as everyone says it is</li>\n<li>iPhone apps on the iPad are ok but not great. I\'m going to use a dedicated app pretty much whenever possible.</li>\n<li>Netlfix on the iPad is brilliant but basically the web UI</li>\n<li>The larger screen really does make the web browsing experience unbelievable. The speed of the processor means that i can basically touch type on the digital widescreen keyboard.</li>\n<li>The Wall Street Journal app (you need a WSJ subscription to use it) is one of the most innovative presentations of news I\'ve seen so far.</li>\n</ul>','iPad first impressions',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2251-revision','','','2010-04-04 00:50:49','2010-04-04 07:50:49','',2251,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/04/2251-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2253,1,'2010-04-04 00:56:21','2010-04-04 07:56:21','A few quick thoughts about the iPad (unimaginatively titled) before I go to sleep:\n\n<ul>\n<li>It feels like the future. The screen is crazy crisp and gorgeous, the iPad is as responsive as everyone says it is</li>\n<li>iPhone apps on the iPad are ok but not great. I\'m going to use a dedicated app pretty much whenever possible.</li>\n<li>Netlfix on the iPad is brilliant but basically the web UI</li>\n<li>The larger screen really does make the web browsing experience unbelievable. The speed of the processor means that i can basically touch type on the digital widescreen keyboard.</li>\n<li>The Wall Street Journal app (you need a WSJ subscription to use it) is one of the most innovative presentations of news I\'ve seen so far.</li>\n<li>I wish it had a camera.</li>\n<li>It can\'t replace a laptop because there\'s no real file management (i.e. I can\'t upload a picture to this blog post via the web interface)</li>\n</ul>','iPad first impressions',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2251-revision-2','','','2010-04-04 00:56:21','2010-04-04 07:56:21','',2251,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/04/2251-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2254,1,'2010-04-04 00:56:26','2010-04-04 07:56:26','A few quick thoughts about the iPad (unimaginatively titled) before I go to sleep:\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>It feels like the future. The screen is crazy crisp and gorgeous, the iPad is as responsive as everyone says it is</li>\r\n<li>iPhone apps on the iPad are ok but not great. I\'m going to use a dedicated app pretty much whenever possible.</li>\r\n<li>Netlfix on the iPad is brilliant but basically the web UI</li>\r\n<li>The larger screen really does make the web browsing experience unbelievable. The speed of the processor means that i can basically touch type on the digital widescreen keyboard.</li>\r\n<li>The Wall Street Journal app (you need a WSJ subscription to use it) is one of the most innovative presentations of news I\'ve seen so far.</li>\r\n<li>I wish it had a camera.</li>\r\n<li>It can\'t replace a laptop because there\'s no real file management (i.e. I can\'t upload a picture to this blog post via the web interface)</li>\r\n</ul>','iPad first impressions',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2251-revision-3','','','2010-04-04 00:56:26','2010-04-04 07:56:26','',2251,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/04/2251-revision-3/',0,'revision','',0),
