I want, but that it doesn\'t make sense for me to have now:\n\n<ul>\n	<li>A nice, comfy leather (or, theoretically, not leather, if it\'s comfy enough) couch that\'s just the right size and shape to sleep on all day.</li>\n	<li>Houseplants - maybe fake</li>\n	<li>Set of wireless speakers that can be connected to a standard amp via speaker cable or headphone jack.</li>\n         <li>Queen size mattress and bed frame</li>\n\n</ul>','Jason\'s Wish List',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','1998-revision-19','','','2010-04-10 15:06:34','2010-04-10 22:06:34','',1998,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/10/1998-revision-19/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2353,1,'2010-05-31 18:04:17','2010-06-01 01:04:17','Or any other browser with a built in searchbox. I always type in \"google.com\" to the address bar and search from Google\'s own home page. \n\nAnd the <em>reason</em> I go to all this trouble every time I want to search is that it annoys me <em>to no end</em> that the last search string is retained in the searchbox after the search is performed. \n\nWhy in the world do I want everyone and anyone who looks at my browser or computer screen to see my most recent search string? \n\n','I don\'t use the Google Searchbox in Firefox',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','i-dont-use-the-google-searchbox-in-firefox','','','2010-05-31 18:04:19','2010-06-01 01:04:19','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2353',0,'post','',0),
	(2266,1,'2010-04-23 17:44:20','2010-04-24 00:44:20','<div class=\'posterous_autopost\'><div class=\"posterous_bookmarklet_entry\"> <blockquote class=\"posterous_long_quote\">The latest news on the street is that Google is slowly rolling out a much anticipated feature – <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Message_Access_Protocol\">IMAP support</a>. If you point your browser to Gmail <a href=\"http://mail.google.com/support/bin/answer.py?ctx=%67mail&amp;hl=en&amp;answer=75726\">Help documentation outlining the steps necessary to get your mail clients setup for IMAP</a>, you’ll soon realize the the directions are <em>the bare minimum</em>. It is recommended that you follow the directions from start to finish, but the actual configuration does not stop there.</blockquote>    <div class=\"posterous_quote_citation\">via <a href=\"http://5thirtyone.com/archives/862\">5thirtyone.com</a></div> <p>I\'m basically saving this so I can find it later. Having trouble with Google apps for no reason on all my devices. Sigh.</p></div>      <p style=\"font-size: 10px;\">  <a href=\"http://posterous.com\">Posted via web</a>   from <a href=\"http://jasonp107.com/how-to-proper-gmail-imap-for-iphone-and-apple-1\">Jason Preston\'s posterous</a>  </p>  </div>','How-to: Proper Gmail IMAP for iPhone & Apple Mail - Derek Punsalan – 5THIRTYONE',0,'','publish','closed','open','','how-to-proper-gmail-imap-for-iphone-apple-mail-derek-punsalan-%e2%80%93-5thirtyone','','','2010-04-23 17:44:20','2010-04-24 00:44:20','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/23/how-to-proper-gmail-imap-for-iphone-apple-mail-derek-punsalan-%e2%80%93-5thirtyone/',0,'post','',0),
	(2267,1,'2010-04-26 15:55:29','2010-04-26 22:55:29','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.itstrending.com/\">ItsTrending.com</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>A real time feed of the most shared content on Facebook</blockquote>\n\n','ItsTrending.com - A real time feed of the most shared content on Facebook',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','itstrending-com-a-real-time-feed-of-the-most-shared-content-on-facebook','','','2010-04-26 15:55:31','2010-04-26 22:55:31','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2267',0,'post','',0),
	(2268,1,'2010-04-26 15:55:29','2010-04-26 22:55:29','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.itstrending.com/\">ItsTrending.com</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>A real time feed of the most shared content on Facebook</blockquote>\n\n','ItsTrending.com - A real time feed of the most shared content on Facebook',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2267-revision','','','2010-04-26 15:55:29','2010-04-26 22:55:29','',2267,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/26/2267-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2269,1,'2010-04-27 14:46:35','2010-04-27 21:46:35','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2010/04/27/eff\">Daring Fireball Linked List: Playing Coy</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Actually, it’s very apparent, and quite simple. They’re looking at Jason Chen and Gizmodo on felony charges for buying stolen property. This police raid was not about what Gizmodo published.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s my understanding that had they not *bought* the iPhone prototype, there would be no criminal charges. The thief is an idiot for asking to be paid, and Gizmodo is doubly dumb for not asking their legal department before agreeing. \n','Daring Fireball Linked List: Playing Coy',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','daring-fireball-linked-list-playing-coy','','','2010-04-27 14:46:38','2010-04-27 21:46:38','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2269',0,'post','',0),
	(2270,1,'2010-04-27 14:46:35','2010-04-27 21:46:35','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2010/04/27/eff\">Daring Fireball Linked List: Playing Coy</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Actually, it’s very apparent, and quite simple. They’re looking at Jason Chen and Gizmodo on felony charges for buying stolen property. This police raid was not about what Gizmodo published.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s my understanding that had they not *bought* the iPhone prototype, there would be no criminal charges. The thief is an idiot for asking to be paid, and Gizmodo is doubly dumb for not asking their legal department before agreeing. \n','Daring Fireball Linked List: Playing Coy',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2269-revision','','','2010-04-27 14:46:35','2010-04-27 21:46:35','',2269,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/27/2269-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2271,1,'2010-04-27 16:25:52','2010-04-27 23:25:52','\nThis from iCon, the totally unauthorized biography of Steve Jobs written in 2006, about Steve\'s leadership of the original Macintosh team:\n\n<blockquote>\"we all had a joke about Steve. If you wanted to get him to agree to a new idea, something that was a good idea but that he hadn\'t thought of, you told him the idea, and then just let him reject it. A couple of weeks later he would come rushing over to you and tell you how he had just had a great idea and would proceed to tell you the same idea you had told him before.\" As far as Steve was concerned, it was not <em>his</em> idea, so he had no problem with it being incorporated into the design.</blockquote>\n\nThen take <a href=\"http://cheerfulsw.com/2010/ipad-a-staggering-work-of-obvious/\">this</a>:\n\n<blockquote>1998: A revolutionary, lovable Apple PDA with little squareish icons, on-screen keyboard, common icons across the bottom, single-tasking, and the best compact keyboard of the decade, complete with an ungainly but functional fold-out case. The Newton.\n\n2010: A revolutionary, lovable Apple PDA with little squareish icons, on-screen keyboard, common icons across the bottom, single-tasking, and the best compact keyboard of the decade, complete with an ungainly but functional fold-out case. The iPad.</blockquote>\n\nAnd the fact that the Newton, created by John Sculley, was <a href=\"http://catb.org/jargon/html/S/steved.html\">steved</a> when Jobs took over as interim Apple CEO in 1997...\n\n...and it makes you wonder where the iPad came from ;)\n\n\n','iPad: Who\'s idea was it?',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','ipad-whos-idea-was-it','','\nhttp://cheerfulsw.com/2010/ipad-a-staggering-work-of-obvious/','2010-04-27 16:25:56','2010-04-27 23:25:56','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2271',0,'post','',0),
	(2272,1,'2010-04-27 16:25:52','2010-04-27 23:25:52','\nThis from iCon, the totally unauthorized biography of Steve Jobs written in 2006, about Steve\'s leadership of the original Macintosh team:\n\n<blockquote>\"we all had a joke about Steve. If you wanted to get him to agree to a new idea, something that was a good idea but that he hadn\'t thought of, you told him the idea, and then just let him reject it. A couple of weeks later he would come rushing over to you and tell you how he had just had a great idea and would proceed to tell you the same idea you had told him before.\" As far as Steve was concerned, it was not <em>his</em> idea, so he had no problem with it being incorporated into the design.</blockquote>\n\nThen take <a href=\"http://cheerfulsw.com/2010/ipad-a-staggering-work-of-obvious/\">this</a>:\n\n<blockquote>1998: A revolutionary, lovable Apple PDA with little squareish icons, on-screen keyboard, common icons across the bottom, single-tasking, and the best compact keyboard of the decade, complete with an ungainly but functional fold-out case. The Newton.\n\n2010: A revolutionary, lovable Apple PDA with little squareish icons, on-screen keyboard, common icons across the bottom, single-tasking, and the best compact keyboard of the decade, complete with an ungainly but functional fold-out case. The iPad.</blockquote>\n\nAnd the fact that the Newton, created by John Sculley, was <a href=\"http://catb.org/jargon/html/S/steved.html\">steved</a> when Jobs took over as interim Apple CEO in 1997...\n\n...and it makes you wonder where the iPad came from ;)\n\n\n','iPad: Who\'s idea was it?',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2271-revision','','','2010-04-27 16:25:52','2010-04-27 23:25:52','',2271,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/27/2271-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2273,1,'2010-04-28 18:06:43','2010-04-29 01:06:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.slate.com/id/2252141/pagenum/all/#p2\">Broadband access in the United States is even worse than you think</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>We\'d never buy a package of \"up to a dozen eggs\" at the supermarket, so why are broadband providers allowed to systematically promise more than they deliver? </blockquote>\n\nAmen. ','Broadband access in the United States is even worse than you think',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','broadband-access-in-the-united-states-is-even-worse-than-you-think','','','2010-04-28 18:06:46','2010-04-29 01:06:46','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2273',0,'post','',0),
	(2274,1,'2010-04-28 18:06:43','2010-04-29 01:06:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.slate.com/id/2252141/pagenum/all/#p2\">Broadband access in the United States is even worse than you think</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>We\'d never buy a package of \"up to a dozen eggs\" at the supermarket, so why are broadband providers allowed to systematically promise more than they deliver? </blockquote>\n\nAmen. ','Broadband access in the United States is even worse than you think',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2273-revision','','','2010-04-28 18:06:43','2010-04-29 01:06:43','',2273,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/28/2273-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2275,1,'2010-04-29 09:50:03','2010-04-29 16:50:03','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.apple.com/hotnews/thoughts-on-flash/\">Thoughts on Flash</a></div>\n\nSteve Jobs trashes Flash and Adobe:\n\n<blockquote>The third party may not adopt enhancements from one platform unless they are available on all of their supported platforms. Hence developers only have access to the lowest common denominator set of features. Again, we cannot accept an outcome where developers are blocked from using our innovations and enhancements because they are not available on our competitor’s platforms.</blockquote>\n\nPretty much everything outlined in this post has been covered already by bloggers. Makes you wonder if Jobs didn\'t just wait for the rest of the internet to give him his arguments - heh.','Thoughts on Flash',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','thoughts-on-flash','','','2010-04-29 09:50:05','2010-04-29 16:50:05','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2275',0,'post','',0),
	(2276,1,'2010-04-29 09:50:03','2010-04-29 16:50:03','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.apple.com/hotnews/thoughts-on-flash/\">Thoughts on Flash</a></div>\n\nSteve Jobs trashes Flash and Adobe:\n\n<blockquote>The third party may not adopt enhancements from one platform unless they are available on all of their supported platforms. Hence developers only have access to the lowest common denominator set of features. Again, we cannot accept an outcome where developers are blocked from using our innovations and enhancements because they are not available on our competitor’s platforms.</blockquote>\n\nPretty much everything outlined in this post has been covered already by bloggers. Makes you wonder if Jobs didn\'t just wait for the rest of the internet to give him his arguments - heh.','Thoughts on Flash',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2275-revision','','','2010-04-29 09:50:03','2010-04-29 16:50:03','',2275,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/29/2275-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2277,1,'2010-04-29 15:10:09','2010-04-29 22:10:09','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2010/04/the-coming-meltdown-in-higher-education-as-seen-by-a-marketer.html\">The coming melt-down in higher education</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Back before the digital revolution, access to information was an issue. The size of the library mattered. One reason to go to college was to get access. Today, that access is worth a lot less. The valuable things people take away from college are interactions with great minds (usually professors who actually teach and actually care) and non-class activities that shape them as people.</blockquote>\n\nI had a fantastic time in College, and it was where I grew up socially. I think the experience is beyond useful for personal growth, but Seth\'s right, the cost of education is unsustainably high. \n\n','Seth\'s Blog: The coming melt-down in higher education (as seen by a marketer)',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','seths-blog-the-coming-melt-down-in-higher-education-as-seen-by-a-marketer','','','2010-04-29 15:10:12','2010-04-29 22:10:12','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2277',0,'post','',0),
	(2278,1,'2010-04-29 15:10:09','2010-04-29 22:10:09','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://sethgodin.typepad.com/seths_blog/2010/04/the-coming-meltdown-in-higher-education-as-seen-by-a-marketer.html\">The coming melt-down in higher education</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Back before the digital revolution, access to information was an issue. The size of the library mattered. One reason to go to college was to get access. Today, that access is worth a lot less. The valuable things people take away from college are interactions with great minds (usually professors who actually teach and actually care) and non-class activities that shape them as people.</blockquote>\n\nI had a fantastic time in College, and it was where I grew up socially. I think the experience is beyond useful for personal growth, but Seth\'s right, the cost of education is unsustainably high. \n\n','Seth\'s Blog: The coming melt-down in higher education (as seen by a marketer)',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2277-revision','','','2010-04-29 15:10:09','2010-04-29 22:10:09','',2277,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/29/2277-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2279,1,'2010-04-30 14:12:25','2010-04-30 21:12:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.newsweek.com/id/237110/\">Roger Ebert: Why I Hate 3-D Movies</a></div>\n\nFinally, someone more credible than me AGREES with me that 3D movies are an inferior form:\n\n<blockquote>3-D is a waste of a perfectly good dimension. Hollywood\'s current crazy stampede toward it is suicidal. It adds nothing essential to the moviegoing experience. For some, it is an annoying distraction. For others, it creates nausea and headaches. It is driven largely to sell expensive projection equipment and add a $5 to $7.50 surcharge on already expensive movie tickets.</blockquote>\n\n','Roger Ebert: Why I Hate 3-D Movies',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','roger-ebert-why-i-hate-3-d-movies','','','2010-04-30 14:12:28','2010-04-30 21:12:28','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2279',0,'post','',0),
	(2280,1,'2010-04-30 14:12:25','2010-04-30 21:12:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.newsweek.com/id/237110/\">Roger Ebert: Why I Hate 3-D Movies</a></div>\n\nFinally, someone more credible than me AGREES with me that 3D movies are an inferior form:\n\n<blockquote>3-D is a waste of a perfectly good dimension. Hollywood\'s current crazy stampede toward it is suicidal. It adds nothing essential to the moviegoing experience. For some, it is an annoying distraction. For others, it creates nausea and headaches. It is driven largely to sell expensive projection equipment and add a $5 to $7.50 surcharge on already expensive movie tickets.</blockquote>\n\n','Roger Ebert: Why I Hate 3-D Movies',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2279-revision','','','2010-04-30 14:12:25','2010-04-30 21:12:25','',2279,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/04/30/2279-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2281,1,'2010-05-05 13:13:16','2010-05-05 20:13:16','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.48hrmag.com/\">48 Hour Magazine</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Here\'s how it works: Issue Zero begins May 7th. We\'ll unveil a theme and you\'ll have 24 hours to produce and submit your work. We\'ll take the next 24 to snip, mash and gild it. The end results will be a shiny website and a beautiful glossy paper magazine, delivered right to your old-fashioned mailbox. We promise it will be insane. Better yet, it might even work. </blockquote>\n\nPretty splendid idea. I  might even submit something. ','48 Hour Magazine',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','48-hour-magazine','','','2010-05-05 13:13:19','2010-05-05 20:13:19','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2281',0,'post','',0),
	(2282,1,'2010-05-05 13:13:16','2010-05-05 20:13:16','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.48hrmag.com/\">48 Hour Magazine</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Here\'s how it works: Issue Zero begins May 7th. We\'ll unveil a theme and you\'ll have 24 hours to produce and submit your work. We\'ll take the next 24 to snip, mash and gild it. The end results will be a shiny website and a beautiful glossy paper magazine, delivered right to your old-fashioned mailbox. We promise it will be insane. Better yet, it might even work. </blockquote>\n\nPretty splendid idea. I  might even submit something. ','48 Hour Magazine',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2281-revision','','','2010-05-05 13:13:16','2010-05-05 20:13:16','',2281,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/05/05/2281-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
