	(2395,1,'2010-08-24 11:08:03','2010-08-24 18:08:03','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://asalesguy.com/2010/08/20/dont-confuse-like-with-rights/\">Freedom is for other people too</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Take LIKE out of the freedom discussion and it makes things just a little easier.  I don’t like a lot of things people do, but that doesn’t mean they don’t have the right to do them.\n</blockquote>\n\nThis is one of those issues I tend to get my back up about - both ways. \n\nThere are some people who think everything is a right. Jamba Juice is not a right. \n\nThere are some people who think that rights only apply to things they like. That\'s just incorrect. \n\n\n\n','Freedom is for other people too',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','freedom-is-for-other-people-too','','','2010-08-24 11:08:08','2010-08-24 18:08:08','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2395',0,'post','',0),
	(2396,1,'2010-08-24 11:08:03','2010-08-24 18:08:03','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://asalesguy.com/2010/08/20/dont-confuse-like-with-rights/\">Freedom is for other people too</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Take LIKE out of the freedom discussion and it makes things just a little easier.  I don’t like a lot of things people do, but that doesn’t mean they don’t have the right to do them.\n</blockquote>\n\nThis is one of those issues I tend to get my back up about - both ways. \n\nThere are some people who think everything is a right. Jamba Juice is not a right. \n\nThere are some people who think that rights only apply to things they like. That\'s just incorrect. \n\n\n\n','Freedom is for other people too',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2395-revision','','','2010-08-24 11:08:03','2010-08-24 18:08:03','',2395,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/08/24/2395-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2397,1,'2010-08-25 13:52:47','2010-08-25 20:52:47','When I first started working, at the end of college and into my first year of \"real world\" employment, I was scared of the phone. \n\nEmail and Instant Messaging offered ways to communicate that didn\'t put the same kind of pressure on me; I could check in with people before I gave a response to any given question. I could always put my best foot forward. No grammatical errors. \n\nWhen I had to get a bid from a supplier or figure out details for a mass mailing (we no longer send snail mail brochures for our events, of course), I would always try communicating digitally before, with great reluctance, I would pick up the phone and call someone. \n\nOver time I\'ve grown to love the phone. Now I find myself e-mailing people and asking when they\'re available to talk on the phone or, even better, meet up in person. \n\nEveryone is just a person. It turns out that work can be a lot more fun if you take the trouble to deal with people instead of a checklist. That\'s one of the things I really enjoy about the event business. At the end of the day, my work is really about getting people to have fun. \n\nIt\'s taken me a few years, but I am no longer scared of the phone. How cool.','Scared of the Phone',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','scared-of-the-phone','','','2010-08-25 13:52:50','2010-08-25 20:52:50','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2397',0,'post','',0),
	(2398,1,'2010-08-25 13:52:47','2010-08-25 20:52:47','When I first started working, at the end of college and into my first year of \"real world\" employment, I was scared of the phone. \n\nEmail and Instant Messaging offered ways to communicate that didn\'t put the same kind of pressure on me; I could check in with people before I gave a response to any given question. I could always put my best foot forward. No grammatical errors. \n\nWhen I had to get a bid from a supplier or figure out details for a mass mailing (we no longer send snail mail brochures for our events, of course), I would always try communicating digitally before, with great reluctance, I would pick up the phone and call someone. \n\nOver time I\'ve grown to love the phone. Now I find myself e-mailing people and asking when they\'re available to talk on the phone or, even better, meet up in person. \n\nEveryone is just a person. It turns out that work can be a lot more fun if you take the trouble to deal with people instead of a checklist. That\'s one of the things I really enjoy about the event business. At the end of the day, my work is really about getting people to have fun. \n\nIt\'s taken me a few years, but I am no longer scared of the phone. How cool.','Scared of the Phone',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2397-revision','','','2010-08-25 13:52:47','2010-08-25 20:52:47','',2397,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/08/25/2397-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2399,1,'2010-08-27 13:34:16','2010-08-27 20:34:16','I\'m planning to attend <a href=\"http://seattle.startupdrinks.com\">Seattle Startup Drinks</a> tonight. It\'s been a while since I\'ve had the time to get myself out to many of the shindigs that are happening <em>all over</em> Seattle all the time, and frankly I miss it. \n\nThere are a lot of cool people in Seattle doing a lot of cool things. \n\nOne of the takeaways from running parties of all different sizes over the past few years (for work) is that if you bring the right crowd together, most of the other details don\'t matter as much as you think they do. If you\'re putting a mixer together, the first thing you should do is find a few people that need to attend, and make sure you accommodate them. Any venue will do, any crowd will not. \n\nSeattle is good at pulling a fun crowd together for drinks. I don\'t know if it\'s the weather that makes us all a little unhinged, but it\'s a very vibrant community and I\'m excited to catch up with people tonight. ','Bringing Cool People Together',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','bringing-cool-people-together','','','2010-08-27 13:34:19','2010-08-27 20:34:19','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2399',0,'post','',0),
	(2400,1,'2010-08-27 13:34:16','2010-08-27 20:34:16','I\'m planning to attend <a href=\"http://seattle.startupdrinks.com\">Seattle Startup Drinks</a> tonight. It\'s been a while since I\'ve had the time to get myself out to many of the shindigs that are happening <em>all over</em> Seattle all the time, and frankly I miss it. \n\nThere are a lot of cool people in Seattle doing a lot of cool things. \n\nOne of the takeaways from running parties of all different sizes over the past few years (for work) is that if you bring the right crowd together, most of the other details don\'t matter as much as you think they do. If you\'re putting a mixer together, the first thing you should do is find a few people that need to attend, and make sure you accommodate them. Any venue will do, any crowd will not. \n\nSeattle is good at pulling a fun crowd together for drinks. I don\'t know if it\'s the weather that makes us all a little unhinged, but it\'s a very vibrant community and I\'m excited to catch up with people tonight. ','Bringing Cool People Together',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2399-revision','','','2010-08-27 13:34:16','2010-08-27 20:34:16','',2399,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/08/27/2399-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2456,1,'2010-10-05 11:25:27','2010-10-05 18:25:27','Getting people to add a new (personal) online service to the list of things they already use is one of the hardest things to do. If I\'m already on Twitter, why do I need to use something that lets me see what my friends are doing? If I\'m already on Facebook, why do I need to join a new social network?\r\n\r\nI think the key to Foursquare\'s rapid adoption is that it behaves a lot like a Twitter client, and piggybacks on behavior we have already established.\r\n\r\nWhen I check in on Foursquare, most of the time what I am really doing is Tweeting, and using Foursquare as a way to conveniently meta-tag my tweet with location and friend information. Eventually of course the \"check-in on Foursquare\" behavior will become natural and routine, and they will have entered the space of \"apps we already use.\"\r\n\r\nThat\'s a good way to do it, I think. ','New service adoption',0,'','publish','closed','open','','new-service-adoption','','','2010-10-05 11:25:27','2010-10-05 18:25:27','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2456',0,'post','',0),
	(2402,1,'2010-08-28 18:23:45','2010-08-29 01:23:45','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://chris.pirillo.com/the-future-of-gnomedex/\">The Future of Gnomedex</a></div>\n\nChris Pirillo, on why he\'s leaving Gnomedex behind:\n\n<blockquote>Without a dedicated team of rock star organizers and directors, tasks slip through the cracks – and you often won’t realize this until the event happens. When someone’s attention is diverted to other projects, yours will not receive the attention it requires.</blockquote>\n\nMy <a href=\"http://stevebroback.com\">boss</a> likes to say that running a great event is like running a great restaurant - if you\'ve done it right you make it look easy. Gnomedex always looked easy. \n\nBut beyond that, running events (I <a href=\"http://tweet-house.com\">should know</a>) is a metric buttload of work, and something the size, scale, and quality of Gnomedex is not a weekend project. Thank you Chris, and everyone else involved for so many years, for contributing to the Seattle community as long as you have been. \n','The Future of Gnomedex',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','the-future-of-gnomedex','','\nhttp://chris.pirillo.com/the-future-of-gnomedex/','2010-08-28 20:54:00','2010-08-29 03:54:00','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2402',0,'post','',0),
