	(2418,1,'2010-09-07 16:26:25','2010-09-07 23:26:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://chatterbox.typepad.com/portlandarchitecture/2010/09/return-of-the-hung-far-low-sign.html?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+PortlandArchitecture+%28Portland+Architecture%29&utm_content=Google+Reader\">Hung Far Low</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>For years people have enjoyed how the name seems to euphemistically allude to sizable male endowment. It was (and is) a pop cultural icon of Portland, both for the joke and for its role as the primary visual symbol of the city\'s Old Town/Chinatown neighborhood - even more so than the Chinatown gate on Burnside.</blockquote>\n\nThanks, <a href=\"http://twitter.com/al3x/status/23860659755\">Al3x</a>.\n','Hung Far Low',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','hung-far-low','','','2010-09-07 16:26:28','2010-09-07 23:26:28','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2418',0,'post','',0),
	(2419,1,'2010-09-07 16:26:25','2010-09-07 23:26:25','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://chatterbox.typepad.com/portlandarchitecture/2010/09/return-of-the-hung-far-low-sign.html?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+PortlandArchitecture+%28Portland+Architecture%29&utm_content=Google+Reader\">Hung Far Low</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>For years people have enjoyed how the name seems to euphemistically allude to sizable male endowment. It was (and is) a pop cultural icon of Portland, both for the joke and for its role as the primary visual symbol of the city\'s Old Town/Chinatown neighborhood - even more so than the Chinatown gate on Burnside.</blockquote>\n\nThanks, <a href=\"http://twitter.com/al3x/status/23860659755\">Al3x</a>.\n','Hung Far Low',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2418-revision','','','2010-09-07 16:26:25','2010-09-07 23:26:25','',2418,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/09/07/2418-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2420,1,'2010-09-07 23:02:18','2010-09-08 06:02:18','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.scientificamerican.com/article.cfm?id=are-human-beings-still-ev\">Are human beings still evolving? </a></div>\n\n<blockquote>First of all, humans haven\'t really changed the rules of natural selection. We might think that because we have culture--and with it all kinds of medical interventions and technologies--that we are immune from natural selection, but nature proceeds as usual. </blockquote>\n\n','Are human beings still evolving? ',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','are-human-beings-still-evolving','','','2010-09-07 23:02:21','2010-09-08 06:02:21','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2420',0,'post','',0),
	(2421,1,'2010-09-07 23:02:18','2010-09-08 06:02:18','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.scientificamerican.com/article.cfm?id=are-human-beings-still-ev\">Are human beings still evolving? </a></div>\n\n<blockquote>First of all, humans haven\'t really changed the rules of natural selection. We might think that because we have culture--and with it all kinds of medical interventions and technologies--that we are immune from natural selection, but nature proceeds as usual. </blockquote>\n\n','Are human beings still evolving? ',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2420-revision','','','2010-09-07 23:02:18','2010-09-08 06:02:18','',2420,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/09/07/2420-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2422,1,'2010-09-08 14:51:57','2010-09-08 21:51:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://googleblog.blogspot.com/2010/09/search-now-faster-than-speed-of-type.html\">Google\'s Core Competency</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The user benefits of Google Instant are many—but the primary one is time saved. Our testing has shown that Google Instant saves the average searcher two to five seconds per search. That may not seem like a lot at first, but it adds up. With Google Instant, we estimate that we’ll save our users 11 hours with each passing second!</blockquote>\n\nI\'m floored by Google instant. This is one of those reminders that despite their occasional blunders with social networks and oddball tools, Google is very, very, <em>very</em> good at it\'s core service: providing search results. ','Google\'s Core Competency',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','googles-core-competency','','','2010-09-08 14:52:01','2010-09-08 21:52:01','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2422',0,'post','',0),
	(2423,1,'2010-09-08 14:51:57','2010-09-08 21:51:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://googleblog.blogspot.com/2010/09/search-now-faster-than-speed-of-type.html\">Google\'s Core Competency</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The user benefits of Google Instant are many—but the primary one is time saved. Our testing has shown that Google Instant saves the average searcher two to five seconds per search. That may not seem like a lot at first, but it adds up. With Google Instant, we estimate that we’ll save our users 11 hours with each passing second!</blockquote>\n\nI\'m floored by Google instant. This is one of those reminders that despite their occasional blunders with social networks and oddball tools, Google is very, very, <em>very</em> good at it\'s core service: providing search results. ','Google\'s Core Competency',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2422-revision','','','2010-09-08 14:51:57','2010-09-08 21:51:57','',2422,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/09/08/2422-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
