,0),
	(2455,1,'2010-09-29 10:10:24','2010-09-29 17:10:24','How many inventions are predicted by science fiction?\n\nThree of my favorites are:\n\nSliding doors (every sci-fi movie)\nMp3 players (star trek, notably)\niPhones (typically \"Datapads\")\n\nAnother interesting statistic I\'d love to know: how many founders of tech startups are avid sci-fi fans?','Science fiction future',0,'','publish','closed','open','','science-fiction-future','','','2010-09-29 10:10:24','2010-09-29 17:10:24','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/09/29/science-fiction-future/',0,'post','',0),
	(2457,1,'2010-10-05 11:24:34','2010-10-05 18:24:34','Getting people to add a new (personal) online service to the list of things they already use is one of the hardest things to do. If I\'m already on Twitter, why do I need to use something that lets me see what my friends are doing? If I\'m already on Facebook, why do I need to join a new social network?\n\nI think the key to Foursquare\'s rapid adoption is that it behaves a lot like a Twitter client, and piggybacks on behavior we have already established.\n\nWhen I check in on Foursquare, most of the time what I am really doing is Tweeting, and using Foursquare as a way to conveniently meta-tag my tweet with location and friend information. ','New service adoption',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2456-revision','','','2010-10-05 11:24:34','2010-10-05 18:24:34','',2456,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/10/05/2456-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2458,1,'2010-10-07 15:40:34','2010-10-07 22:40:34','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.cinchcast.com/\">Cinch</a></div>\n\nInteresting \"micro-podcasting\" tool from BlogTalk Radio. I just downloaded the iPhone app, maybe I\'ll actually find a use for this.\n','Cinch',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','cinch','','','2010-10-07 15:40:37','2010-10-07 22:40:37','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2458',0,'post','',0),
	(2459,1,'2010-10-07 15:40:34','2010-10-07 22:40:34','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.cinchcast.com/\">Cinch</a></div>\n\nInteresting \"micro-podcasting\" tool from BlogTalk Radio. I just downloaded the iPhone app, maybe I\'ll actually find a use for this.\n','Cinch',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2458-revision','','','2010-10-07 15:40:34','2010-10-07 22:40:34','',2458,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/10/07/2458-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2460,1,'2010-10-08 11:12:27','2010-10-08 18:12:27','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://weblog.muledesign.com/2010/10/the_chokehold_of_calendars.php\">The Chokehold of Calendars</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Let’s start with the premise that you have a 40 hour week. (If you just started crying you need a new job.) That’s 40 hours of time to do your job. Now look at your calendar. If your job is to spend a very large part of those 40 hours in meetings scheduled for you by other people then you’re fine. If your job is to produce things such as code, comps, analyses, flow documents, etc., then why isn’t the time to do THAT on your calendar?</blockquote>\n\nVERY interesting perspective on calendars. I don\'t think it\'s exactly zero-sum of course (meetings, calls, interruptions ARE technically work). \n\nYes there are (plenty of) pointless meetings, but in my experience at a four-person office where you usually need more than one person to make a major decision, \"meetings\" can be some of our most productive time. ','The Chokehold of Calendars',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','the-chokehold-of-calendars','','','2010-10-08 11:12:30','2010-10-08 18:12:30','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2460',0,'post','',0),
	(2461,1,'2010-10-08 11:12:27','2010-10-08 18:12:27','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://weblog.muledesign.com/2010/10/the_chokehold_of_calendars.php\">The Chokehold of Calendars</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Let’s start with the premise that you have a 40 hour week. (If you just started crying you need a new job.) That’s 40 hours of time to do your job. Now look at your calendar. If your job is to spend a very large part of those 40 hours in meetings scheduled for you by other people then you’re fine. If your job is to produce things such as code, comps, analyses, flow documents, etc., then why isn’t the time to do THAT on your calendar?</blockquote>\n\nVERY interesting perspective on calendars. I don\'t think it\'s exactly zero-sum of course (meetings, calls, interruptions ARE technically work). \n\nYes there are (plenty of) pointless meetings, but in my experience at a four-person office where you usually need more than one person to make a major decision, \"meetings\" can be some of our most productive time. ','The Chokehold of Calendars',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2460-revision','','','2010-10-08 11:12:27','2010-10-08 18:12:27','',2460,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/10/08/2460-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2545,1,'2011-05-13 17:00:49','0000-00-00 00:00:00','test title!','Test title',0,'','draft','open','closed','','','','','2011-05-13 17:00:49','2011-05-14 00:00:49','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2545',0,'post','',0),
	(2463,1,'2010-10-11 10:26:51','2010-10-11 17:26:51','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424052748704127904575544302659920236.html\">Social Design</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"It\'s so easy to go try and find an algorithmic solution to a problem,\" he says. \"But this isn\'t about computers—it\'s about people.\" At an event last week, Mr. Cox gave a name to Facebook\'s preference for developing social, rather than technological, solutions: \"social design.\"</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s very smart of Facebook to recognize the advantages of harnessing human behavior instead of trying to imitate it computationally. The classic example in my mind is Amazon\'s \"other people who bought this also bought...\" list. It turns out that emergent purchasing behavior is more useful than algorithmic recommendations. ','Social Design',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','social-design','','','2010-10-11 10:26:54','2010-10-11 17:26:54','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2463',0,'post','',0),
	(2464,1,'2010-10-11 10:26:51','2010-10-11 17:26:51','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424052748704127904575544302659920236.html\">Social Design</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>\"It\'s so easy to go try and find an algorithmic solution to a problem,\" he says. \"But this isn\'t about computers—it\'s about people.\" At an event last week, Mr. Cox gave a name to Facebook\'s preference for developing social, rather than technological, solutions: \"social design.\"</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s very smart of Facebook to recognize the advantages of harnessing human behavior instead of trying to imitate it computationally. The classic example in my mind is Amazon\'s \"other people who bought this also bought...\" list. It turns out that emergent purchasing behavior is more useful than algorithmic recommendations. ','Social Design',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2463-revision','','','2010-10-11 10:26:51','2010-10-11 17:26:51','',2463,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/10/11/2463-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2465,1,'2010-10-11 12:00:29','2010-10-11 19:00:29','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/10/windows_phone_devices_details.html\">Windows Phone 7</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>In the U.S., a total of five Windows Phone devices will be available in the U.S. this holiday season from AT&T and T-Mobile, according to a list released by the company this morning. The first of them will debut at AT&T starting Nov. 8. In Europe, the first of the new Windows Phones will be released on Oct. 21, from carriers including Deutsche Telekom, Orange and Vodafone.</blockquote>\n\nI got a chance to play with a few Windows Phone 7 devices a few weeks ago, and they were very responsive, cool phones. Unlike the iPhone, hardware will not be consistent across all devices, but the Xbox Live integration is very cool, and the UI is totally unique. They keyboard was fantastic to use. \n\nI will not be surprised if Windows Phone 7 devices sell very well. ','Windows Phone 7',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','windows-phone-7','','','2010-10-11 12:00:32','2010-10-11 19:00:32','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2465',0,'post','',0),
	(2466,1,'2010-10-11 12:00:29','2010-10-11 19:00:29','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/10/windows_phone_devices_details.html\">Windows Phone 7</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>In the U.S., a total of five Windows Phone devices will be available in the U.S. this holiday season from AT&T and T-Mobile, according to a list released by the company this morning. The first of them will debut at AT&T starting Nov. 8. In Europe, the first of the new Windows Phones will be released on Oct. 21, from carriers including Deutsche Telekom, Orange and Vodafone.</blockquote>\n\nI got a chance to play with a few Windows Phone 7 devices a few weeks ago, and they were very responsive, cool phones. Unlike the iPhone, hardware will not be consistent across all devices, but the Xbox Live integration is very cool, and the UI is totally unique. They keyboard was fantastic to use. \n\nI will not be surprised if Windows Phone 7 devices sell very well. ','Windows Phone 7',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2465-revision','','','2010-10-11 12:00:29','2010-10-11 19:00:29','',2465,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/10/11/2465-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2467,1,'2010-10-12 15:55:47','2010-10-12 22:55:47','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://pinterest.com/enid/want-wear/\"><em>Pin</em>terest</a></div>\n\nThis is interesting. \"Pin\" things that are cool. Kind of like product bookmarking. \n\n','Pinterest',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','pinterest','','','2010-10-12 15:55:49','2010-10-12 22:55:49','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2467',0,'post','',0),
