	(2478,1,'2010-11-04 11:53:04','2010-11-04 18:53:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/11/everyone-is-using-foursquare-and.html\">Survey about foursquare use conducted via...landlines?</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The Pew Internet Project discovered through a recent landline and cellular phone survey that just four percent of online adults are using location-based services.</blockquote>\n\nI have a few preconceptions about the accuracy of this data. ','Survey about foursquare use conducted via...landlines?',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','survey-about-foursquare-use-conducted-via-landlines','','','2010-11-04 11:53:08','2010-11-04 18:53:08','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2478',0,'post','',0),
	(2479,1,'2010-11-04 11:53:04','2010-11-04 18:53:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/11/everyone-is-using-foursquare-and.html\">Survey about foursquare use conducted via...landlines?</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The Pew Internet Project discovered through a recent landline and cellular phone survey that just four percent of online adults are using location-based services.</blockquote>\n\nI have a few preconceptions about the accuracy of this data. ','Survey about foursquare use conducted via...landlines?',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2478-revision','','','2010-11-04 11:53:04','2010-11-04 18:53:04','',2478,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/04/2478-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2480,1,'2010-11-04 20:33:57','2010-11-05 03:33:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://lexiconblog.com/2010/10/19/26-reasons-brands-work%e2%80%a6or-sometimes-don%e2%80%99t/\">Sound Symbolism</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>That\'s where sound symbolism can come to the rescue. Every letter in the alphabet is associated with one or more sounds that it can make — that\'s sound symbolism. Often, a word\'s semantic meaning far overshadows its sound symbolism…as long as the person encountering it knows what the word means. If the word is foreign, strangely spelled, or completely invented, our brains fall back on what the word sounds like and whether we find that appealing or not.</blockquote>\n\nEssentially, if we don\'t recognize a word, we judge it by what it <em>sounds</em> like it should mean. Can be a very cool branding trick, if you know how to take advantage of it. \n','Sound Symbolism',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','sound-symbolism','','\nhttp://lexiconblog.com/2010/10/19/26-reasons-brands-work%e2%80%a6or-sometimes-don%e2%80%99t/','2010-11-04 20:34:01','2010-11-05 03:34:01','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2480',0,'post','',0),
	(2481,1,'2010-11-04 20:33:57','2010-11-05 03:33:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://lexiconblog.com/2010/10/19/26-reasons-brands-work%e2%80%a6or-sometimes-don%e2%80%99t/\">Sound Symbolism</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>That\'s where sound symbolism can come to the rescue. Every letter in the alphabet is associated with one or more sounds that it can make — that\'s sound symbolism. Often, a word\'s semantic meaning far overshadows its sound symbolism…as long as the person encountering it knows what the word means. If the word is foreign, strangely spelled, or completely invented, our brains fall back on what the word sounds like and whether we find that appealing or not.</blockquote>\n\nEssentially, if we don\'t recognize a word, we judge it by what it <em>sounds</em> like it should mean. Can be a very cool branding trick, if you know how to take advantage of it. \n','Sound Symbolism',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2480-revision','','','2010-11-04 20:33:57','2010-11-05 03:33:57','',2480,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/04/2480-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2482,1,'2010-11-09 19:37:03','2010-11-10 02:37:03','I\'ve been following the question <a href=\"http://www.quora.com/Social-Networks/What-is-lacking-in-social-networking-now\">What is lacking in Social Networking now?</a> on Quora, which is a fascinating series of thoughts about the current state of social networks, and how people hope that they will evolve over the coming years.\n\nMany of the answers seem to center around the way that current social networks don\'t properly reflect the flexibility of our real-life brains, and the ability that our squishy grey matter has to appropriately categorize every other human we meet, more or less on the fly. It\'s like we have the world\'s most forgiving, flexible, robust, and quickest database in our heads, and it\'s tightly linked to all our sensory inputs. We\'re pretty cool. \n\nI think that this is a real opportunity in social networking - a problem seeking a solution - and many companies will undoubtedly be working hard on creating amorphous, flexible, one-off networks that more accurately reflect the way we live our lives, BUT:\n\nThe future of social networking is going to be much more like RSS. I think that all social mapping becomes more useful the more invisible it gets. I don\'t want to have to sit in front of a web browser to catch up with my friends on the east coast, I don\'t want to log in to Facebook to get my event invitations. \n\nWhat I really want is direct hardware integration with my smart social network. I want event invitations to appear directly on my calendar (which is in the cloud) for me to accept or reject. I want my Television to bring up an overlay that my friend wants to chat with me: display the incoming messages on the TV, let me type my responses on my iPad. \n\nI think the future of social networking will be in making the social network disappear. ','The Future of Social Networks',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','the-future-of-social-networks','','','2010-11-09 19:37:06','2010-11-10 02:37:06','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2482',0,'post','',0),
	(2483,1,'2010-11-09 19:37:03','2010-11-10 02:37:03','I\'ve been following the question <a href=\"http://www.quora.com/Social-Networks/What-is-lacking-in-social-networking-now\">What is lacking in Social Networking now?</a> on Quora, which is a fascinating series of thoughts about the current state of social networks, and how people hope that they will evolve over the coming years.\n\nMany of the answers seem to center around the way that current social networks don\'t properly reflect the flexibility of our real-life brains, and the ability that our squishy grey matter has to appropriately categorize every other human we meet, more or less on the fly. It\'s like we have the world\'s most forgiving, flexible, robust, and quickest database in our heads, and it\'s tightly linked to all our sensory inputs. We\'re pretty cool. \n\nI think that this is a real opportunity in social networking - a problem seeking a solution - and many companies will undoubtedly be working hard on creating amorphous, flexible, one-off networks that more accurately reflect the way we live our lives, BUT:\n\nThe future of social networking is going to be much more like RSS. I think that all social mapping becomes more useful the more invisible it gets. I don\'t want to have to sit in front of a web browser to catch up with my friends on the east coast, I don\'t want to log in to Facebook to get my event invitations. \n\nWhat I really want is direct hardware integration with my smart social network. I want event invitations to appear directly on my calendar (which is in the cloud) for me to accept or reject. I want my Television to bring up an overlay that my friend wants to chat with me: display the incoming messages on the TV, let me type my responses on my iPad. \n\nI think the future of social networking will be in making the social network disappear. ','The Future of Social Networks',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2482-revision','','','2010-11-09 19:37:03','2010-11-10 02:37:03','',2482,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/09/2482-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2484,1,'2010-11-10 13:45:57','2010-11-10 20:45:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blog.calbucci.com/2010/11/googles-10-raise-is-sign-good-times-are.html\">Microsoft, Google, and Brain Drain</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>I always thought that Google was a lot like Microsoft in many ways, except they started 23 years later, but they are running twice as fast, which means by 2020 both companies will have exactly the same age.</blockquote>\n\n','Microsoft, Google, and Brain Drain',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','microsoft-google-and-brain-drain','','','2010-11-10 13:46:01','2010-11-10 20:46:01','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2484',0,'post','',0),
	(2485,1,'2010-11-10 13:45:57','2010-11-10 20:45:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blog.calbucci.com/2010/11/googles-10-raise-is-sign-good-times-are.html\">Microsoft, Google, and Brain Drain</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>I always thought that Google was a lot like Microsoft in many ways, except they started 23 years later, but they are running twice as fast, which means by 2020 both companies will have exactly the same age.</blockquote>\n\n','Microsoft, Google, and Brain Drain',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2484-revision','','','2010-11-10 13:45:57','2010-11-10 20:45:57','',2484,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/10/2484-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2486,1,'2010-11-11 13:10:38','2010-11-11 20:10:38','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://sxsw.com/node/5864\">Xbox Live is the new cable</a></div>\n\nThis from the announcement about Felicia Day keynoting at SXSW 2011:\n\n<blockquote>In a ground-breaking deal, \"The Guild\" partnered with Xbox to produce and release seasons 2, 3 and 4 on Xbox Live, while allowing Day to retain the intellectual property rights to her show.</blockquote>\n\nI finally realized: Xbox Live is the future of cable. It\'s a closed-platform (i.e. easy to develop for), IP-based, interactive link and distribution system directly to millions of people\'s television sets; it allows for two-way communication, and people already have their credit cards tied to these accounts. \n\nContent is automatically time-shiftable, selectable, chat-around-able...this is brilliant. With Kinect in the picture (heh), you can now offer <em>video call in to shows</em> from people\'s own homes. \n\nOh. Em. Gee. \n\nWho wants to start the first TV channel on Xbox Live?\n\n','Xbox Live is the new cable',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','xbox-live-is-the-new-cable','','','2010-11-11 13:10:41','2010-11-11 20:10:41','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2486',0,'post','',0),
	(2487,1,'2010-11-11 13:10:38','2010-11-11 20:10:38','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://sxsw.com/node/5864\">Xbox Live is the new cable</a></div>\n\nThis from the announcement about Felicia Day keynoting at SXSW 2011:\n\n<blockquote>In a ground-breaking deal, \"The Guild\" partnered with Xbox to produce and release seasons 2, 3 and 4 on Xbox Live, while allowing Day to retain the intellectual property rights to her show.</blockquote>\n\nI finally realized: Xbox Live is the future of cable. It\'s a closed-platform (i.e. easy to develop for), IP-based, interactive link and distribution system directly to millions of people\'s television sets; it allows for two-way communication, and people already have their credit cards tied to these accounts. \n\nContent is automatically time-shiftable, selectable, chat-around-able...this is brilliant. With Kinect in the picture (heh), you can now offer <em>video call in to shows</em> from people\'s own homes. \n\nOh. Em. Gee. \n\nWho wants to start the first TV channel on Xbox Live?\n\n','Xbox Live is the new cable',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2486-revision','','','2010-11-11 13:10:38','2010-11-11 20:10:38','',2486,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/11/2486-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2488,1,'2010-11-11 13:27:48','2010-11-11 20:27:48','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backscatter_X-ray#Health_effects\">I am officially requesting pat-downs instead of body scanner at airports</a></div>\n\nThis is from Wikipedia, on the \"backscatter x-ray\" technology being used by new TSA screening procedures:\n\n<blockquote>Fathers exposed to medical diagnostic x-rays are more likely to have infants who contract leukemia, especially if exposure is closer to conception or includes two or more X-rays of the lower gastrointestinal (GI) tract or lower abdomen.[30] In medical radiography the x-ray beam is adjusted to expose only the area of which an image is required, so that generally shielding is applied to the patient to avoid exposing the gonads,[31] whereas in an airport backscatter scan, the testicles of men and boys will be deliberately subjected to the direct beam, and radiation will also reach the ovaries of female subjects. Whilst the overall dose averaged over the entire body is lower in a backscatter X-ray scan than in a typical medical X-ray examination, because of the shielding of the gonads used in medical radiography this in itself does not mean that the dose to the testicles would be less in an airport scan.</blockquote>\n\nI admittedly have an irrational fear of radiation, but honestly, doesn\'t everyone think these new backscatter machines are ridiculous?','I am officially requesting pat-downs instead of body scanner at airports',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','i-am-officially-requesting-pat-downs-instead-of-body-scanner-at-airports','','','2010-11-11 13:27:51','2010-11-11 20:27:51','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2488',0,'post','',0),
	(2489,1,'2010-11-11 13:27:48','2010-11-11 20:27:48','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backscatter_X-ray#Health_effects\">I am officially requesting pat-downs instead of body scanner at airports</a></div>\n\nThis is from Wikipedia, on the \"backscatter x-ray\" technology being used by new TSA screening procedures:\n\n<blockquote>Fathers exposed to medical diagnostic x-rays are more likely to have infants who contract leukemia, especially if exposure is closer to conception or includes two or more X-rays of the lower gastrointestinal (GI) tract or lower abdomen.[30] In medical radiography the x-ray beam is adjusted to expose only the area of which an image is required, so that generally shielding is applied to the patient to avoid exposing the gonads,[31] whereas in an airport backscatter scan, the testicles of men and boys will be deliberately subjected to the direct beam, and radiation will also reach the ovaries of female subjects. Whilst the overall dose averaged over the entire body is lower in a backscatter X-ray scan than in a typical medical X-ray examination, because of the shielding of the gonads used in medical radiography this in itself does not mean that the dose to the testicles would be less in an airport scan.</blockquote>\n\nI admittedly have an irrational fear of radiation, but honestly, doesn\'t everyone think these new backscatter machines are ridiculous?','I am officially requesting pat-downs instead of body scanner at airports',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2488-revision','','','2010-11-11 13:27:48','2010-11-11 20:27:48','',2488,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/11/2488-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2490,1,'2010-11-17 12:35:33','2010-11-17 19:35:33','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/11/google-adds-mobile-editing-voice.html\">Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs</a></div>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>Google this morning announced mobile editing for Google Docs, to be rolled out in the coming days for devices running Android (version 2.2) and Apple iOS (version 3.0 and higher). The feature makes it possible to use a mobile browser to edit documents stored online, and synchronized to a PC web browser.</blockquote>\r\n\r\nFinally.','Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs',0,'','publish','open','closed','','google-adds-mobile-editing-voice-input-for-google-docs','','','2010-11-28 18:31:20','2010-11-29 01:31:20','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2490',0,'post','',0),
	(2491,1,'2010-11-17 12:35:33','2010-11-17 19:35:33','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/11/google-adds-mobile-editing-voice.html\">Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Google this morning announced mobile editing for Google Docs, to be rolled out in the coming days for devices running Android (version 2.2) and Apple iOS (version 3.0 and higher). The feature makes it possible to use a mobile browser to edit documents stored online, and synchronized to a PC web browser.</blockquote>\n\nFinally.','Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs',0,'','inherit','closed','open','','2490-revision','','','2010-11-17 12:35:33','2010-11-17 19:35:33','',2490,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/17/2490-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2492,1,'2010-11-18 22:04:13','2010-11-19 05:04:13','Over the past several years we\'ve had a lot of examples of \"sticky\" web services -- apps that, for some reason or another, manage to draw users in over and over again. Facebook may be the king of all web services with insane, record-breaking time on site numbers and a whopping 78% of its users returning to the site at least once per day.\r\n\r\nTwitter is still experiencing explosive user growth, and commands quite a bit of attention from users who check in to their feed many times a day. If you go for a day without Twitter, do you feel that nagging sense of \"missing out\"? You\'re not alone. \r\n\r\nSomeone intent on building the next great web company might well ask: what is it that makes these sites so sticky? Is there a common set of tactics that expand beyond the throwaway answer \"network effects\"?\r\n\r\nI think there are several core aspects of human behavior, tied to our psychology and our biology, that successful web apps activate. \r\n\r\nSatisfy curiosity\r\n\r\nCall & response\r\n\r\nMake me laugh\r\n\r\nPerpetual newness (toast)\r\n\r\nGossiping ( give / receive )\r\n\r\nImmediacy    ','What makes a web app addictive?',0,'','draft','open','open','','','','','2010-11-28 18:29:59','2010-11-29 01:29:59','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2492',0,'post','',0),
	(2494,1,'2010-11-18 22:04:13','2010-11-19 05:04:13','Over the past several years we\'ve had a lot of examples of \"sticky\" web services -- apps that, for some reason or another, manage to draw users in over and over again. Facebook may be the king of all web services with insane, record-breaking time on site numbers and a whopping 78% of its users returning to the site at least once per day.\n\nTwitter is still experiencing explosive user growth, and commands quite a bit of attention from users who check in to their feed many times a day. If you go for a day without Twitter, do you feel that nagging sense of \"missing out\"? You\'re not alone. \n\nSomeone intent on building the next great web company might well ask: what is it that makes these sites so sticky? Is there a common set of tactics that expand beyond the throwaway answer \"network effects\"?\n\nI think there are several core aspects of human behavior, tied to our psychology and our biology, that successful web apps activate. \n\nSatisfy curiosity\n\nCall & response\n\nMake me laugh\n\nPerpetual newness (toast)\n\nGossiping ( give / receive )\n\nImmediacy    ','What makes a web app addictive?',0,'','inherit','closed','closed','','2492-revision','','','2010-11-18 22:04:13','2010-11-19 05:04:13','',2492,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/18/2492-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2495,1,'2010-11-17 12:35:41','2010-11-17 19:35:41','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://techflash.com/seattle/2010/11/google-adds-mobile-editing-voice.html\">Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Google this morning announced mobile editing for Google Docs, to be rolled out in the coming days for devices running Android (version 2.2) and Apple iOS (version 3.0 and higher). The feature makes it possible to use a mobile browser to edit documents stored online, and synchronized to a PC web browser.</blockquote>\n\nFinally.','Google adds mobile editing, voice input for Google Docs',0,'','inherit','closed','closed','','2490-revision-2','','','2010-11-17 12:35:41','2010-11-17 19:35:41','',2490,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/17/2490-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2496,1,'2010-11-28 18:34:22','2010-11-29 01:34:22','Like a lot of geeks, I\'ve read a lot of sci-fi over the course of my lifetime, and one of the things I\'m starting to get really excited about is that the world is finally getting the <a href=\"http://starwars.wikia.com/wiki/Datapad\">datapad</a>. \n\nLooking back, the personal mobile phone is the device that started it all - people became introduced to, and grew used to:\n\n<ol>\n<li>Carrying a pocket-sized electronic device</li>\n<li>A device that was used for communication</li>\n<li>Belonging to one person individually</li>\n</ol>\n\nWhen you think about it, these are <em>huge</em> changes in every communications and computing paradigm that previously existed. You used to call a <em>location</em> looking for a particular <em>person</em>, and now you call a <em>person</em>...who cares where they are?\n\nCarrying a personalized, individually-owned portable communication device is 90% of what sci-fi calls a datapad. The missing magical element has been effective, wide-scale data connectivity...\n\n...right, internet access. \n\nNow we have all kinds of Android-powered mobile devices, iOS phones and pads, and now smart mobile handsets powered by Windows Phone 7. All of which can magically interact with gaziagabytes of data through cell-tower based data transfer technology, just like magic. We have datapads. \n\nWhat I\'m excited to learn over the next few years:\n\n<ol>\n<li>Will the iPad become primarily a personal device or a family/group access point?</li>\n<li>Assuming that mobile internet access will become the most frequent consumer access point for the internet. How will these interface restrictions redefine the online services we take for granted/consider mature today?</li>\n</ol>','The datapad',0,'','publish','open','closed','','the-datapad','','','2010-11-28 18:36:14','2010-11-29 01:36:14','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2496',0,'post','',0),
