	(2498,1,'2010-11-28 18:34:25','2010-11-29 01:34:25','Like a lot of geeks, I\'ve read a lot of sci-fi over the course of my lifetime, and one of the things I\'m starting to get really excited about is that the world is finally getting the <a href=\"http://starwars.wikia.com/wiki/Datapad\">datapad</a>. \n\nLooking back, the personal mobile phone is the device that started it all - people became introduced to, and grew used to:\n\n<ol>\n<li>Carrying a pocket-sized electronic device</li>\n<li>A device that was used for communication</li>\n<li>Belonging to one person individually</li>\n</ol>\n\nWhen you think about it, these are <em>huge</em> changes in every communications and computing paradigm that previously existed. You used to call a <em>location</em> looking for a particular <em>person</em>, and now you call a <em>person</em>...who cares where they are?\n\nCarrying a personalized, individually-owned portable communication device is 90% of what sci-fi calls a datapad. The missing magical element has been effective, wide-scale data connectivity...\n\n...right, internet access. \n\nNow we have all kinds of Android-powered mobile devices, iOS phones and pads, and now smart mobile handsets powered by Windows Phone 7. All of which can magically interact with gaziagabytes of data through cell-tower based data transfer technology, just like magic. We have datapads. \n\nWhat I\'m excited to learn over the next few years:\n\n<ol>\n<li>Will the iPad become primarily a personal device or a family/group access point?</li>\n<li>Assuming that mobile internet access will become the most frequent consumer access point for the internet. How will these interface restrictions redefine the online services we take for granted/consider mature today?</li>\n</ol>','The datapad',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2496-revision-2','','','2010-11-28 18:34:25','2010-11-29 01:34:25','',2496,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/28/2496-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2500,1,'2010-12-02 13:07:23','2010-12-02 20:07:23','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2010/12/02/gowalla-3-foursquare/\">The phrase you\'re looking for is \"embrace and extend\"</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>First, Gowalla now lets you broadcast your checkins on not only its app, but Twitter, Tumblr, Facebook Places and Foursquare as well. Gowalla even lets you earn Foursquare badges and Facebook Deals through the iPhone app. As an added bonus, Gowalla also pulls in Foursquare Tips whenever you check in.</blockquote>\n\n','The phrase you\'re looking for is \"embrace and extend\"',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','the-phrase-youre-looking-for-is-embrace-and-extend','','\nhttp://mashable.com/2010/12/02/gowalla-3-foursquare/','2010-12-02 13:07:26','2010-12-02 20:07:26','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2500',0,'post','',0),
	(2501,1,'2010-12-02 13:07:23','2010-12-02 20:07:23','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2010/12/02/gowalla-3-foursquare/\">The phrase you\'re looking for is \"embrace and extend\"</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>First, Gowalla now lets you broadcast your checkins on not only its app, but Twitter, Tumblr, Facebook Places and Foursquare as well. Gowalla even lets you earn Foursquare badges and Facebook Deals through the iPhone app. As an added bonus, Gowalla also pulls in Foursquare Tips whenever you check in.</blockquote>\n\n','The phrase you\'re looking for is \"embrace and extend\"',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2500-revision','','','2010-12-02 13:07:23','2010-12-02 20:07:23','',2500,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/12/02/2500-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2503,1,'2010-11-24 12:40:08','2010-11-24 19:40:08','This page is basically a rolling list of everything I come across that looks cool and that I think I might want.\r\n\r\nI\'ve decided to bold the things that I know I want, and leave everything else in the \"I wouldn\'t mind having this\" category in normal text.\r\n\r\nSo, (friends and family I assume), here\'s the list of everything I think I might want. Naturally, there\'s nothing I particularly need to have, but lists are always helpful.\r\n\r\nAnyways, here goes:\r\n\r\n<h3>The List</h3>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Feel free to get me another year of any of the following addictions: Zune, Netflix, Cell phone, or PC Gamer...</li>\r\n	<li>Subscription to Esquire</li>\r\n	<li>Pants: 32x32 non-jeans, non-courds, non-suit pants.</li>\r\n	<li>Sweaters: maybe one with one of those cool new \"cowl-necks\" and one that comes in a rich navy blue?</li>\r\n        <li>USB CF card reader with no cord, <a href=\"http://www.google.com/products/catalog?hl=en&source=hp&q=usb+cf+card+reader&um=1&ie=UTF-8&cid=5552213667371327134&ei=IeAlS53XGY3UsQPz38ngDg&sa=X&oi=product_catalog_result&ct=image&resnum=2&ved=0CDQQ8gIwAQ#\">like this one</a></li>\r\n	<li>A Network-Attached-Storage device, preferably <a href=\"http://www.tigerdirect.com/applications/SearchTools/item-details.asp?EdpNo=3952307&Sku=D700-2364\">this one</a></li>\r\n	<li>A 500GB SATA 3.5\" HDD to go with it...</li>\r\n	<li>Good books</li>\r\n	<li>A cool looking scotch decanter (you know, that thing for scotch with a stopper in the top?)</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Surprises</strong>!</li>\r\n\r\n\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h3>Wishes for the future</h3>\r\n\r\nThat is, things I want, but that it doesn\'t make sense for me to have now:\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>A nice, comfy leather (or, theoretically, not leather, if it\'s comfy enough) couch that\'s just the right size and shape to sleep on all day.</li>\r\n	<li>Houseplants - maybe fake</li>\r\n	<li>Set of wireless speakers that can be connected to a standard amp via speaker cable or headphone jack.</li>\r\n         <li>Queen size mattress and bed frame</li>\r\n\r\n</ul>','Jason\'s Wish List',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','1998-revision-23','','','2010-11-24 12:40:08','2010-11-24 19:40:08','',1998,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2010/11/24/1998-revision-23/',0,'revision','',0),
