	(2518,1,'2011-04-27 14:48:57','2011-04-27 21:48:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blog.delicious.com/\">YouTube Founders Acquire Delicious</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Today, we’re pleased to announce that Delicious has been acquired by the founders of YouTube, Chad Hurley and Steve Chen. As creators of the largest online video platform, they have firsthand experience enabling millions of users to share their experiences with the world. They are committed to running and improving Delicious going forward.</blockquote>\n\nThat was bound to happen; Delicious is too popular and useful to go quietly into the dustbin. John Gruber <a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2011/04/27/delicious\">notes</a> that someone probably doesn\'t have the password to the actual delicious blog - which is why this post has no permalink. #whoops #wefiredeveryone\n\n','YouTube Founders Acquire Delicious',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','youtube-founders-acquire-delicious','','','2011-04-27 14:49:00','2011-04-27 21:49:00','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2518',0,'post','',0),
	(2519,1,'2011-04-27 14:48:57','2011-04-27 21:48:57','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blog.delicious.com/\">YouTube Founders Acquire Delicious</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Today, we’re pleased to announce that Delicious has been acquired by the founders of YouTube, Chad Hurley and Steve Chen. As creators of the largest online video platform, they have firsthand experience enabling millions of users to share their experiences with the world. They are committed to running and improving Delicious going forward.</blockquote>\n\nThat was bound to happen; Delicious is too popular and useful to go quietly into the dustbin. John Gruber <a href=\"http://daringfireball.net/linked/2011/04/27/delicious\">notes</a> that someone probably doesn\'t have the password to the actual delicious blog - which is why this post has no permalink. #whoops #wefiredeveryone\n\n','YouTube Founders Acquire Delicious',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2518-revision','','','2011-04-27 14:48:57','2011-04-27 21:48:57','',2518,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/27/2518-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2520,1,'2011-04-27 15:26:47','2011-04-27 22:26:47','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/digits/2011/04/27/visa-backs-jack-dorseys-square/\">Visa Backs Jack Dorsey’s Square</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Square, the San Francisco start-up that lets anyone accept credit card payments through mobile devices, on Wednesday announced it had received an undisclosed “strategic investment” amount from Visa, the No. 1 credit card company.</blockquote>\n\n<a href=\"http://squareup.com\">Square</a> has been quietly working at changing the way credit card payments work, and in the process, they\'re also going to drastically increase the number of transactions that happen with a credit card instead of cash. \n\nI think this is probably a great investment from VISA.\n\n\n\n','Visa Backs Jack Dorsey’s Square - Digits - WSJ',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','visa-backs-jack-dorsey%e2%80%99s-square-digits-wsj','','','2011-04-27 15:26:50','2011-04-27 22:26:50','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2520',0,'post','',0),
	(2521,1,'2011-04-27 15:26:47','2011-04-27 22:26:47','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/digits/2011/04/27/visa-backs-jack-dorseys-square/\">Visa Backs Jack Dorsey’s Square</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Square, the San Francisco start-up that lets anyone accept credit card payments through mobile devices, on Wednesday announced it had received an undisclosed “strategic investment” amount from Visa, the No. 1 credit card company.</blockquote>\n\n<a href=\"http://squareup.com\">Square</a> has been quietly working at changing the way credit card payments work, and in the process, they\'re also going to drastically increase the number of transactions that happen with a credit card instead of cash. \n\nI think this is probably a great investment from VISA.\n\n\n\n','Visa Backs Jack Dorsey’s Square - Digits - WSJ',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2520-revision','','','2011-04-27 15:26:47','2011-04-27 22:26:47','',2520,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/27/2520-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2522,1,'2011-04-28 15:59:47','2011-04-28 22:59:47','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.avc.com/a_vc/2011/04/finding-and-buying-a-domain-name.html\">A VC: Finding And Buying A Domain Name</a></div>\n\nI\'ve felt for a while that domain names are actually a great investment - they are completely unique (there is only one doctors.com) and they are very limited. \n\nIn a world where literally every business and every startup needs a domain to pin themselves on, this kind of domain price inflation makes sense:\n\n<blockquote>We\'ve noticed the average price of a good domain has risen fairly dramatically in the past year. We used to advise companies to spend $10k or less on a domain, then we upped that recommendation to $25k. We recently upped it again to $50k.</blockquote>\n\nOne of the interesting questions to me is whether a domain name, once used by a failed startup, ever comes back around for use. As an example, will anyone ever use <a href=\"http://kozmo.com/\">Kozmo.com</a> again?\n\n','A VC: Finding And Buying A Domain Name',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','a-vc-finding-and-buying-a-domain-name','','','2011-04-28 15:59:50','2011-04-28 22:59:50','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2522',0,'post','',0),
	(2523,1,'2011-04-28 15:59:47','2011-04-28 22:59:47','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.avc.com/a_vc/2011/04/finding-and-buying-a-domain-name.html\">A VC: Finding And Buying A Domain Name</a></div>\n\nI\'ve felt for a while that domain names are actually a great investment - they are completely unique (there is only one doctors.com) and they are very limited. \n\nIn a world where literally every business and every startup needs a domain to pin themselves on, this kind of domain price inflation makes sense:\n\n<blockquote>We\'ve noticed the average price of a good domain has risen fairly dramatically in the past year. We used to advise companies to spend $10k or less on a domain, then we upped that recommendation to $25k. We recently upped it again to $50k.</blockquote>\n\nOne of the interesting questions to me is whether a domain name, once used by a failed startup, ever comes back around for use. As an example, will anyone ever use <a href=\"http://kozmo.com/\">Kozmo.com</a> again?\n\n','A VC: Finding And Buying A Domain Name',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2522-revision','','','2011-04-28 15:59:47','2011-04-28 22:59:47','',2522,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/28/2522-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2524,1,'2011-04-29 18:27:43','2011-04-30 01:27:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blogs.forbes.com/tomiogeron/2011/04/28/how-like-com-shut-down-a-competitor-and-broke-up-its-funding-round/\">How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding Round</a></div>\n\nIn general, I think the idea of patents is good. Many tech companies complain about patents in the tech space, and I agree that the system we currently have in place often seems to award patents erroneously. \n\nBut, I do think that patents (when properly awarded) can be a critical part of protecting your business, and based on the story about Like.com, I\'m not feeling like this was an abuse of the patent system so much as just a great strategy from Like.com (with a little luck thrown in):\n\n<blockquote>But Like.com had a patent on product applications of computer vision. Like.com, which was well-funded with $47 million in venture backing, filed a lawsuit against Modista the day before its funding was going to close.</blockquote>\n\nInteresting read.','How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','how-like-com-shut-down-a-competitor','','','2011-04-29 20:03:05','2011-04-30 03:03:05','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2524',0,'post','',0),
	(2525,1,'2011-04-29 18:27:43','2011-04-30 01:27:43','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blogs.forbes.com/tomiogeron/2011/04/28/how-like-com-shut-down-a-competitor-and-broke-up-its-funding-round/\">How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding Round</a></div>\n\nIn general, I think the idea of patents is good. Many tech companies complain about patents in the tech space, and I agree that the system we currently have in place often seems to award patents erroneously. \n\nBut, I do think that patents (when properly awarded) can be a critical part of protecting your business, and based on the story about Like.com, I\'m not feeling like this was an abuse of the patent system so much as just a great strategy from Like.com (with a little luck thrown in):\n\n<blockquote>But Like.com had a patent on product applications of computer vision. Like.com, which was well-funded with $47 million in venture backing, filed a lawsuit against Modista the day before its funding was going to close.</blockquote>\n\nInteresting read.','How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding Round - Tomio Geron - Social Markets - Forbes',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2524-revision','','','2011-04-29 18:27:43','2011-04-30 01:27:43','',2524,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/29/2524-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2527,1,'2011-05-02 11:19:28','2011-05-02 18:19:28','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://tweetlibrary.com/damon/osamaraidlivetweets\">Uh oh, now I\'m the guy who liveblogged the Osama raid without knowing it</a></div>\n\nFascinating.','Uh oh, now I\'m the guy who liveblogged the Osama raid without knowing it',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','uh-oh-now-im-the-guy-who-liveblogged-the-osama-raid-without-knowing-it','','','2011-05-02 11:19:31','2011-05-02 18:19:31','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2527',0,'post','',0),
	(2526,1,'2011-04-29 18:27:46','2011-04-30 01:27:46','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://blogs.forbes.com/tomiogeron/2011/04/28/how-like-com-shut-down-a-competitor-and-broke-up-its-funding-round/\">How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding Round</a></div>\n\nIn general, I think the idea of patents is good. Many tech companies complain about patents in the tech space, and I agree that the system we currently have in place often seems to award patents erroneously. \n\nBut, I do think that patents (when properly awarded) can be a critical part of protecting your business, and based on the story about Like.com, I\'m not feeling like this was an abuse of the patent system so much as just a great strategy from Like.com (with a little luck thrown in):\n\n<blockquote>But Like.com had a patent on product applications of computer vision. Like.com, which was well-funded with $47 million in venture backing, filed a lawsuit against Modista the day before its funding was going to close.</blockquote>\n\nInteresting read.','How Like.com Shut Down A Competitor–And Broke Up Its Funding Round - Tomio Geron - Social Markets - Forbes',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2524-revision-2','','','2011-04-29 18:27:46','2011-04-30 01:27:46','',2524,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/29/2524-revision-2/',0,'revision','',0),
