	(2536,1,'2011-04-26 18:29:21','2011-04-27 01:29:21','Jason-Preston.com is my personal blog, and it contains a collection of articles I\'ve written and links I\'ve found interesting over the past six years. I\'ve always had an interest in technology, startups, and <a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/games-library/\">video games</a>, so I\'d expect to find plenty of things in those categories. \r\n\r\n<h3>About Jason:</h3>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jason-bnw-border.gif\" alt=\"jason-bnw-border\" title=\"jason-bnw-border\" width=\"240\" height=\"297\" class=\"alignright size-full wp-image-2055\" /><a name=\"long\"></a>Jason Preston is the Vice President of Strategy and Product Development for the <a href=\"http://parnassusgroup.com\">Parnassus Group</a>, a Redmond-based company that produces conferences and parties around emerging technologies, and provides social media intelligence to clients and partners seeking to effectively participate in online communities. \r\n\r\nJason has worked with clients on shaping blogger engagement strategies, creating blogs, and understanding the business value of social media tools like Twitter and Facebook. He has spoken at numerous local and national conferences including the Web Community Forum, The Tweet House, and Ignite Seattle. He has written about social media and emerging technologies in <a href=\"http://crosscut.com\">Crosscut</a> and <a href=\"http://www.seattlebusinessmonthly.com/\">Seattle Business Magazine</a>.\r\n\r\nIn his spare time, Jason maintains a number of side-projects, the most interesting of which is <a href=\"http://potluks.com\">Potluk</a>, a web service that allows users to create their own personal \"Hacker News\" site - a private or public collection of links that group members can vote and comment on.\r\n\r\nHe graduated from Occidental College in 2007 with a Bachelor\'s degree in Diplomacy and World Affairs, which is a fancy way of saying \"Economics, Business, Politics, and Philosophy.\"\r\n\r\n<a name=\"short\"></a>\r\n<h3>Shorter Bio</h3>\r\n\r\nJason Preston is the Vice President of Strategy and Product Development at the Parnassus Group, a social media intelligence and events production company based in Redmond. Jason has spoken at numerous local and national conferences including the Web Community Forum, The Tweet House, and Ignite Seattle. He has written about social media and emerging technologies in <a href=\"http://crosscut.com\">Crosscut</a> and <a href=\"http://www.seattlebusinessmonthly.com/\">Seattle Business Magazine</a>.\r\n\r\nIn his spare time, Jason maintains a number of side-projects, the most interesting of which is <a href=\"http://potluks.com\">Potluk</a>, a web service that allows users to create their own personal \"Hacker News\" site - a private or public collection of links that group members can vote and comment on.\r\n\r\n<h3>Speaking</h3>\r\n\r\n<strong>Jason Preston</strong> is an accomplished public speaker and a recognized expert in social media living in Seattle, washington with his wife <a href=\"http://moniguzman.com\">Mónica Guzmán Preston</a>. \r\n\r\nHe has presented for the Tweet House, the Web Community Forum, the Redmond Chamber of Commerce, the Greater Seattle Chamber of Commerce, SCORE, the Social Networking for Business Conference, Ignite Seattle, 140 | The Twitter Conference, and others.\r\n\r\nIf you\'re interested in having Jason speak at your event, please <a href=\"mailto:jason@prestons.me\">contact him</a> via e-mail. \r\n\r\n\r\n<strong>Video of Jason\'s work:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n        <li>The Twitter Conference: <a href=\"http://vimeo.com/7148408\">The Comedy Session - Being Funny, In Short</a></li>\r\n	<li>Ignite Seattle: <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dTY9PjrFOhA\">How to Say Anything in 140 Characters or Less</a></li>\r\n	<li>Seattle Chamber: <a href=\"http://vimeo.com/3442553\">Buzz Monitoring in Social Media</a></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n<h3>Pictures (right-click to download):</h3>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jp-200.jpg\"><img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jp-200.jpg\" alt=\"jp-200\" title=\"jp-200\" width=\"200\" height=\"266\" class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-2061\" /></a>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jp-large.jpg\">Regular headshot - Large Image</a></li>\r\n	<li><a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jp-200.jpg\">Regular headshot - 200px wide</a></li>\r\n	<li><a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jp-100.jpg\">Regular headshot - 100px wide</a></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jason-bnw-border.gif\"><img src=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jason-bnw-border.gif\" alt=\"jason-bnw-border\" title=\"jason-bnw-border\" width=\"240\" height=\"297\" class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-2055\" /></a>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><a href=\"http://www.jason-preston.com/wp-content/uploads/2008/07/jason-bnw-border.gif\">Black & White - 240px wide</a></li>\r\n</ul>','About',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','1810-revision-32','','','2011-04-26 18:29:21','2011-04-27 01:29:21','',1810,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/04/26/1810-revision-32/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2538,1,'2011-05-10 15:47:03','2011-05-10 22:47:03','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mixergy.com/ta-mccann-gist-interview/\">Gist’s Founder: How I Built And Sold My Company To RIM</a></div>\n\nI\'ll be honest - I haven\'t watched this yet, but the title has me hooked. \n\n','Gist’s Founder: How I Built And Sold My Company To RIM',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2537-revision','','','2011-05-10 15:47:03','2011-05-10 22:47:03','',2537,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/10/2537-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2539,1,'2011-05-12 15:41:02','2011-05-12 22:41:02','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2011/05/12/facebook-google-revenue/#14231Facebook-Versus-Google-Following-the-Money\">Google v. Facebook: Following the Money</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Also, given Google’s recent launch of +1 — a half social, half traffic-generating web search feature — Facebook might be feeling even more pressure to make sure users are wary of the tool and less likely to use it without overthinking it. After all +1 is a Facebook Like competitor.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s more than just a battle over Facebook\'s insane growth in display ad revenue -- it is Google battle to maintain the sanctity of their core business: search. The <a href=\"http://socialdatastrategies.com/2011/how-to-get-facebook-social-data-for-a-url/\">social interactions</a> that are happening around all kinds of URLs on Facebook is an incredibly rich trove of metadata from which Facebook could launch a very powerful web search engine. \n\nI think it\'s clear that Facebook has tons of growth ahead of it in both the ad market it\'s growing now, and at least one other market they haven\'t even entered yet: search. \n','Google v. Facebook: Following the Money',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','google-v-facebook-following-the-money','','','2011-05-12 15:41:04','2011-05-12 22:41:04','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2539',0,'post','',0),
	(2540,1,'2011-05-12 15:41:02','2011-05-12 22:41:02','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://mashable.com/2011/05/12/facebook-google-revenue/#14231Facebook-Versus-Google-Following-the-Money\">Google v. Facebook: Following the Money</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>Also, given Google’s recent launch of +1 — a half social, half traffic-generating web search feature — Facebook might be feeling even more pressure to make sure users are wary of the tool and less likely to use it without overthinking it. After all +1 is a Facebook Like competitor.</blockquote>\n\nIt\'s more than just a battle over Facebook\'s insane growth in display ad revenue -- it is Google battle to maintain the sanctity of their core business: search. The <a href=\"http://socialdatastrategies.com/2011/how-to-get-facebook-social-data-for-a-url/\">social interactions</a> that are happening around all kinds of URLs on Facebook is an incredibly rich trove of metadata from which Facebook could launch a very powerful web search engine. \n\nI think it\'s clear that Facebook has tons of growth ahead of it in both the ad market it\'s growing now, and at least one other market they haven\'t even entered yet: search. \n','Google v. Facebook: Following the Money',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2539-revision','','','2011-05-12 15:41:02','2011-05-12 22:41:02','',2539,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/12/2539-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2541,1,'2011-05-12 15:46:26','2011-05-12 22:46:26','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.geekwire.com/2011/nordstrom-testing-ipods-devices-mobile-checkout-stores\">Nordstrom testing iPods, other devices for checkout</a></div>\n\nA while back (I\'m not sure when because I\'m not sure how to find out the date a question was asked on Quora) <a href=\"http://www.quora.com/Why-is-Apple-the-only-retailer-to-remove-the-checkout-counter?q=why+is+apple+the+only+retailer\">I asked</a>:\n\n<blockquote>Why is Apple the only retailer to remove the checkout counter?\n\nIs there a legal, data-backed, or operational reason why stores like Best Buy and Express don\'t equip their employees with mobile devices that can charge credit cards, or is it simply cultural?</blockquote>\n\nAnd today, Geekwire shares that:\n\n<blockquote>Seattle-based retailer Nordstrom mentioned during its annual shareholders meeting yesterday that it has been testing mobile point-of-sale devices at its Bellevue and Seattle stores, to reduce wait times for customers checking out with credit cards</blockquote>\n\nGuess it\'s just cultural. Seems like a great opportunity for a company like <a href=\"http://squareup.com\">Square</a> to start handling the processing fees for quite a lot of retail sales. \n\n\n\n','Nordstrom testing iPods, other devices for checkout',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','nordstrom-testing-ipods-other-devices-for-checkout','','','2011-05-12 15:46:29','2011-05-12 22:46:29','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2541',0,'post','',0),
	(2542,1,'2011-05-12 15:46:26','2011-05-12 22:46:26','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.geekwire.com/2011/nordstrom-testing-ipods-devices-mobile-checkout-stores\">Nordstrom testing iPods, other devices for checkout</a></div>\n\nA while back (I\'m not sure when because I\'m not sure how to find out the date a question was asked on Quora) <a href=\"http://www.quora.com/Why-is-Apple-the-only-retailer-to-remove-the-checkout-counter?q=why+is+apple+the+only+retailer\">I asked</a>:\n\n<blockquote>Why is Apple the only retailer to remove the checkout counter?\n\nIs there a legal, data-backed, or operational reason why stores like Best Buy and Express don\'t equip their employees with mobile devices that can charge credit cards, or is it simply cultural?</blockquote>\n\nAnd today, Geekwire shares that:\n\n<blockquote>Seattle-based retailer Nordstrom mentioned during its annual shareholders meeting yesterday that it has been testing mobile point-of-sale devices at its Bellevue and Seattle stores, to reduce wait times for customers checking out with credit cards</blockquote>\n\nGuess it\'s just cultural. Seems like a great opportunity for a company like <a href=\"http://squareup.com\">Square</a> to start handling the processing fees for quite a lot of retail sales. \n\n\n\n','Nordstrom testing iPods, other devices for checkout',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2541-revision','','','2011-05-12 15:46:26','2011-05-12 22:46:26','',2541,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/12/2541-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2543,1,'2011-05-12 16:02:22','2011-05-12 23:02:22','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://activeadmin.info/\">Active Admin | The administration framework for Ruby on Rails</a></div>\n\nFor those Ruby on Rails programmers out there, at first glance this looks awesome:\n\n<blockquote>Active Admin is a Ruby on Rails plugin for generating administration style interfaces. It abstracts common business application patterns to make it simple for developers to implement beautiful and elegant interfaces with very little effort.</blockquote>\n\n','Active Admin | The administration framework for Ruby on Rails',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','active-admin-the-administration-framework-for-ruby-on-rails','','','2011-05-12 16:02:25','2011-05-12 23:02:25','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2543',0,'post','',0),
	(2544,1,'2011-05-12 16:02:22','2011-05-12 23:02:22','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://activeadmin.info/\">Active Admin | The administration framework for Ruby on Rails</a></div>\n\nFor those Ruby on Rails programmers out there, at first glance this looks awesome:\n\n<blockquote>Active Admin is a Ruby on Rails plugin for generating administration style interfaces. It abstracts common business application patterns to make it simple for developers to implement beautiful and elegant interfaces with very little effort.</blockquote>\n\n','Active Admin | The administration framework for Ruby on Rails',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2543-revision','','','2011-05-12 16:02:22','2011-05-12 23:02:22','',2543,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/12/2543-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
