	(2644,1,'2011-10-17 12:11:17','2011-10-17 19:11:17','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.fastcompany.com/make-your-move\">\"Make Your Move\" Interviews</a></div>\r\n\r\nGreat short, inspirational interview videos from Fast Company with tech company founders.\r\n','\"Make Your Move\" Interviews',0,'','publish','open','closed','','make-your-move-interviews','','','2011-10-17 12:11:17','2011-10-17 19:11:17','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2644',0,'post','',0),
	(2548,1,'2011-05-15 22:52:04','2011-05-16 05:52:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.avc.com/a_vc/2011/05/social-medias-secret-weapon-email.html\">Social Media\'s Secret Weapon - Email</a></div>\n\nFred Wilson:\n\n<blockquote>I remember four or five years ago, people, myself included, were asking if social media was going to lead to the end of email as we know it. In an ironic twist of fate, it turns out that email is social media\'s secret weapon. And more and more social applications are leveraging the power of email to drive repeat usage and retention.</blockquote>\n\nI spent a little time combing through my old answers on Quora because I thought that at some point I had written an answer explaining why instant e-mail notifications are so important to social media sites: people (like myself) live their work lives through their e-mail. It is the dashboard through which all things eventually must filter. \n\nWhen I build <a href=\"http://potluks.com\">Potluk</a>, I built it with e-mail at the heart of the system because that is where link sharing communication is most useful. E-mail is behind the power of Facebook\'s newest group features. \n\nNew social startups, especially consumer facing social services that want to see engagement numbers grow, would be wise to heed Fred Wilson\'s advice. ','Social Media\'s Secret Weapon - Email',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','social-medias-secret-weapon-email','','','2011-05-15 22:52:06','2011-05-16 05:52:06','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2548',0,'post','',0),
	(2549,1,'2011-05-15 22:52:04','2011-05-16 05:52:04','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.avc.com/a_vc/2011/05/social-medias-secret-weapon-email.html\">Social Media\'s Secret Weapon - Email</a></div>\n\nFred Wilson:\n\n<blockquote>I remember four or five years ago, people, myself included, were asking if social media was going to lead to the end of email as we know it. In an ironic twist of fate, it turns out that email is social media\'s secret weapon. And more and more social applications are leveraging the power of email to drive repeat usage and retention.</blockquote>\n\nI spent a little time combing through my old answers on Quora because I thought that at some point I had written an answer explaining why instant e-mail notifications are so important to social media sites: people (like myself) live their work lives through their e-mail. It is the dashboard through which all things eventually must filter. \n\nWhen I build <a href=\"http://potluks.com\">Potluk</a>, I built it with e-mail at the heart of the system because that is where link sharing communication is most useful. E-mail is behind the power of Facebook\'s newest group features. \n\nNew social startups, especially consumer facing social services that want to see engagement numbers grow, would be wise to heed Fred Wilson\'s advice. ','Social Media\'s Secret Weapon - Email',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2548-revision','','','2011-05-15 22:52:04','2011-05-16 05:52:04','',2548,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/15/2548-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
	(2550,1,'2011-05-16 18:59:53','2011-05-17 01:59:53','Since I\'ve started programming on my own I\'ve run in to all kinds of tools and services that I had no idea existed. Things like Heroku and Recurly and Sendgrid that all conspire to make the life of an app developer much much easier.\n\nI don\'t remember the name of it at the moment but the other day I found a Rails gem that pre-builds an admin interface for a web app with most of the common features that normally you\'d need to code.\n\nMany developers practice DRY coding, which stands for \"Don\'t Repeat Yourself,\" and these tools and services seem to embrace that mentality.\n\nBut more importantly it looks as though existing popular web services have managed to create enough of a standard practice in a number of key areas that we can now start to build and rely on a number of the basic infrastructure pieces that have traditionally been the burden of every new web startup.\n\nThat\'s very cool and exciting because it lowers the barrier to entry even further, and lets us begin to build a whole new layer on the stack.','The next layer',0,'','publish','open','closed','','the-next-layer','','','2011-05-16 18:59:53','2011-05-17 01:59:53','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/16/the-next-layer/',0,'post','',0),
	(2551,1,'2011-05-17 11:41:00','2011-05-17 18:41:00','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.fastcompany.com/1753756/social-flights-puts-private-flying-within-reach-of-the-everyday-traveler?partner=homepage_newsletter\">Fly Like You\'re Rich With Social Flights (And Private Planes)</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The founders of Social Flights believe that, if you had the chance to grab a seat on a private plane that would fly point-to-point, as the wealthy are able to do today, and the cost of your seat was comparable to what you’d pay to fly commercial, you’d grab it.</blockquote>\n\nSounds logical to me. However, It\'s an interesting thought - creating a \"liquid\" marketplace for private jet seats. Is there enough demand to warrant enough flights for the price per seat to come down to a reasonable level? I guess we\'ll find out. ','Fly Like You\'re Rich With Social Flights (And Private Planes)',0,'','publish','closed','closed','','fly-like-youre-rich-with-social-flights-and-private-planes','','','2011-05-17 11:41:02','2011-05-17 18:41:02','',0,'http://www.jason-preston.com/?p=2551',0,'post','',0),
	(2552,1,'2011-05-17 11:41:00','2011-05-17 18:41:00','<div class=\"ll\"><a href=\"http://www.fastcompany.com/1753756/social-flights-puts-private-flying-within-reach-of-the-everyday-traveler?partner=homepage_newsletter\">Fly Like You\'re Rich With Social Flights (And Private Planes)</a></div>\n\n<blockquote>The founders of Social Flights believe that, if you had the chance to grab a seat on a private plane that would fly point-to-point, as the wealthy are able to do today, and the cost of your seat was comparable to what you’d pay to fly commercial, you’d grab it.</blockquote>\n\nSounds logical to me. However, It\'s an interesting thought - creating a \"liquid\" marketplace for private jet seats. Is there enough demand to warrant enough flights for the price per seat to come down to a reasonable level? I guess we\'ll find out. ','Fly Like You\'re Rich With Social Flights (And Private Planes)',0,'','inherit','open','closed','','2551-revision','','','2011-05-17 11:41:00','2011-05-17 18:41:00','',2551,'http://www.jason-preston.com/index.php/2011/05/17/2551-revision/',0,'revision','',0),
